{"version":"6.9","hitCount":112742,"nextCursorMark":"AoIIQRwQnCg1NTUxMjM5MA==","nextPageUrl":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=((\"large language model\" OR LLM OR \"generative AI\") AND (governance OR accountability OR compliance OR audit OR risk)) AND FIRST_PDATE:[1900-01-01 TO 2026-07-31]&cursorMark=AoIIQRwQnCg1NTUxMjM5MA==&resultType=core&pageSize=100&format=json","request":{"queryString":"((\"large language model\" OR LLM OR \"generative AI\") AND (governance OR accountability OR compliance OR audit OR risk)) AND FIRST_PDATE:[1900-01-01 TO 2026-07-31]","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":100,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"PMC13299464","source":"PMC","pmcid":"PMC13299464","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13299464"]},"title":"Governing Generative AI for Healthy Ageing: A Normative Conceptual Framework for Societal Alignment, Epistemic Authority, and Value Convergence in Geriatric Care","authorString":"Alves Ferreira J, Tukaiev S, Giannouli V.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Alves Ferreira J","firstName":"João Miguel","lastName":"Alves Ferreira","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Pharmacology and Experimental Therapeutics, Faculty of Medicine, University of Coimbra, 3004-531 Coimbra, Portugal"}]}},{"fullName":"Tukaiev S","firstName":"Sergii","lastName":"Tukaiev","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Communication, Culture, and Society, Institute of Public Health, Università Della Svizzera Italiana, 6900 Lugano, Switzerland;"}]}},{"fullName":"Giannouli V","firstName":"Vaitsa","lastName":"Giannouli","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, Democritus University of Thrace, 68300 Didymoteicho, Greece"}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"12","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4221244,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Healthcare (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","issn":"2227-9032","essn":"2227-9032","isoabbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","nlmid":"101666525"}},"pubYear":"2026","language":"eng","pubModel":"Undetermined","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Other"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Healthy Ageing","Epistemic Authority","Automation Bias","Responsibility Gap","Digital Ageism","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Models","Warranted Trust","Geriatric Governance","Societal Ai Alignment"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"PubMedCentral","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13299464/?tool=EBI"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"PubMedCentral","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13299464/pdf/?tool=EBI"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13299464"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13299464?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCreation":"2026-06-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-26","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},{"id":"42293851","source":"MED","pmid":"42293851","pmcid":"PMC13263806","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13263806"]},"doi":"10.21037/tau-2026-1-0097","title":"Large language models for post-discharge follow-up in erectile dysfunction care: a narrative review.","authorString":"Zhang Y, Lv B, Xu R, Shi C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zhang Y","firstName":"Yiman","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nursing, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lv B","firstName":"Bodong","lastName":"Lv","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu R","firstName":"Runnan","lastName":"Xu","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shi C","firstName":"Chenghao","lastName":"Shi","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-4402-814X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nursing, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-4402-814X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4217778,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Translational andrology and urology","medlineAbbreviation":"Transl Androl Urol","issn":"2223-4691","essn":"2223-4691","isoabbreviation":"Transl Androl Urol","nlmid":"101581119"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"185","abstractText":"<h4>Background and objective</h4>Erectile dysfunction (ED) is common and closely linked to cardiometabolic disease, psychological distress, and relationship problems. Although effective treatments are available, outcomes depend on sustained post-discharge follow-up to assess response, adherence, adverse effects, and psychosocial needs. In practice, ED follow-up is often hindered by stigma, embarrassment, limited access, and fragmented communication. This narrative review examines the potential role, risks, and implementation requirements of large language model (LLM)-assisted follow-up in ED care.<h4>Methods</h4>We conducted a targeted literature search in PubMed and Embase up to 15 January 2026, supplemented by Google Scholar for citation tracking. We selected clinically relevant evidence and conceptual work on ED follow-up, conversational artificial intelligence (AI), and safety governance, and synthesized findings thematically.<h4>Key content and findings</h4>Among 280 included sources, only 10 were ED-specific, while 270 provided transferable evidence from chronic disease, oncology, and mental health follow-up settings. LLMs may reduce communication barriers, enable low-threshold symptom check-ins, reinforce discharge education, and collect patient-reported outcomes more frequently than traditional follow-up. Potential clinical value is greatest for structured, low-risk tasks such as education reinforcement, adherence support, and routine symptom monitoring. However, ED follow-up includes medication safety, nuanced counseling, mental health and relationship contexts, and sensitive data governance. Key risks include inaccurate or unsafe recommendations, privacy breaches, inappropriate handling of mental health red flags, and unclear accountability. A risk-stratified, human-in-the-loop implementation model can balance feasibility and safety by reserving autonomous LLM interactions for low-risk content while triggering clinician review and escalation for predefined high-risk scenarios.<h4>Conclusions</h4>LLM-assisted follow-up could become a useful adjunct in ED care if implemented with clear boundaries, auditability, privacy protection, and robust human oversight. A nurse-supervised, trigger-based governance model, which aligns with current follow-up workflows, may enhance continuity and responsiveness without compromising clinical accountability. Prospective evaluations should prioritize patient acceptability, safety outcomes, workload impact, and implementation feasibility.","affiliation":"Department of Nursing, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Erectile dysfunction (ED)","Human-in-the-loop","Post-discharge Follow-up","Large Language Model (Llm)","Conversational Artificial Intelligence (Conversational Ai)"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21037/tau-2026-1-0097"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13263806"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13263806?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-26"},{"id":"42388443","source":"MED","pmid":"42388443","pmcid":"PMC13318555","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13318555"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.ccrj.2026.100194","title":"A pragmatic risk-stratified framework for using large language models in intensive care medicine: A narrative review.","authorString":"Devanand NA, Verghese S, Bacchi S, Bain C, Subramaniam A, Sundararajan K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Devanand NA","firstName":"Nilesh Anand","lastName":"Devanand","initials":"NA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0742-520X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care, Royal Adelaide Hospital, Port Road, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Verghese S","firstName":"Santosh","lastName":"Verghese","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care, Flinders Medical Centre, Southern Adelaide Local Health Network, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Office of the Chief Medical Information Officer, Digital Health, Adelaide, South Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Bacchi S","firstName":"Stephen","lastName":"Bacchi","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5130-8628"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Neurology, Lyell McEwin Hospital, Haydown Road, Elizabeth Vale, South Australia, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Bain C","firstName":"Christopher","lastName":"Bain","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5281-2364"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Digital Health, Faculty of Information Technology, Monash University, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Subramaniam A","firstName":"Ashwin","lastName":"Subramaniam","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8292-7357"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care Medicine, Monash Health, Dandenong, Victoria, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care, Peninsula Health, Frankston, Victoria, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Peninsula Clinical School, Monash University, Frankston, Victoria, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Australian and New Zealand Intensive Care Research Centre, Department of Epidemiology and Preventive Medicine, Monash University, Melbourne, Victoria, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Sundararajan K","firstName":"Krishnaswamy","lastName":"Sundararajan","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3377-6062"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care, Royal Adelaide Hospital, Port Road, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."},{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, The University of Adelaide, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5130-8628"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0742-520X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3377-6062"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5281-2364"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8292-7357"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"28","journalIssueId":4219710,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","journal":{"title":"Critical care and resuscitation : journal of the Australasian Academy of Critical Care Medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"Crit Care Resusc","issn":"1441-2772","essn":"2652-9335","isoabbreviation":"Crit Care Resusc","nlmid":"100888170"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"100194","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>To provide Australian intensive care clinicians with a pragmatic framework for the safe integration of large language models (LLMs) into intensive care unit (ICU) practise, addressing the current lack of Australian-specific guidance and limited local evidence.<h4>Design</h4>Narrative review.<h4>Data sources</h4>Peer-reviewed publications, preprints, and policy documents relating to LLM use in health care, with a focus on critical care applications and governance.<h4>Review methods</h4>Evidence and expert commentary were synthesised to develop a clinician-led, risk-stratified framework for ICU implementation, with emphasis on safety, oversight, and applicability within Australian health systems. Clinical use cases, risks, governance considerations, and practical safeguards for day-to-day ICU practise were identified.<h4>Results</h4>LLMs have potential utility in data-dense ICU environments, including summarising complex clinical information, supporting documentation, assisting clinical reasoning, and facilitating research tasks. However, evidence for LLM performance in ICU contexts remains limited, particularly in Australia. Key risks include inaccurate or fabricated outputs (\"<i>hallucinations</i>\"), bias, lack of situational awareness, privacy concerns, and over-reliance in high-stakes decision-making. We propose a risk-stratified framework that categorises LLM applications by clinical risk and reversibility, and aligns each category with proportional oversight, verification processes, and governance safeguards, emphasising clinician-in-the-loop decision-making.<h4>Conclusions</h4>LLMs may serve as adjunctive cognitive tools in Australian ICUs when used in clearly defined, low-to intermediate-risk contexts under clinician oversight. Safe integration requires robust governance frameworks emphasising transparency, data protection, and proportionate clinician decision-making. Further Australian-based evaluation is needed before high-risk clinical applications can be considered for routine practise.","affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care, Royal Adelaide Hospital, Port Road, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","intensive care unit","Clinical Decision Support","Health Governance","Large Language Model"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ccrj.2026.100194"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13318555"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13318555?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-25"},{"id":"42072996","source":"MED","pmid":"42072996","pmcid":"PMC13115902","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13115902"]},"doi":"10.3390/healthcare14081098","title":"Governing Generative AI in Healthcare: A Normative Conceptual Framework for Epistemic Authority, Trust, and the Architecture of Responsibility.","authorString":"Akgün FE, Akgün M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Akgün FE","firstName":"Fatma Eren","lastName":"Akgün","initials":"FE","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8013-7565"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Elderly Care Programme, Department of Health Care Services, Vocational School of Health Services, Ağrı İbrahim Çeçen University, 04100 Ağrı, Türkiye."}]}},{"fullName":"Akgün M","firstName":"Metin","lastName":"Akgün","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3404-4274"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Diseases, School of Medicine, Ağrı İbrahim Çeçen University, 04100 Ağrı, Türkiye."},{"affiliation":"Department of Systematic Philosophy and Logic, Faculty of Letters, Atatürk University, 25240 Erzurum, Türkiye."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8013-7565"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3404-4274"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"8","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4188735,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Healthcare (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","issn":"2227-9032","essn":"2227-9032","isoabbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","nlmid":"101666525"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1098","abstractText":"<h4>Background/objectives</h4>Large language models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT are rapidly being integrated into healthcare for tasks ranging from clinical documentation to diagnostic support. Current ethical discussions focus predominantly on bias, privacy, and accuracy, leaving three critical governance questions unresolved: What kind of knowledge does an LLM output represent in clinical reasoning? When is a clinician's or patient's trust in that output justified? Who bears responsibility when an AI-informed decision leads to patient harm? This study proposes the Epistemic Authority-Trust-Responsibility (ETR) Architecture, a normative conceptual framework that addresses these three questions as an integrated governance challenge.<h4>Methods</h4>The framework was developed through normative conceptual analysis-a method that constructs governance proposals by synthesising philosophical principles, ethical theories, and empirical evidence. The literature was identified through structured searches of PubMed, PhilPapers, and EUR-Lex (January 2020-March 2026), drawing on the philosophy of medical knowledge, the ethics of trust and testimony, and the moral philosophy of responsibility.<h4>Results</h4>The ETR Architecture produces four outputs: (i) a four-tier classification system that distinguishes LLM outputs-from administrative drafts to clinical evidence claims-and matches each tier to appropriate verification requirements; (ii) the concept of the 'epistemic placebo', formally defined as a governance measure that creates a documented appearance of compliance while lacking at least one operative element of genuine oversight; (iii) a model specifying four conditions under which trust in healthcare AI is justified; (iv) four testable hypotheses with associated research designs connecting governance design to trust calibration and patient safety.<h4>Conclusions</h4>The 2025-2027 regulatory transition period offers a critical window for shaping how healthcare institutions govern AI. We argue that deploying LLMs without explicitly classifying their outputs and building appropriate oversight risks allows governance norms to be set by technology vendors rather than by evidence-informed, patient-centred policy.","affiliation":"Elderly Care Programme, Department of Health Care Services, Vocational School of Health Services, Ağrı İbrahim Çeçen University, 04100 Ağrı, Türkiye.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["other","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Patient Safety","Healthcare","Epistemic Authority","Automation Bias","Responsibility Gap","Human Oversight","Ai Governance","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Model","Warranted Trust"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare14081098"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13115902"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13115902?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-20"},{"id":"42308509","source":"MED","pmid":"42308509","pmcid":"PMC13324317","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13324317"]},"doi":"10.2196/92240","title":"Ethical Considerations in Personal Health Large Language Models.","authorString":"Liu J, Liu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Liu J","firstName":"Jialin","lastName":"Liu","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Center, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu S","firstName":"Siru","lastName":"Liu","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Informatics, Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Nashville, TN, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","issn":"1439-4456","essn":"1438-8871","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e92240","abstractText":"Personal health large language models (PH-LLMs) have rapidly evolved from research prototypes into consumer-facing, data-linked systems that support symptom triage, medication questions, mental health check-ins, and longitudinal self-management. Their direct-to-consumer use without clinical oversight creates a distinct ethical risk profile that general artificial intelligence governance frameworks do not fully address. This viewpoint focuses on text-based, platform-mediated PH-LLMs and synthesizes PH-LLM-specific challenges across 6 domains: privacy, accuracy, equity, transparency, human-artificial intelligence interaction, and regulatory governance. These risks may be amplified by health literacy gaps, longitudinal data aggregation, persuasive conversational design, and fragmented oversight across the consumer-clinical boundary. Grounded in the 4 principles of biomedical ethics, we propose a governance framework that operationalizes beneficence, nonmaleficence, autonomy, and justice through design and deployment controls, including health literacy-aligned communication, crisis and pharmacological safeguards, hallucination mitigation, role disclosure, granular consent, fairness auditing, and accessible design. We further outline implementation mechanisms, including risk-tiered certification, tiered accountability, and postdeployment oversight through adverse-event reporting, transparency reporting, and independent safety evaluation. This framework is intended as an evidence-informed but partly anticipatory approach to governing PH-LLMs in personal health management.","affiliation":"Information Center, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Health Literacy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Ethics","Patient Safety","Health Literacy","Privacy","Governance","Health Equity","Digital Health","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Health Ai","Personal Health Large Language Model"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/92240"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13324317"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13324317?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-17","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-18","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-17"},{"id":"42333365","source":"MED","pmid":"42333365","pmcid":"PMC13283513","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13283513"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.109393","title":"Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning in Hospital Quality Management, Patient Safety, and Accreditation Readiness: A Systematic Review and Narrative Synthesis.","authorString":"Marathe N, Ranjan M, Singh MP, Trigunait P, Prakash A, Kaushal N, Pawar A, Kshatriya GS, Dubey R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Marathe N","firstName":"Nitin","lastName":"Marathe","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Hospital Administration, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Ranjan M","firstName":"Manish","lastName":"Ranjan","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Microbiology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Jammu, Jammu, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Singh MP","firstName":"Madhusudan P","lastName":"Singh","initials":"MP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Deoghar, Deoghar, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Trigunait P","firstName":"Pragati","lastName":"Trigunait","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Prakash A","firstName":"Avinash","lastName":"Prakash","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Anaesthesiology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Kaushal N","firstName":"Nitin","lastName":"Kaushal","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Community Medicine and Public Health, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Pawar A","firstName":"Amit","lastName":"Pawar","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Transfusion Medicine, Military Hospital Doda, Jammu, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Kshatriya GS","firstName":"Girish Singh","lastName":"Kshatriya","initials":"GS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Transfusion Medicine, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Deoghar, Deoghar, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Dubey R","firstName":"Rounak","lastName":"Dubey","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Transfusion Medicine, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0295-3262"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4188949,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","issn":"2168-8184","essn":"2168-8184","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e109393","abstractText":"As healthcare systems become increasingly data-driven, artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) are being explored as tools to support quality management, patient safety, and accreditation. This systematic review synthesizes the available evidence across these domains through a structured literature search of major biomedical databases, encompassing studies published over the past decade and narratively synthesized to capture implementation context alongside technical performance. Three electronic databases were searched, and 23 studies were included. The available empirical evidence suggests that these technologies are most useful for repetitive, operationally well-defined, and digitally captured tasks, while local validation, acceptance testing, and continuous monitoring consistently emerge as prerequisites for safe institutional deployment. Governance capacity and organizational readiness are identified as equally determinative of outcomes as algorithmic performance itself. Collectively, the evidence supports positioning AI and ML as augmentative instruments within human-led quality governance structures, rather than as autonomous operational systems. Evidence was heterogeneous, with most empirical studies being single-center, retrospective, or implementation-focused. The risk-of-bias assessment indicated limited high-quality comparative evidence, limiting the strength of the pooled conclusions.","affiliation":"Hospital Administration, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Quality assurance","Accreditation","Patient Safety","Machine Learning","Natural Language Processing","Deep Learning","Healthcare Governance","Hospital Quality Management"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.109393"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13283513"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13283513?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-21"},{"id":"42211163","source":"MED","pmid":"42211163","pmcid":"PMC13212543","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13212543"]},"doi":"10.14789/ejmj.jmj25-0036-r","title":"Generative AI in Medicine and Healthcare: A Comprehensive Review of Foundational Technologies, Clinical Applications, and Future Perspectives.","authorString":"Nakamura T, Uchida W, Yamamoto A, Aoki S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Nakamura T","firstName":"Tomoshige","lastName":"Nakamura","initials":"T"},{"fullName":"Uchida W","firstName":"Wataru","lastName":"Uchida","initials":"W"},{"fullName":"Yamamoto A","firstName":"Akira","lastName":"Yamamoto","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Aoki S","firstName":"Shigeki","lastName":"Aoki","initials":"S"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"72","journalIssueId":4203321,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Juntendo medical journal","medlineAbbreviation":"Juntendo Med J","issn":"2759-7504","essn":"2759-7504","isoabbreviation":"Juntendo Med J","nlmid":"9918993695406676"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"176-188","abstractText":"Generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) is poised to induce a paradigm shift in medicine and healthcare. This review provides a comprehensive overview of its foundational technologies, clinical applications, and pathways to practical integration. We first explain the principles of three core technologies: Transformers, exemplified by Large Language Models (LLMs); Diffusion Models for high-fidelity data generation; and Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) for 3D scene synthesis. We then systematically review their applications across diverse domains: automating clinical documentation, accelerating drug discovery, enhancing medical imaging diagnostics, and innovating surgical simulation. To bridge the gap to real-world implementation, we address critical system-level challenges, discussing practical solutions such as Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to mitigate hallucinations, on-premises LLMs to ensure data security, and no-code platforms to empower clinician-led development. Finally, we examine critical ethical, legal, and social issues-including data bias, interpretability, and accountability-emphasizing the need for a robust governance framework. This review underscores that generative AI is evolving beyond a mere efficiency tool into a powerful partner capable of augmenting the expertise of healthcare professionals and fundamentally shaping the future of medicine.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Medical imaging","Clinical Workflow","Digital Health","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.14789/ejmj.JMJ25-0036-R"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212543"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212543?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-10"},{"id":"42160468","source":"MED","pmid":"42160468","pmcid":"PMC13189224","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13189224"]},"doi":"10.2196/95899","title":"Large Language Models in Clinical Trial Recruitment: Sociotechnical and Economic Framework Development Study.","authorString":"Qian Q.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Qian Q","firstName":"Qian","lastName":"Qian","initials":"Q","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-3578-4384"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Industrial and Systems Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Room EF625, Core F, Main Campus, Hung Hom, Kowloon, Hong Kong, China (Hong Kong), 86 15021400019, 86 15021400019."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-3578-4384"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["plain_english","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"5","journalIssueId":4189272,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR AI","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR AI","issn":"2817-1705","essn":"2817-1705","isoabbreviation":"JMIR AI","nlmid":"9918645789006676"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e95899","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Large language models (LLMs) have shown substantial promise in patient-trial matching, but most published studies still evaluate the performance under controlled technical conditions rather than within real recruitment workflows. Less is known about how LLM-enabled clinical artificial intelligence (AI) systems should be embedded into organizational settings where privacy constraints, human oversight, patient-facing concerns, and governance costs shape deployment outcomes.<h4>Objective</h4>This study develops a theory-grounded conceptual framework for analyzing how LLM-enabled clinical AI can be integrated into clinical trial recruitment workflows and how such integration affects operational, governance, and economic outcomes.<h4>Methods</h4>Using structured conceptual analysis and targeted evidence synthesis, the study draws on recent literature on LLM-based patient matching, human-AI collaboration in clinical settings, and clinical AI governance. Sociotechnical systems theory and transaction cost economics are integrated to build the LLM-Embedded Clinical Recruitment Architecture (LECRA) and to derive 6 testable propositions.<h4>Results</h4>LECRA conceptualizes recruitment as a closed-loop sociotechnical and economic system, spanning data complexity, model configuration and processing, human-AI collaboration, and economic and governance consequences. The revised framework identifies privacy constraints, hallucination risk, bias, patient trust, oversight intensity, and regulatory validation costs as key moderators of performance. It also reframes recruitment performance as a multidimensional construct and outlines an empirical roadmap for future testing.<h4>Conclusions</h4>LECRA offers a more deployment-sensitive account of when LLM-enabled recruitment is likely to create value and when the benefits may be offset by coordination, compliance, or trust-related frictions. This framework is intended to support future empirical studies and more realistic implementation decisions rather than to claim validated superiority of LLM-assisted recruitment.","affiliation":"Department of Industrial and Systems Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Room EF625, Core F, Main Campus, Hung Hom, Kowloon, Hong Kong, China (Hong Kong), 86 15021400019, 86 15021400019.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Clinical Trials","Patient Selection","Governance","Sociotechnical Systems","Transaction Cost Economics","Human-ai Collaboration","Clinical Ai","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/95899"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13189224"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13189224?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-20","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-20","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-21","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-20"},{"id":"42080195","source":"MED","pmid":"42080195","pmcid":"PMC13129269","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13129269"]},"doi":"10.1177/17562848261441693","title":"Responsible use of large language models in gastroenterology and hepatology.","authorString":"Khanna S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Khanna S","firstName":"Sahil","lastName":"Khanna","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7619-8338"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Mayo Clinic, 200 1st Street SW, Rochester, MN 55905, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7619-8338"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4085889,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Therapeutic advances in gastroenterology","medlineAbbreviation":"Ther Adv Gastroenterol","issn":"1756-283X","essn":"1756-2848","isoabbreviation":"Ther Adv Gastroenterol","nlmid":"101478893"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"17562848261441693","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) and related generative artificial intelligence (AI) systems are rapidly entering clinical workflows, including in gastroenterology and hepatology, where text-heavy documentation, guideline-driven care, and high-volume patient messaging create a strong demand for decision support and automation. Deployment raises distinctive risks: hallucinations and unsafe recommendations, automation bias, privacy and confidentiality threats, inequitable care, intellectual property and licensing uncertainty, and unclear allocation of responsibility across clinicians, institutions, and vendors. Regulators are increasingly emphasizing a lifecycle approach to trustworthy AI, including validation, risk management, human oversight, cybersecurity, monitoring, and transparency. Frameworks for healthcare protection regulate how health information may be shared with external model providers and how outputs should be logged, audited, and retained. This review synthesizes practical guidance for responsible LLM use, organized around (1) tiered use cases and risk stratification; (2) ethical principles (beneficence, non-maleficence, autonomy, justice, and accountability); (3) core legal and regulatory considerations; and (4) operational governance, evaluation, and monitoring strategies. Actionable checklists to support institutional adoption, use, and transparency are provided. Responsible use requires aligning LLM capabilities with risk, preventing inappropriate data sharing, validating performance in representative populations, ensuring human oversight and clear accountability, mitigating harm, and maintaining an evidence-based, continuously monitored deployment lifecycle.","affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Mayo Clinic, 200 1st Street SW, Rochester, MN 55905, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Regulation","Ethics","Gastroenterology","Hepatology","Law","Privacy","Clinical Decision Support","Governance","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/17562848261441693"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13129269"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13129269?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-26"},{"id":"41908138","source":"MED","pmid":"41908138","pmcid":"PMC13021839","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13021839"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1798519","title":"Mapping the use of large language models in hiring decisions: a scoping review.","authorString":"Tripathi A, Tripathi A, Darena F, Mishra PK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tripathi A","firstName":"Arpit","lastName":"Tripathi","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Business and Economics, Mendel University in Brno, Brno, Czechia."}]}},{"fullName":"Tripathi A","firstName":"Ankit","lastName":"Tripathi","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Business and Economics, Mendel University in Brno, Brno, Czechia."}]}},{"fullName":"Darena F","firstName":"Frantisek","lastName":"Darena","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Business and Economics, Mendel University in Brno, Brno, Czechia."}]}},{"fullName":"Mishra PK","firstName":"Pawan Kumar","lastName":"Mishra","initials":"PK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2704-8330"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Business and Economics, Mendel University in Brno, Brno, Czechia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2704-8330"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","issn":"2624-8212","essn":"2624-8212","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1798519","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly being explored and deployed across recruitment and selection processes, reshaping how hiring decisions are supported, communicated, and justified. Unlike earlier algorithmic hiring tools, LLMs operate through language-mediated interaction, influencing interpretive and evaluative layers of decision-making. This scoping review maps the academic literature on LLMs in hiring to examine (i) where and how these systems are applied across the hiring pipeline, (ii) what forms of evidence and outcomes are assessed, (iii) which risks and mitigation strategies are documented, and (iv) how disciplinary structures shape research focus. Following PRISMA-ScR guidelines, we synthesize research published between 2018 and 2026 across multiple disciplines using a transparent, lexicon-based coding approach. The results reveal a rapidly expanding but uneven literature, characterized by concentration in early hiring stages, selective outcome measurement favoring efficiency and performance, high awareness of ethical risks with limited empirical validation of controls, and structurally constrained interdisciplinarity. The review highlights key gaps and provides a foundation for future interdisciplinary and field-based research on responsible LLM use in hiring.","affiliation":"Faculty of Business and Economics, Mendel University in Brno, Brno, Czechia.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Recruitment","Human Resource Management","Scoping Review","Socio-technical Systems","Hiring","Algorithmic Decision-making","Generative Ai","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1798519"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13021839"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13021839?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-30","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-30","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-30","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-13"},{"id":"42212181","source":"MED","pmid":"42212181","pmcid":"PMC13212166","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13212166"]},"doi":"10.2147/jmdh.s581251","title":"Cybersecurity and Privacy Risks of Generative AI Mental-Health Chatbots: A Systematic Review and Regulatory Framework.","authorString":"Sawesi S, Sabbineni H, Shagamreddy RR, Rashrash B.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sawesi S","firstName":"Suhila","lastName":"Sawesi","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3510-8543"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Sabbineni H","firstName":"Hemalatha","lastName":"Sabbineni","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-9517-7889"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Shagamreddy RR","firstName":"Rajashekhar Reddy","lastName":"Shagamreddy","initials":"RR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Rashrash B","firstName":"Budur","lastName":"Rashrash","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, West Virginia School of Osteopathic Medicine, Lewisburg, WV, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3510-8543"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-9517-7889"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4091825,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of multidisciplinary healthcare","medlineAbbreviation":"J Multidiscip Healthc","issn":"1178-2390","essn":"1178-2390","isoabbreviation":"J Multidiscip Healthc","nlmid":"101512691"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"581251","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose</h4>Large language models (LLMs) and other generative artificial intelligence systems are increasingly used in mental health care for psychoeducation, emotional support, screening, and crisis-related interactions. To our knowledge, this is the first structured synthesis explicitly mapping LLM-specific cybersecurity and privacy risks to Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) regulatory frameworks. We aimed to characterize deployment patterns, identify multi-layered risks, and evaluate alignment of reported safeguards with established healthcare governance standards.<h4>Methods</h4>A PRISMA-guided systematic review was conducted using PubMed, APA PsycNet, and Google Scholar. After screening eligible records against predefined inclusion criteria, 33 studies were included. Two reviewers independently extracted data on application domains, deployment settings, risk categories, attack surfaces, data sensitivity, and reported or recommended controls.<h4>Results</h4>Generative AI chatbots were most frequently used for therapy or emotional support (13/33, 39.4%), followed by safety evaluation or benchmarking (9/33, 27.3%) and psychoeducation or advice (6/33, 18.2%). Suicide prevention or crisis detection was the most common domain (10/33, 30.3%). Most systems relied on general-purpose LLMs (21/33, 63.6%) and were deployed via consumer-facing platforms (16/33, 48.5%). Key risks included harmful or unsafe outputs, failures in crisis response, exposure of sensitive personal information, and limited transparency. Critically, 78.8% of studies (26/33) were rated high risk for cybersecurity evaluation rigor, indicating that formal adversarial testing and structured threat modeling remain rare.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Current governance frameworks have not fully adapted to generative conversational AI in mental health contexts. Because the therapeutic interface functions as a primary attack surface, single-layer security evaluation (assessing only software validation or content safety in isolation) is inadequate. More comprehensive approaches are needed, including stronger cybersecurity controls, privacy-preserving data practices, and explicit alignment with FDA SaMD guidance, HIPAA, ISO 14971, and the NIST AI Risk Management Framework.","affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["risk management","Clinical Safety","Data Protection","Regulatory Frameworks","Digital Therapeutics","Conversational Systems"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/JMDH.S581251"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212166"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212166?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-22"},{"id":"42341297","source":"MED","pmid":"42341297","pmcid":"PMC13293561","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13293561"]},"doi":"10.2196/88058","title":"Enhancing Physician Resilience to Generative AI: Multilevel Framework for Shared Authority, Verification, and Skill Preservation.","authorString":"Pan H, Liu J, Liu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Pan H","firstName":"Hongxia","lastName":"Pan","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2215-525X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Rehabilitation Medicine Center and Institute of Rehabilitation Medicine, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu J","firstName":"Jialin","lastName":"Liu","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Informatics, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Guoxue Xiang Street, Chengdu, Sichuan, 610041, China, 86 28-85422416."},{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu S","firstName":"Siru","lastName":"Liu","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Informatics, Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Nashville, TN, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2215-525X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","issn":"1439-4456","essn":"1438-8871","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e88058","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>As generative artificial intelligence (AI), particularly large language model-based tools, is increasingly integrated into diagnosis, triage, decision support, and treatment planning, it offers potential gains in efficiency and information access. However, real-world deployment also introduces important risks, including hallucinations, miscalibrated confidence, automation bias, and increased verification burden on physicians. This burden may divert attention from independent clinical reasoning, contribute to deskilling, and increase vulnerability when models fail silently or perform poorly in unfamiliar clinical contexts. Existing AI governance frameworks emphasize data quality, transparency, accountability, and ethical deployment, but pay less attention to physician-facing resilience, defined in this paper as the capacity to sustain independent and safe clinical judgment when collaborating with generative AI. In this viewpoint, we propose a multilevel governance framework organized around 3 coordinated domains: cognitive workload shaping, clinical authority governance and allocation, and organizational safety governance and accountability. Together, these domains aim to reduce verification burden, preserve physician decisional authority, and align institutional oversight with safe and context-sensitive AI use. The framework includes mechanisms such as risk-sensitive verification triggers, bounded delegation, structured interprofessional review, and organizational monitoring to support safe clinical integration while minimizing avoidable workflow disruption. At the same time, implementation may be limited by workflow friction, alert fatigue, variable institutional resources, and the need for ongoing monitoring and recalibration to ensure that safeguards remain clinically useful rather than burdensome. Accordingly, this paper outlines a structured governance framework to guide safer integration of generative AI into clinical care and inform future evaluation across specialties, workflows, and institutional settings.","affiliation":"Rehabilitation Medicine Center and Institute of Rehabilitation Medicine, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Clinical Competence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Physicians","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","AI","Physician","Accountability","Cognitive Load","Authority","Clinical Safety","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Generative Ai"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/88058"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13293561"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13293561?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-24","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-24"},{"id":"42224270","source":"MED","pmid":"42224270","pmcid":"PMC13225220","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13225220"]},"doi":"10.2196/97419","title":"Hospitalists Are Already Using AI-Why Implementation Will Determine Its Impact.","authorString":"Maw A, Pandita A, Burden M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Maw A","firstName":"Anna","lastName":"Maw","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2829-7331"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Hospital Medicine, University of Colorado School of Medicine, Leprino Building, 4th Floor, Mailstop F-782, 12401 E 17th Avenue, Aurora, CO, 80045, United States, 1 720 848 4289."}]}},{"fullName":"Pandita A","firstName":"Aakriti","lastName":"Pandita","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5591-2328"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Hospital Medicine, University of Colorado School of Medicine, Leprino Building, 4th Floor, Mailstop F-782, 12401 E 17th Avenue, Aurora, CO, 80045, United States, 1 720 848 4289."}]}},{"fullName":"Burden M","firstName":"Marisha","lastName":"Burden","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8262-3994"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Hospital Medicine, University of Colorado School of Medicine, Leprino Building, 4th Floor, Mailstop F-782, 12401 E 17th Avenue, Aurora, CO, 80045, United States, 1 720 848 4289."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2829-7331"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5591-2328"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8262-3994"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","issn":"1439-4456","essn":"1438-8871","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e97419","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>The adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) into clinical practice is accelerating, outpacing the development of organizational guidance, training, and governance. A recent study indicated that two-thirds of hospitalists are using AI, particularly large language model (LLM)-based platforms, in their clinical work. However, as with prior disruptive health technologies, adoption alone does not ensure meaningful improvement in care. Drawing on lessons from electronic health record implementation, we argue that AI's ultimate impact will be determined not by use rates, but by implementation quality and fit. Poorly implemented digital tools have been shown to increase clinician workload and burnout, despite their intended benefits. Early evidence on LLM-based diagnostic AI further underscores this risk: clinical-decision making supported by AI may be suboptimal when integration, training, and workflow design are inadequate. To provide value, AI tools must be thoughtfully embedded into clinical reasoning processes through evidence-informed training, intentional workflow design, and supportive organizational culture. As AI technologies are rapidly adopted, three priorities come into focus: training clinicians on AI inputs and interpreting outputs, applying implementation science frameworks for AI deployment in clinical environments, and establishing strategies for ongoing evaluation of the impact of AI tools over time. Implementation science frameworks offer practical guidance to assess workflow integration, training needs, infrastructure, and potential unintended consequences that can then inform adaptation of implementation strategies to enhance contextual fit. In parallel, learning health system infrastructure can enable continuous monitoring and iterative adaptation using routinely collected clinical and workflow data that reflect the value of the intervention across the quintuple aim of clinical outcomes, health equity, cost, and patient and clinician experience. AI adoption in hospital medicine is likely inevitable. Its ability to advance the quintuple aim will depend on how effectively these tools are implemented, supported, evaluated, and adapted in practice.","affiliation":"Division of Hospital Medicine, University of Colorado School of Medicine, Leprino Building, 4th Floor, Mailstop F-782, 12401 E 17th Avenue, Aurora, CO, 80045, United States, 1 720 848 4289.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["article-commentary","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Hospitalists"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Electronic Health Records"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Clinical Decision Support Systems","Implementation Science","Hospital Medicine","Generative Ai","Health Care Technology Adoption"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/97419"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13225220"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13225220?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-04","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},{"id":"41355258","source":"MED","pmid":"41355258","pmcid":"PMC12688312","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12688312"]},"doi":"10.5213/inj.2550274.137","title":"Privacy-by-Design Framework for Large Language Model Chatbots in Urology.","authorString":"Kim EJ, Kim J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kim EJ","firstName":"Eun Joung","lastName":"Kim","initials":"EJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Game Contents, Kyungil University, Gyeongsan, Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Kim J","firstName":"JungYoon","lastName":"Kim","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Game Media, College of Future Industry, Gachon University, Seongnam, Korea."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2396-9514"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"Suppl 2","volume":"29","journalIssueId":4067801,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-11-01","journal":{"title":"International neurourology journal","medlineAbbreviation":"Int Neurourol J","issn":"2093-4777","essn":"2093-6931","isoabbreviation":"Int Neurourol J","nlmid":"101534513"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"S65-S72","abstractText":"This review presents a privacy-by-design-based technical and governance framework for the safe clinical deployment of large language model (LLM) chatbots in urology. Given the high sensitivity of urological data involving urinary, sexual, and reproductive health, the proposed approach integrates on-site algorithmic deidentification, federated learning with differential privacy and secure aggregation, and secure retrieval-augmented generation with source citation and audit logging. Collectively, these components establish a federated, explainable, and auditable pipeline that preserves data sovereignty while improving clinical reliability and regulatory compliance. Urology thus serves as a critical test bed for validating the safety, governance, and accountability standards required for broader adoption of LLM-based medical chatbots across clinical domains.","affiliation":"Department of Game Contents, Kyungil University, Gyeongsan, Korea.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"2025-RISE-15","agency":"Gyeongsangbuk-do RISE project","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Urology","Medical Chatbot","Large Language Model","Privacy-by-design Framework"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.5213/inj.2550274.137"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12688312"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12688312?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2025-12-08","dateOfCreation":"2025-12-08","firstIndexDate":"2025-12-08","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-12-22","dateOfRevision":"2025-12-11","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-11-30","firstPublicationDate":"2025-11-30"},{"id":"42347687","source":"MED","pmid":"42347687","pmcid":"PMC13295427","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13295427"]},"doi":"10.2196/91727","title":"Personal Health Large Language Models and the Negotiation of Medical Authority in Clinical Care: Opportunities, Risks, and Governance.","authorString":"Xie W, Liu J, Liu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Xie W","firstName":"Wenyi","lastName":"Xie","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-7826-2927"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical Intensive Care Unit, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu J","firstName":"Jialin","lastName":"Liu","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Center, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, No. 37 Guo Xue Xiang Street, Chengdu, Sichuan, 610041, China, 86 28-85422416, 86 28-85582944."},{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu S","firstName":"Siru","lastName":"Liu","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Informatics, Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Nashville, TN, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-7826-2927"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","issn":"1439-4456","essn":"1438-8871","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e91727","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>Personal health large language models (PH-LLMs) are patient-facing conversational systems that synthesize user-entered information, patient-generated health data, wearable data, and selected personal health records-where users choose to connect them-into personalized, longitudinal, action-oriented health narratives. Unlike generic health chatbots that mainly provide one-off responses to isolated questions, PH-LLMs may generate continuing interpretations, priorities, and candidate next steps that patients bring into clinical encounters. In contrast to electronic health record-tethered clinical artificial intelligence (AI), they often originate outside institutional oversight and may be selected, used, or trusted by patients before professional review. This viewpoint examines how PH-LLMs may reshape the negotiation of medical authority by contributing to a shift from the traditional dyadic clinician-patient relationship toward a triadic model of negotiated authority, in which clinicians may increasingly need to mediate among clinical evidence, patient values, and algorithmic narratives. PH-LLMs may support patient participation by organizing symptoms, contextualizing home-monitoring and wearable data, improving health literacy, assisting chronic disease self-management, and preparing patients for more collaborative visits. Patient-facing AI narratives may also introduce distinct risks. At the individual level, these include inaccurate or incomplete responses arising from imprecise queries or missing context, misinterpretation of otherwise accurate information in the absence of clinical context, and contextually biased or poorly matched advice across demographic, cultural, linguistic, disability-related, or socioeconomic contexts. At the system level, they include authority conflict when AI recommendations diverge from clinical judgment, fragmentation of clinical truth, privacy and data-governance concerns, diffusion of accountability when harm results from advice produced outside clinical governance, and inequitable access to premium tools and continuous monitoring devices. To address these challenges, we propose a 3-layer clinical governance framework for patient-brought PH-LLM narratives. The first layer, evidence and provenance, makes AI-generated narratives epistemically legible by clarifying platform identity, data sources, temporal anchoring, uncertainty, and privacy-relevant data-use and retention conditions. The second layer, clinical arbitration and workflow integration, uses risk-stratified intake, proportionate documentation, escalation triggers, and equity-preserving workflows to embed PH-LLM outputs into routine care. The third layer, competence and accountability, defines the communication competencies, AI literacy supports, institutional responsibilities, vendor accountability, and risk-proportionate verification duties needed for triadic care. This framework is a conceptual and governance-oriented proposal rather than a validated clinical protocol. Future empirical work should evaluate its feasibility, documentation burden, equity effects, clinical safety impact, and acceptability among patients, clinicians, and health systems. Governed through these interdependent layers, PH-LLMs may serve as supporting infrastructure for safer, person-centered longitudinal care.","affiliation":"Medical Intensive Care Unit, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Negotiating"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Physician-Patient Relations"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Health Records, Personal"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Electronic Health Records"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Clinical Governance","Accountability","Shared Decision-making","Medical Authority","Digital Health","Clinician-patient Relationship","Patient-generated Health Data","Personal Health Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/91727"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13295427"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13295427?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-25","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-25"},{"id":"PMC13296990","source":"PMC","pmcid":"PMC13296990","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13296990"]},"title":"Beyond accuracy: how generative artificial intelligence reshapes the foundations of public health discourse","journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"36","journalIssueId":4214586,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"European journal of public health","medlineAbbreviation":"Eur J Public Health","issn":"1101-1262","essn":"1464-360X","isoabbreviation":"Eur J Public Health","nlmid":"9204966"}},"pubYear":"2026","language":"eng","pubModel":"Undetermined","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comment","discussion"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"PubMedCentral","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13296990/?tool=EBI"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"PubMedCentral","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13296990/pdf/?tool=EBI"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13296990"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13296990?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-27","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-25"},{"id":"42269012","source":"MED","pmid":"42269012","pmcid":"PMC13252702","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13252702"]},"doi":"10.2196/93470","title":"Ethical Governance of Large Language Models in Health Care: Trust, Responsibility, and Equity in Routine Use.","authorString":"Yang X, Xu C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Yang X","firstName":"Xiongwen","lastName":"Yang","initials":"X","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4968-8953"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Surgery, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, 83 Zhongshan East Road, Guiyang, Guizhou, 550002, China, 86 18620726507, 86 18620726507."},{"affiliation":"NHC Key Laboratory of Pulmonary Immunological Diseases, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, Guiyang, Guizhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu C","firstName":"Chuan","lastName":"Xu","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-5849-8780"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Surgery, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, 83 Zhongshan East Road, Guiyang, Guizhou, 550002, China, 86 18620726507, 86 18620726507."},{"affiliation":"NHC Key Laboratory of Pulmonary Immunological Diseases, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, Guiyang, Guizhou, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4968-8953"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-5849-8780"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","issn":"1439-4456","essn":"1438-8871","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e93470","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>Large language models (LLMs) are becoming increasingly embedded in routine health care communication, raising ethical challenges that extend beyond model performance alone. This Viewpoint argues that ethical risks in LLM-enabled health care emerge through patterns of reliance, institutional embedding, and governance during real-world use. Using \"adoption-phase ethics\" as an analytic lens, this paper examines 3 interrelated dimensions of ethical risk. First, trust in LLM-enabled health care is shaped not only by technical accuracy, but also by institutional and relational conditions surrounding its use. Second, responsibility may become distributed and ambiguous when LLM-mediated information influences clinical communication and decision-making. Third, equity concerns arise from unequal capacities to interpret, contest, and benefit from LLM-generated information. We argue that ethical governance of LLMs in health care requires continuous, system-level oversight that extends beyond model evaluation alone, including clear accountability structures, role-sensitive implementation, and equity-oriented governance practices. By reframing ethical analysis around routine integration rather than technical performance alone, this Viewpoint aims to support more responsible and sustainable use of LLMs in health care.","affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Surgery, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, 83 Zhongshan East Road, Guiyang, Guizhou, 550002, China, 86 18620726507, 86 18620726507.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Trust"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Social Responsibility"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Delivery of Health Care","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ES","qualifierName":"ethics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Medical Ethics","Responsibility","Technology Adoption","Health Care Communication","Ethical Governance","Large Language Models","Equity And Trust"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/93470"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13252702"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13252702?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-11","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-10"},{"id":"PPR1275962","source":"PPR","doi":"10.14293/pr2199.004004.v1","title":"Application and Impact of Large Language Model (LLM) Agents in Financial Operations and Public Policy","authorString":"Izev M, Johansson G.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Izev M","firstName":"Mckenzie","lastName":"Izev","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-8450-4944"}},{"fullName":"Johansson G","firstName":"Greg","lastName":"Johansson","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-7856-0639"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-7856-0639"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-8450-4944"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"The rapid digitalization of finance, accelerated by advances in artificial intelligence, has positioned Large Language Models (LLMs) as transformative tools not only for private-sector financial operations but also for public-sector economic governance. While much of the existing literature has focused on LLM applications in investment and trading, their potential to reshape core financial operations—such as regulatory compliance, fraud detection, contract intelligence, and risk reporting—remains underexplored. Simultaneously, governments and central banks are beginning to grapple with how LLM agents can assist in policy formulation, macroeconomic simulation, fiscal transparency, and real-time economic sentiment monitoring. This research undertakes a systematic examination of the deployment of LLM agents across these dual domains, with a particular emphasis on operational efficiency, decision support quality, and institutional accountability. Preliminary findings suggest that while LLM agents significantly reduce processing times for tasks like regulatory reporting and contract review, their outputs exhibit concerning variability in reasoning consistency and are susceptible to subtle prompt-induced biases. In the policy domain, LLMs demonstrate promise in summarizing vast economic literature and generating scenario simulations, yet they struggle with causal reasoning and fail to adequately quantify uncertainty—raising serious concerns for their use in fiscal and monetary decision-making. The research concludes by proposing a human-in-the-loop governance architecture that balances automation with oversight, alongside a set of regulatory benchmarks for evaluating LLM reliability in financial and policy settings. By bridging the technical, operational, and ethical dimensions of LLM adoption, this study contributes to the emerging field of AI governance in finance and offers practical guidance for institutions navigating the transition toward AI-augmented financial ecosystems.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"ScienceOpen Preprints","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.14293/pr2199.004004.v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1275962","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-07-01","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-01"},{"id":"41534067","source":"MED","pmid":"41534067","pmcid":"PMC12853083","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12853083"]},"doi":"10.2196/86550","title":"From Agents to Governance: Essential AI Skills for Clinicians in the Large Language Model Era.","authorString":"Cao W, Zhang Q, Liu J, Liu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Cao W","firstName":"Weiping","lastName":"Cao","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9117-6367"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, The People's Hospital of Leshan, Leshan, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang Q","firstName":"Qing","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"Q","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5652-4036"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu J","firstName":"Jialin","lastName":"Liu","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Center, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Informatics, West China Medical School, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu S","firstName":"Siru","lastName":"Liu","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Informatics, Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Nashville, TN, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5652-4036"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9117-6367"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4082344,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","issn":"1439-4456","essn":"1438-8871","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e86550","abstractText":"Large language models are rapidly transitioning from pilot schemes to routine clinical practice. This creates an urgent need for clinicians to develop the necessary skills to strike the right balance between seizing opportunities and taking accountability. We propose a 3-tier competency framework to support clinicians' evolution from cautious users to responsible stewards of artificial intelligence (AI). Tier 1 (foundational skills) defines the minimum competencies for safe use, including prompt engineering, human-AI agent interaction, security and privacy awareness, and the clinician-patient interface (transparency and consent). Tier 2 (intermediate skills) emphasizes evaluative expertise, including bias detection and mitigation, interpretation of explainability outputs, and the effective clinical integration of AI-generated workflows. Tier 3 (advanced skills) establishes leadership capabilities, mandating competencies in ethical governance (delineating accountability and liability boundaries), regulatory strategy, and model life cycle management-specifically, the ability to govern algorithmic adaptation and change protocols. Integrating this framework into continuing medical education programs and role-specific job descriptions could enhance clinicians' ability to use AI safely and responsibly. This could standardize deployment and support safer clinical practice, with the potential to improve patient outcomes.","affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Agent","Education","Continuing Medical Education","Competency","Clinician","Large Language Model"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/86550"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12853083"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12853083?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-15","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-30","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-26","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-14"},{"id":"42199991","source":"MED","pmid":"42199991","pmcid":"PMC13199327","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13199327"]},"doi":"10.3389/froh.2026.1837073","title":"Feasibility of a multi-metric framework for evaluating patient-facing AI communication in cosmetic dentistry: an exploratory proof-of-concept study.","authorString":"Al-Haddad A, Al-Karadsheh O, Hassona Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Al-Haddad A","firstName":"Alaa","lastName":"Al-Haddad","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7210-0391"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Restorative Dentistry, School of Dentistry, The University of Jordan, Amman, Jordan."}]}},{"fullName":"Al-Karadsheh O","firstName":"Omar","lastName":"Al-Karadsheh","initials":"O","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7040-7207"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery and Oral Medicine, School of Dentistry, The University of Jordan, Amman, Jordan."}]}},{"fullName":"Hassona Y","firstName":"Yazan","lastName":"Hassona","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery and Oral Medicine, School of Dentistry, The University of Jordan, Amman, Jordan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7210-0391"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7040-7207"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"7","journalIssueId":4118129,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in oral health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Oral Health","issn":"2673-4842","essn":"2673-4842","isoabbreviation":"Front Oral Health","nlmid":"9918227262706676"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1837073","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used by the public to obtain oral health information, yet reproducible methods to benchmark the communication quality of patient-facing outputs remain underdeveloped. Prior evaluations have focused mainly on factual accuracy and guideline concordance, while giving less attention to whether responses are understandable, actionable, empathetic, well structured, and bounded by appropriate safety messaging. This gap is especially relevant in cosmetic dentistry, where patients often make elective and potentially irreversible decisions based on online information.<h4>Methods</h4>This proof-of-concept comparative benchmarking study used a consolidated 80-prompt test set derived from thematic analysis of real-world patient inquiries and cross-LLM synthesis across four cosmetic dentistry domains: tooth whitening, veneers, implants, and orthodontic aligners. Responses from an instruction-configured assistant (CSA-GPT) and a general-purpose baseline (ChatGPT5.2) were generated under controlled conditions, yielding 160 responses. Two board-certified specialists independently evaluated all responses using a theory-informed, exploratory 20-point rubric assessing readability (Flesch-Kincaid Grade Level, FKGL), ethical disclaimer inclusion, practicality, empathetic tone, and structural clarity. A separate clinical safety audit assessed major factual errors and critical omissions. Between-model comparisons used paired analyses with effect sizes, and linear mixed-effects models examined Model, Domain, and Model × Domain interaction.<h4>Results</h4>CSA-GPT outperformed ChatGPT5.2 across all evaluated communication metrics. Mean total rubric score was 17.95 ± 1.62 for CSA-GPT vs. 9.55 ± 1.94 for ChatGPT5.2 (<i>p</i> < 0.001; Cohen's <i>d</i> = 3.22). Mean FKGL was lower for CSA-GPT (6.07 ± 1.28) than for ChatGPT5.2 (9.12 ± 1.71; <i>p</i> < 0.001). Practicality, empathetic tone, and structural clarity were all significantly higher for CSA-GPT (all <i>p</i> < 0.001). Mandatory disclaimers were present in 100% of CSA-GPT responses and 0% of ChatGPT5.2 responses. Safety audit error rates were low and did not differ significantly between models (1.25% vs. 3.75%, <i>p</i> = 0.25). Mixed-effects models confirmed a strong overall model effect, with domain-dependent interactions for readability, empathy, and structural clarity.<h4>Conclusions</h4>In this exploratory proof-of-concept study, instruction configuration improved the patient-facing communication quality of LLM responses in cosmetic dentistry across readability, practicality, empathetic tone, structural clarity, and safety boundary-setting, without increasing major factual errors. These findings support the feasibility of a multi-metric benchmarking approach for evaluating patient-facing dental AI, while highlighting the need for psychometric refinement, external validation, and broader testing before such approaches can inform governance or implementation.","affiliation":"Department of Restorative Dentistry, School of Dentistry, The University of Jordan, Amman, Jordan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Health Informatics","Cosmetic Dentistry","Communication Quality","Large Language Models","Patient-facing Ai","Exploratory Benchmarking"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/froh.2026.1837073"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13199327"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13199327?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-11"},{"id":"42281931","source":"MED","pmid":"42281931","pmcid":"PMC13250854","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13250854"]},"doi":"10.3389/fmed.2026.1811333","title":"Development and validation of an artificial intelligence proof-of-concept tool for risk-based quality assessment of generic medicines: a South African case study.","authorString":"Danks L, Semete-Makokotlela B, Reynecke C, Dijeng S, Kgosietsile B, van Tonder J, van Graan C, Hinch J, Kruger A, Walker S, Salek S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Danks L","firstName":"Lorraine","lastName":"Danks","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Health, Medicine and Life Sciences, University of Hertfordshire, Hatfield, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Semete-Makokotlela B","firstName":"Boitumelo","lastName":"Semete-Makokotlela","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"South African Health Products Regulatory Authority (SAHPRA), Pretoria, South Africa."}]}},{"fullName":"Reynecke C","firstName":"Christelna","lastName":"Reynecke","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"South African Health Products Regulatory Authority (SAHPRA), Pretoria, South Africa."}]}},{"fullName":"Dijeng S","firstName":"Seima","lastName":"Dijeng","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Botswana Medicines Regulatory Authority (BoMRA), Gaborone, Botswana."}]}},{"fullName":"Kgosietsile B","firstName":"Bathusi","lastName":"Kgosietsile","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Botswana Medicines Regulatory Authority (BoMRA), Gaborone, Botswana."}]}},{"fullName":"van Tonder J","firstName":"Japie","lastName":"van Tonder","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Scigenix (Pty) Ltd., Pretoria, South Africa."}]}},{"fullName":"van Graan C","firstName":"Cornelia","lastName":"van Graan","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9429-9562"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Scigenix (Pty) Ltd., Pretoria, South Africa."}]}},{"fullName":"Hinch J","firstName":"Jason","lastName":"Hinch","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Scigenix (Pty) Ltd., Pretoria, South Africa."}]}},{"fullName":"Kruger A","firstName":"Adriaan","lastName":"Kruger","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"nuvoteQ (Pty) Ltd., Pretoria, South Africa."}]}},{"fullName":"Walker S","firstName":"Stuart","lastName":"Walker","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Health, Medicine and Life Sciences, University of Hertfordshire, Hatfield, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Salek S","firstName":"Sam","lastName":"Salek","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Health, Medicine and Life Sciences, University of Hertfordshire, Hatfield, United Kingdom."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9429-9562"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"13","journalIssueId":4110742,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Med (Lausanne)","issn":"2296-858X","essn":"2296-858X","isoabbreviation":"Front Med (Lausanne)","nlmid":"101648047"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1811333","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Regulatory authorities in African countries face persistent challenges in ensuring the timely authorization of quality generic medicines due to limited technical capacity and extensive review timelines. The South African medicines regulator (SAHPRA) piloted a risk-based assessment (RBA) framework to expedite the Chemistry, Manufacturing, and Controls (CMC) evaluation of generic medicines. Building on this initiative, an artificial intelligence (AI)-driven tool, termed LEXI, was developed and validated to automate SAHPRA's RBA triage for generic medicines and was externally validated at two African NRAs.<h4>Methods</h4>LEXI was designed based on SAHPRA's established RBA quality matrix, using 210 of its product dossiers, and was validated under the Good Automated Manufacturing Practice (GAMP5) framework. The system employed Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) architecture with Meta's Llama 3.1 large language model, supported by regulatory data integrations from the European Directorate for the Quality of Medicines (EDQM) and the World Health Organization Prequalification Programme. Validation comprised three qualification phases-installation (IQ), operational (OQ), and performance (PQ)-and was conducted across two African national regulatory authorities (NRAs): SAHPRA and the Botswana Medicines Regulatory Authority (BoMRA).<h4>Results</h4>During external validation across 60 dossiers, LEXI demonstrated 91.7% overall predictive accuracy (55/60; 95% CI: 81.6-97.2%), with sensitivity of 96.9% (31/32; 95% CI: 83.8-99.9%) and specificity of 85.7% (24/28; 95% CI: 67.3-96.0%), with a median 91% time reduction; performance varied by criterion and dossier structure. Criterion-level accuracy exceeded 95% for most critical quality attributes (CQAs). Adaptations allowed for effective operation across different dossier structures and local regulatory environments, confirming its interoperability and robustness.<h4>Discussion</h4>The validation of LEXI highlights the feasibility of secure, traceable AI integration into regulatory workflows. The tool augments human expertise by automating risk classification and dossier triage, enabling consistent, evidence-based decision support. These findings align with global regulatory trends at mature authorities, emphasizing AI's role in improving regulatory agility while maintaining scientific rigor.<h4>Conclusion</h4>LEXI represents a validated proof-of-concept for AI-assisted, risk-based regulatory assessment in resource-constrained settings. Its demonstrated efficiency and accuracy support its potential to enhance regulatory workflows and reduce review backlogs of generic medicines, pending further validation across a broader range of product types, including complex formulations, and diverse regulatory environments.","affiliation":"School of Health, Medicine and Life Sciences, University of Hertfordshire, Hatfield, United Kingdom.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Generic Medicines","Universal Health Coverage","Low- And Middle-income Countries","Risk-based Assessment","Sahpra","Bomra","Llama 3.1"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2026.1811333"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13250854"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13250854?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-27"},{"id":"41111869","source":"MED","pmid":"41111869","pmcid":"PMC12534133","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12534133"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.92571","title":"Building a Safe and Transparent Workflow for Large Language Model (LLM)-Assisted Clinical Trials and Prediction Models: A Technical Report.","authorString":"Frutuoso J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Frutuoso J","firstName":"João","lastName":"Frutuoso","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1536-6676"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Critical Care, Unidade Local de Saúde Lisboa Ocidental, Lisbon, PRT."},{"affiliation":"Management, Natura Clinica Medica, Lisbon, PRT."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1536-6676"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"17","journalIssueId":4009374,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-09-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","issn":"2168-8184","essn":"2168-8184","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"e92571","abstractText":"The use of large language models (LLMs) in clinical trials and prediction models is expanding rapidly, offering opportunities for efficiency but also raising concerns about privacy, fairness, accuracy, and accountability. This technical report proposes a structured workflow to support research teams in adopting LLMs while preserving scientific standards and public trust. The workflow is organized into seven sequential steps: (i) scope definition and governance, (ii) retrieval-augmented literature review, (iii) model evaluation and benchmarking, (iv) documentation and audit trail, (v) expert quality gates, (vi) manuscript disclosure, and (vii) privacy and security safeguards. To facilitate adoption, we provide reusable checklists that map study types to relevant international reporting guidelines, including Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials - Artificial Intelligence (CONSORT-AI), Standard Protocol Items: Recommendations for Interventional Trials - Artificial Intelligence (SPIRIT-AI), Transparent Reporting of a Multivariable Prediction Model for Individual Prognosis or Diagnosis - Artificial Intelligence (TRIPOD+AI), Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA), and Developmental and Exploratory Clinical Investigations of Decision - Support Systems Driven by Artificial Intelligence (DECIDE-AI). The framework is designed to mitigate risks such as fabricated citations, biased outputs from skewed datasets, and over-reliance on automated text. Rather than replacing human reasoning, it aims to augment it, offering greater speed while maintaining accountability, reproducibility, and transparency. By combining governance rules, technical safeguards, and human oversight, this workflow provides a practical and auditable path for integrating LLMs into clinical trials and prediction models without eroding confidence in scientific work.","affiliation":"Critical Care, Unidade Local de Saúde Lisboa Ocidental, Lisbon, PRT.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["other","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Publication Ethics","Research Integrity","Reporting Guidelines","Generative Ai","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.92571"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12534133"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12534133?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2025-10-20","dateOfCreation":"2025-10-20","firstIndexDate":"2025-10-20","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-12-04","dateOfRevision":"2025-10-22","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-09-17","firstPublicationDate":"2025-09-17"},{"id":"42328270","source":"MED","pmid":"42328270","pmcid":"PMC13275664","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13275664"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1730457","title":"Designing and evaluating large language model-enabled clinical decision support for heart failure: a modular and risk-tiered framework.","authorString":"Zhu W, Peng J, Yan Z, Chen Y, Xu J, Zhang L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zhu W","firstName":"Wenfang","lastName":"Zhu","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Anhui Chest Hospital, Anhui, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Peng J","firstName":"Jin","lastName":"Peng","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"West China College of Basic Medicine and Forensic Medicine, Sichuan University, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yan Z","firstName":"Zhi","lastName":"Yan","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Transformation of Scientific and Technological Achievements, Capital Medical University, Beijing, China."},{"affiliation":"School of Medical Technology, Sichuan College of Traditional Chinese Medicine, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen Y","firstName":"Yuhong","lastName":"Chen","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Anhui Chest Hospital, Anhui, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu J","firstName":"Jinpeng","lastName":"Xu","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Anhui Chest Hospital, Anhui, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang L","firstName":"Liang","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiac Surgery, Anhui Chest Hospital, Anhui, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","issn":"2673-253X","essn":"2673-253X","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1730457","abstractText":"Heart failure (HF) care requires repeated decisions across suspected disease, diagnostic confirmation, phenotyping, guideline-directed medical therapy, device consideration, worsening HF, transition care, and advanced HF planning. Large language models (LLMs) may support this work by synthesizing structured and unstructured electronic health record data, retrieving current evidence, and presenting patient-specific reasoning. However, an HF-specific LLM clinical decision support system should not be framed as a single autonomous agent. We present the Heart Failure Intelligent Agent (HF-IA) as a modular, risk-tiered conceptual framework in which agent functions have different data requirements, reference standards, risk levels, and validation pathways. We argue that evaluation should combine node-level tests, longitudinal case replay, silent prospective validation, and post-deployment monitoring. This framework is conceptual and does not claim clinical effectiveness; its value is to clarify design, evaluation, and governance requirements for future LLM-enabled HF decision support.","affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Anhui Chest Hospital, Anhui, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Heart Failure","Clinical Decision Support","Large Language Model","Retrieval-augmented Generation","Gold-standard Case Replay","Risk-tiered Autonomy"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1730457"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13275664"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13275664?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-22","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-04","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-04"},{"id":"41664164","source":"MED","pmid":"41664164","pmcid":"PMC12896320","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12896320"]},"doi":"10.1186/s13045-026-01781-y","title":"Implementing generative artificial intelligence in precision oncology: safety, governance, and significance.","authorString":"Hamamoto R, Koyama T, Takahashi S, Yasuda T, Kobayashi K, Akagi Y, Kouno N, Sudo K, Hirata M, Sunami K, Kubo T, Katayama H, Takashima A, Taniguchi T, Matsumoto H, Shibaki R, Asada K, Komatsu M, Kaneko S, Yamada M, Horinouchi H, Tanaka K, Goto Y, Kato K, Saito Y, Nakamura K, Yamamoto N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hamamoto R","firstName":"Ryuji","lastName":"Hamamoto","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2632-1334"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan. rhamamot@ncc.go.jp."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan. rhamamot@ncc.go.jp."}]}},{"fullName":"Koyama T","firstName":"Takafumi","lastName":"Koyama","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Experimental Therapeutics, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Takahashi S","firstName":"Satoshi","lastName":"Takahashi","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Yasuda T","firstName":"Tomohiro","lastName":"Yasuda","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kobayashi K","firstName":"Kazuma","lastName":"Kobayashi","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Experimental Therapeutics, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Akagi Y","firstName":"Yu","lastName":"Akagi","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Informatics, Graduate School of Medicine, The University of Tokyo, 7-3-1 Hongo Bunkyo-ku, Tokyo, 113-8655, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kouno N","firstName":"Nobuji","lastName":"Kouno","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, Graduate School of Medicine, Kyoto University, Kyoto, 606-8507, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Sudo K","firstName":"Kazuki","lastName":"Sudo","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Experimental Therapeutics, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Oncology, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Hirata M","firstName":"Makoto","lastName":"Hirata","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9994-9958"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Genetic Medicine and Services, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Sunami K","firstName":"Kuniko","lastName":"Sunami","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Laboratory Medicine, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kubo T","firstName":"Takashi","lastName":"Kubo","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Laboratory Medicine, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Katayama H","firstName":"Hiroshi","lastName":"Katayama","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Research Support Office, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Takashima A","firstName":"Atsuo","lastName":"Takashima","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Gastrointestinal Medical Oncology, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Taniguchi T","firstName":"Tomonori","lastName":"Taniguchi","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Matsumoto H","firstName":"Hiromi","lastName":"Matsumoto","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Shibaki R","firstName":"Ryota","lastName":"Shibaki","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Asada K","firstName":"Ken","lastName":"Asada","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Komatsu M","firstName":"Masaaki","lastName":"Komatsu","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kaneko S","firstName":"Syuzo","lastName":"Kaneko","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Yamada M","firstName":"Masayoshi","lastName":"Yamada","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Endoscopy Division, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Horinouchi H","firstName":"Hidehito","lastName":"Horinouchi","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Oncology, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Tanaka K","firstName":"Katsuya","lastName":"Tanaka","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"IT Integrating and Support Center, National Cancer Center, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Goto Y","firstName":"Yasushi","lastName":"Goto","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Oncology, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kato K","firstName":"Ken","lastName":"Kato","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Gastrointestinal Medical Oncology, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Saito Y","firstName":"Yutaka","lastName":"Saito","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Endoscopy Division, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Nakamura K","firstName":"Kenichi","lastName":"Nakamura","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Research Support Office, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Yamamoto N","firstName":"Noboru","lastName":"Yamamoto","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Experimental Therapeutics, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2632-1334"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9994-9958"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"19","journalIssueId":4120300,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of hematology & oncology","medlineAbbreviation":"J Hematol Oncol","issn":"1756-8722","essn":"1756-8722","isoabbreviation":"J Hematol Oncol","nlmid":"101468937"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"14","abstractText":"The paramount challenge in precision oncology lies in further improving quality of life and response rates for individual patients. Efforts toward these goals are steadily expanding the scope of clinical implementation, despite ongoing challenges such as standardization, cost-effectiveness, and data harmonization. Building upon this maturing foundation, generative AI-which has evolved dramatically in recent years-is particularly valuable at this stage of advancing efficiency and adoption as an auxiliary technology linking literature, guidelines, trial protocols, and patient data. Specifically, through mutation interpretation, trial eligibility matching, and tumor board support, it is expected to contribute to advancing standardization, improving cost-effectiveness, accelerating data harmonization, and further accelerating human-centered decision-making. Accordingly, this review surveys the development history of generative AI and its current healthcare applications, organizing its implementation potential for precision oncology along three axes: (1) generative AI-based interpretation of genetic mutations and estimation of their pathological significance; (2) generative AI-driven verification of clinical trial eligibility; and (3) multimodal foundation models for imaging and pathology that compute \"tumor phenotypes\" using real-world data, contributing to report drafting and molecular surrogate estimation. In response, we propose a strategy centered on retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) and human-in-the-loop (HITL) workflows, encompassing data preparation based on OMOP, mCODE, and FHIR; multicenter prospective evaluation; auditable logs and governance aligned with Good Manufacturing Practice (GMP) and the EU AI Act; and a synthetic data strategy including differential privacy. Ultimately, this approach validates value through real-world outcomes and charts a path toward \"learning oncology,\" accelerating patient-centered decision-making and clinical trial development.","affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan. rhamamot@ncc.go.jp.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"the MEXT subsidy for the Advanced Integrated Intelligence Platform","agency":"Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"BRIDGE","agency":"Cabinet Office, Government of Japan","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Neoplasms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Medical Oncology","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Precision Medicine","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Transformer","Precision Oncology","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Model","Vision Language Model","Retrieval-augmented Generation"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13045-026-01781-y"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12896320"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12896320?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["nct","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-07","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-07","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-09"},{"id":"PPR1188274","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-9684485/v1","title":"Trust-Governed Agentic AI for Cyber-Resilient Digital Markets: Blockchain-Audited Federated Intelligence Under Uncertainty","authorString":"Khorasani NSM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Khorasani NSM","firstName":"Narges Sadat Mirhosseini","lastName":"Khorasani","initials":"NSM"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>Cyber-resilient digital markets increasingly rely on autonomous agents, federated intelligence, blockchain-enabled accountability, and large language model support to sustain trustworthy operation under uncertainty and cyber disruption. However, existing studies often address these components separately, leaving a gap in unified frameworks that can jointly manage trust degradation, adversarial data manipulation, distributed learning, decision validation, and auditable recovery. This paper proposes a trust-governed agentic AI framework for cyber-resilient digital markets under dynamic uncertainty and hybrid cyber threats. The proposed architecture integrates trust-state modeling, trust-weighted consensus, federated intelligence, blockchain-based auditability, and LLM-assisted decision validation and repair. Agent reliability is continuously evaluated through cyber-degraded evidence, while unreliable decisions are down-weighted before consensus and federated aggregation. A blockchain audit layer preserves tamper-resistant accountability records, and an LLM-assisted repair mechanism supports constraint-aware correction of unsafe or inconsistent actions. The framework is formulated through a risk-aware market objective that incorporates operational feasibility, trust admissibility, audit consistency, and cyber-resilience constraints. Simulation-based evaluation under normal, mild, moderate, severe, and extreme degradation scenarios demonstrates that the proposed model improves trust-aware decision reliability, reduces unsafe actions, strengthens audit completeness, and supports resilient market continuation under compromised operating conditions. The results indicate that combining agentic AI with trust governance and blockchain-audited federated intelligence can provide a practical foundation for secure, explainable, and resilient digital market operation.</p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9684485/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1188274","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-05-13","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-13"},{"id":"41964054","source":"MED","pmid":"41964054","pmcid":"PMC13185178","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13185178"]},"doi":"10.1186/s13104-026-07823-5","title":"Protocol deviation outlier estimation combined with generative AI.","authorString":"Spyroglou II, Řeháková M, Klapka R, Kacprzycka A, Fernandez MCL, Adyanthaya R, Young K, Weintraub J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Spyroglou II","firstName":"Ioannis I","lastName":"Spyroglou","initials":"II","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Merck & Co., Inc., Rahway, NJ, 07065, USA. ioannis.spyroglou@msd.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Řeháková M","firstName":"Magdaléna","lastName":"Řeháková","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Merck & Co., Inc., Rahway, NJ, 07065, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Klapka R","firstName":"Radim","lastName":"Klapka","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Merck & Co., Inc., Rahway, NJ, 07065, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Kacprzycka A","firstName":"Anna","lastName":"Kacprzycka","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Merck & Co., Inc., Rahway, NJ, 07065, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Fernandez MCL","firstName":"Maria Consuelo Luguera","lastName":"Fernandez","initials":"MCL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Merck & Co., Inc., Rahway, NJ, 07065, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Adyanthaya R","firstName":"Ronojit","lastName":"Adyanthaya","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Merck & Co., Inc., Rahway, NJ, 07065, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Young K","firstName":"Kyle","lastName":"Young","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Merck & Co., Inc., Rahway, NJ, 07065, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Weintraub J","firstName":"Jolie","lastName":"Weintraub","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Merck & Co., Inc., Rahway, NJ, 07065, USA."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"19","journalIssueId":4179182,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"BMC research notes","medlineAbbreviation":"BMC Res Notes","issn":"1756-0500","essn":"1756-0500","isoabbreviation":"BMC Res Notes","nlmid":"101462768"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"226","abstractText":"OBJECTIVE: Protocol deviations (PDs) are critical indicators of data quality and operational risk in clinical trials. Systematic under- or over-reporting of PDs at investigator sites can obscure true trial performance and compromise regulatory compliance. The goal of this paper is to present an integrated approach combining statistical outlier detection with generative AI-powered content analysis for detecting and characterizing outlier sites in PD reporting, enabling proactive risk management and targeted site oversight. RESULTS: In this study, the simaerep bootstrap algorithm was applied across 578 clinical studies encompassing 39,936 sites. It is shown that the method successfully identifies sites exhibiting statistically significant deviations from expected PD reporting rates, flagging both potential under-reporters and high-rate reporters. In addition, generative AI combined with hierarchical clustering automatically categorized diverse PD narratives into a validated taxonomy of 15 major categories and 47 specific topics through a \"human-in-the-loop\" refinement process with clinical operations experts. Independent validation of the GenAI topic classification using an \"LLM-as-judge\" approach achieved 88.7% accuracy (95% CI: 86.5%–90.6%). This integrated capability equips clinical operations teams to identify sites warranting further investigation and determine whether observed deviation patterns are study-systemic, country-specific, or site-specific, ultimately improving data integrity and risk-based audit strategy.","affiliation":"Merck & Co., Inc., Rahway, NJ, 07065, USA. ioannis.spyroglou@msd.com.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Algorithms"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Clinical Trials as Topic","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"SN","qualifierName":"statistics & numerical data","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["risk management","Data Quality","Clinical Trials","Outlier Detection","Protocol Deviations","Generative Ai"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13104-026-07823-5"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13185178"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13185178?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-11","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-21","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-10"},{"id":"PPR1255175","source":"PPR","doi":"10.14293/pr2199.003853.v1","title":"From Retrieval to Autonomy: Governance and Accountability Challenges in Tool-Calling Large Language Model Agents","authorString":"G. Anand L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"G. Anand L","firstName":"Lawal","lastName":"G. Anand","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-7063-9086"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-7063-9086"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"The evolution of large language models (LLMs) from passive text generators to autonomous agents capable of invoking external tools, accessing databases, executing code, and interacting with digital environments has significantly expanded their practical utility across diverse domains. While tool-calling architectures enhance problem-solving capabilities and enable dynamic decision-making, they also introduce complex governance and accountability challenges that extend beyond traditional concerns associated with standalone LLMs. Autonomous agents can perform multi-step actions with limited human intervention, creating new risks related to transparency, responsibility attribution, security vulnerabilities, privacy violations, and unintended consequences. This paper critically examines the governance implications of tool-calling LLM agents, focusing on the tension between increased autonomy and the need for effective oversight. The study explores key accountability issues, including decision traceability, auditability, explainability, human-in-the-loop control, regulatory compliance, and liability allocation among developers, deployers, and end users. Furthermore, it analyzes emerging governance frameworks and technical safeguards designed to ensure trustworthy agent behavior in high-stakes environments such as healthcare, finance, cybersecurity, and public administration. The paper argues that existing governance mechanisms developed for conventional AI systems are insufficient to address the operational complexity of autonomous tool-using agents. A multidimensional governance approach integrating technical controls, organizational policies, ethical principles, and regulatory standards is therefore proposed. The findings contribute to ongoing discussions on responsible AI deployment by highlighting pathways for balancing innovation, autonomy, and accountability in next-generation intelligent systems.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"ScienceOpen Preprints","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.14293/pr2199.003853.v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1255175","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-06-16","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-19","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-16"},{"id":"42211738","source":"MED","pmid":"42211738","pmcid":"PMC13214549","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13214549"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.xjmad.2026.100181","title":"Current themes of AI in mental health: Actionable evidence and guardrails for mood and anxiety care.","authorString":"Paulus MP.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Paulus MP","firstName":"Martin P","lastName":"Paulus","initials":"MP","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0825-3606"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laureate Institute for Brain Research, Tulsa, OK, USA."},{"affiliation":"Oxley College of Health and Natural Sciences, University of Tulsa, Tulsa, OK, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, University of California, San Diego, La Jolla, CA, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0825-3606"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4182059,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of mood and anxiety disorders","medlineAbbreviation":"J Mood Anxiety Disord","issn":"2950-0044","essn":"2950-0044","isoabbreviation":"J Mood Anxiety Disord","nlmid":"9918646056406676"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"100181","abstractText":"Currently, artificial intelligence (AI) is clinically relevant to mood and anxiety care, but the evidence base is uneven across use cases. This narrative review synthesizes recent literature most relevant to clinicians and investigators. Five themes dominate the current field: patient-facing adjunctive tools, failure modes and safety risks, clinician-facing decision support, passive sensing and measurement infrastructure, and governance. Recent randomized evidence supports a narrow efficacy claim for structured chatbot interventions, with small improvements in depressive and anxiety symptoms and more consistent effects on engagement than on symptom superiority. These studies do not support autonomous psychotherapy, and they do not establish a therapeutic advantage for open-ended large language model systems over more constrained designs. Safety studies, by contrast, identify active concerns: harmful endorsement, weak youth risk assessment, inconsistent crisis handling, and anxiety/OCD reassurance loops. The strongest current clinical signal lies in supervised clinician-facing decision support, where recent trials of AI-assisted antidepressant selection improved treatment persistence and some downstream symptom outcomes. Passive sensing detects behaviorally meaningful signals, but evidence that alert-driven deployment improves care remains insufficient for routine practice. Across stakeholder and policy sources, the most defensible deployment model is human-in-the-loop, stepped, and bounded by explicit handoff rules.","affiliation":"Laureate Institute for Brain Research, Tulsa, OK, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIGMS","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Depression","Phenotyping","Implementation","Anxiety","Governance","Chatbot"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xjmad.2026.100181"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13214549"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13214549?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-14"},{"id":"42156995","source":"MED","pmid":"42156995","pmcid":"PMC13187061","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13187061"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41746-026-02787-z","title":"Expert perspectives on US regulatory approaches to large language models in healthcare.","authorString":"Gilbert S, Dai T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Gilbert S","firstName":"Stephen","lastName":"Gilbert","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Else Kröner Fresenius Center for Digital Health, Faculty of Medicine, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany. stephen.gilbert@tu-dresden.de."},{"affiliation":"Faculty of Business and Economics, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany. stephen.gilbert@tu-dresden.de."}]}},{"fullName":"Dai T","firstName":"Tinglong","lastName":"Dai","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Carey Business School, Johns Hopkins University, Baltimore, MD, USA."},{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Johns Hopkins University, Baltimore, MD, USA."},{"affiliation":"Hopkins Business of Health Initiative, Johns Hopkins University, Washington, DC, USA."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4188236,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","issn":"2398-6352","essn":"2398-6352","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"382","affiliation":"Else Kröner Fresenius Center for Digital Health, Faculty of Medicine, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany. stephen.gilbert@tu-dresden.de.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-026-02787-z"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13187061"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13187061?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-20","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-21","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-19","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-19"},{"id":"42278712","source":"MED","pmid":"42278712","pmcid":"PMC13256130","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13256130"]},"doi":"10.3390/healthcare14111459","title":"Beyond Model Development in Healthcare AI: Post-Development Robustness, Post-Deployment Monitoring, and Lifecycle Governance-A Scoping Review of Reviews.","authorString":"El Arab RA, Mustafa MH, Almagharbeh WT, Saleem NH, Al Abdulmohsen S, Boathab R, Bu Washl M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"El Arab RA","firstName":"Rabie Adel","lastName":"El Arab","initials":"RA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3822-9236"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Almoosa College of Health Sciences, Al Ahsa 36422, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Mustafa MH","firstName":"Mohammad Hussein","lastName":"Mustafa","initials":"MH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-2937-4347"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Dr. Sulaiman Alhabib Medical Group, Riyadh 71491, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Almagharbeh WT","firstName":"Wesam Taher","lastName":"Almagharbeh","initials":"WT","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8435-1208"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical and Surgical Nursing Department, Faculty of Nursing, University of Tabuk, Tabuk 71491, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Saleem NH","firstName":"Noor Hafiz","lastName":"Saleem","initials":"NH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Nursing Department, Fatima College of Health Sciences, Abu Dhabi P.O. Box 3798, United Arab Emirates."}]}},{"fullName":"Al Abdulmohsen S","firstName":"Shahad","lastName":"Al Abdulmohsen","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Almoosa College of Health Sciences, Al Ahsa 36422, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Boathab R","firstName":"Ritaj","lastName":"Boathab","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Almoosa College of Health Sciences, Al Ahsa 36422, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Bu Washl M","firstName":"Mohammed","lastName":"Bu Washl","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Almoosa College of Health Sciences, Al Ahsa 36422, Saudi Arabia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3822-9236"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8435-1208"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-2937-4347"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4215131,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Healthcare (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","issn":"2227-9032","essn":"2227-9032","isoabbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","nlmid":"101666525"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1459","abstractText":"<b>Background:</b> Clinical artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly moving from retrospective model development into prospective evaluation, implementation, and routine care. Existing reviews have addressed specific aspects of this transition, including monitoring, drift, implementation, governance, and human-AI interaction; however, these bodies of work remain methodologically and conceptually fragmented across different review traditions. Methods: We conducted a scoping review of review-level and review-oriented literature. We searched MEDLINE, Embase, Scopus, and Web of Science Core Collection from database inception to 28 February 2026. We charted review characteristics and conducted an inductive thematic synthesis of extracted review-level findings, while distinguishing operational, deployment-proximal, methodological, and conceptual/governance-oriented evidence. <b>Results:</b> We included 25 review-level publications spanning systematic, scoping, methodological, narrative, and governance-oriented reviews. Three major themes emerged. First, clinically important risks were consistently framed as socio-technical rather than purely algorithmic: trustworthiness depended not only on technical performance, but also on fairness, transparency, workflow fit, human oversight, and organisational readiness. Second, the included review literature consistently recommended post-deployment monitoring but showed limited operational maturity; monitoring methods, action thresholds, fairness surveillance, and corrective responses were weakly standardised, and mature evidence from activated systems in routine care remained sparse. Third, trustworthy implementation was increasingly framed as a lifecycle governance challenge extending beyond procurement and initial validation to include local validation, subgroup auditing, drift detection, controlled updating, incident response, and, where necessary, rollback or retirement. <b>Discussion:</b> The review literature suggests a persistent normative-operational gap, meaning that recommendations about what trustworthy clinical AI should require have advanced faster than evidence on how monitoring, updating, and governance are implemented in routine care. The strongest unresolved challenge is therefore not principal generation alone, but the translation of monitoring and governance expectations into actionable operational systems. <b>Conclusions:</b> Post-development trustworthiness in clinical AI should be understood as a lifecycle property, not a one-time technical achievement. Future work should prioritise stronger operational evidence, clearer reporting of deployment-proximal and post-deployment evaluation, methodological standardisation of monitoring metrics and thresholds, implementation research on feasible governance models, and evaluation frameworks for assessing post-deployment safety, fairness, accountability, and sustainability.","affiliation":"Almoosa College of Health Sciences, Al Ahsa 36422, Saudi Arabia.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"1232","agency":"Almoosa Collegeof Health Sciences will cover APC","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Robustness","Trustworthiness","Model Updating","Dataset Shift","Human–Ai Interaction","Post-deployment Monitoring","Clinical Artificial Intelligence","Algorithmovigilance","Local Validation","Lifecycle Governance"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare14111459"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13256130"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13256130?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-12","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-25"},{"id":"42127229","source":"MED","pmid":"42127229","pmcid":"PMC13170740","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13170740"]},"doi":"10.2196/77591","title":"Leveraging Multimodal Large Language Models for Fall Risk Reduction in Older Adults in the Home: Proposed Model Design.","authorString":"Do J, Suresh V, Zhang L, Chavre BM, Cha J, Pugliese R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Do J","firstName":"Justin","lastName":"Do","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-4480-7636"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sidney Kimmel Medical College, Thomas Jefferson University, 925 Chestnut St., Basement Vault, Philadelphia, PA, 19107, United States, 1 4084718003, 1 2154841949."}]}},{"fullName":"Suresh V","firstName":"Vivaswat","lastName":"Suresh","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-4691-8567"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Health Design Lab, Thomas Jefferson University, Philadelphia, PA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang L","firstName":"Lily","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-3955-2697"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sidney Kimmel Medical College, Thomas Jefferson University, 925 Chestnut St., Basement Vault, Philadelphia, PA, 19107, United States, 1 4084718003, 1 2154841949."}]}},{"fullName":"Chavre BM","firstName":"Bharvi M","lastName":"Chavre","initials":"BM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1258-8417"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sidney Kimmel Medical College, Thomas Jefferson University, 925 Chestnut St., Basement Vault, Philadelphia, PA, 19107, United States, 1 4084718003, 1 2154841949."}]}},{"fullName":"Cha J","firstName":"Jeremy","lastName":"Cha","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8173-8263"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sidney Kimmel Medical College, Thomas Jefferson University, 925 Chestnut St., Basement Vault, Philadelphia, PA, 19107, United States, 1 4084718003, 1 2154841949."}]}},{"fullName":"Pugliese R","firstName":"Robert","lastName":"Pugliese","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6677-9243"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sidney Kimmel Medical College, Thomas Jefferson University, 925 Chestnut St., Basement Vault, Philadelphia, PA, 19107, United States, 1 4084718003, 1 2154841949."},{"affiliation":"Health Design Lab, Thomas Jefferson University, Philadelphia, PA, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1258-8417"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6677-9243"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8173-8263"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-3955-2697"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-4480-7636"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-4691-8567"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4190188,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR aging","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Aging","issn":"2561-7605","essn":"2561-7605","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Aging","nlmid":"101740387"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e77591","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>This research letter proposes a novel model design leveraging natively multimodal large language models to identify fall risks and generate visualizations of recommended home environmental modifications, aiming to improve the accessibility and impact of personalized fall prevention advice for older adults. Through a pilot rating study, this work demonstrates that multimodal large language models can generate safe and actionable advice to reduce fall risk in lived spaces of older adults, and also generate realistic edits based on original images. While this concept needs further testing and clinical comparison, it highlights a promising avenue for further innovation of fall prevention tactics.","affiliation":"Sidney Kimmel Medical College, Thomas Jefferson University, 925 Chestnut St., Basement Vault, Philadelphia, PA, 19107, United States, 1 4084718003, 1 2154841949.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["letter","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Risk Assessment","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pilot Projects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Risk Reduction Behavior"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Accidental Falls","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"PC","qualifierName":"prevention & control","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aged, 80 and over"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Fall Risk","Older Adults","Multimodal","Llm","Image Generation","Large Language Model"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/77591"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13170740"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13170740?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-15","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-13"},{"id":"41993038","source":"MED","pmid":"41993038","pmcid":"PMC13079118","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13079118"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1778786","title":"A review for navigating the trade-offs: evaluating open-source and proprietary large language models for clinical and biomedical information extraction.","authorString":"Sugihara Y, Milosavljevic A, Jankovskaja S, Falk M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sugihara Y","firstName":"Yutaka","lastName":"Sugihara","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Science, Faculty of Health and Society, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Biofilms-Research Center for Biointerfaces, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Milosavljevic A","firstName":"Aleksandar","lastName":"Milosavljevic","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Periodontology, Faculty of Odontology, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Jankovskaja S","firstName":"Skaidre","lastName":"Jankovskaja","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Science, Faculty of Health and Society, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Biofilms-Research Center for Biointerfaces, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Falk M","firstName":"Magnus","lastName":"Falk","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Science, Faculty of Health and Society, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Biofilms-Research Center for Biointerfaces, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","issn":"2673-253X","essn":"2673-253X","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1778786","abstractText":"The exponential growth of biomedical data necessitates advanced tools for efficient information extraction (IE) to support clinical decision-making and research. Large language models (LLMs) have emerged as transformative solutions, yet their application in healthcare raises critical trade-offs between open-source (OSS) and proprietary models. This review evaluates IE workflows such as named entity recognition, relation extraction, and terminology normalization, through five axes: performance (including schema fidelity), reproducibility, cost, transparency & auditability, and patient-centric governance. While proprietary models excel in schema compliance and complex reasoning, OSS models offer advantages in auditability, local control, and cost-effectiveness. Challenges such as schema fidelity, reproducibility, and ethical considerations like algorithmic fairness and data sovereignty are emphasized. The analysis highlights that OSS models, though requiring domain-specific adaptation, enable greater transparency and customization for privacy-sensitive tasks, whereas proprietary systems face limitations in bias mitigation and regulatory alignment. By addressing technical, ethical, and operational challenges, this work underscores the importance of context-aware model selection to balance innovation with accountability in clinical AI deployment. The findings advocate for hybrid approaches that integrate OSS flexibility with proprietary capabilities, ensuring equitable, reliable, and compliant healthcare solutions.","affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Science, Faculty of Health and Society, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Information Extraction","Healthcare Ai","Large Language Models","Proprietary Models","Open-source Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1778786"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13079118"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13079118?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-17","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-18","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-01"},{"id":"42318585","source":"MED","pmid":"42318585","pmcid":"PMC13272403","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13272403"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1818128","title":"Evidence-based AI: from trailblazer to trustblazer?","authorString":"Luechtefeld T, Hartung T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Luechtefeld T","firstName":"Thomas","lastName":"Luechtefeld","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Insilica Inc., Rockville, MD, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Hartung T","firstName":"Thomas","lastName":"Hartung","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Alternatives to Animal Testing (CAAT), Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health, Baltimore, MD, United States."},{"affiliation":"CAAT-Europe, University of Konstanz, Konstanz, Germany."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","issn":"2624-8212","essn":"2624-8212","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1818128","abstractText":"Agentic AI systems can plan, call tools, and coordinate specialized sub-agents, enabling multi-step scientific workflows that exceed what single-model text generation can reliably deliver. Yet in high-stakes domains such as regulatory science and toxicology, fluent outputs are not sufficient: adoption hinges on traceability, reproducibility, context-of-use validity, and explicit uncertainty communication. This perspective argues that evidence-based medicine and evidence-based toxicology provide a mature epistemic scaffold for making agentic AI trustworthy by design. We propose an Evidence-based Agent Stack that decomposes end-to-end tasks into protocolized roles (question framing, retrieval, screening, extraction, risk-of-bias appraisal, synthesis, mechanistic/causal integration, uncertainty assessment, and evidence-to-decision translation) with mandatory provenance and versioning. Anchoring agentic workflows in systematic review practice, risk-of-bias frameworks, and emerging regulatory principles (e.g., TREAT and e-validation) can turn \"<i>trailblazing</i>\" AI into \"<i>trustblazing</i>\" AI: systems whose outputs are auditable, updateable, and aligned with decision accountability.","affiliation":"Insilica Inc., Rockville, MD, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Systematic review","Regulatory Science","Evidence-based Toxicology","Risk Of Bias","Retrieval-augmented Generation","Agentic Ai","E-validation","Evidence-based Edicine"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1818128"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13272403"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13272403?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-19","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-20","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-03","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-03"},{"id":"42315134","source":"MED","pmid":"42315134","doi":"10.1093/jamia/ocag102","title":"Translation readiness of model-based synthetic tabular data in healthcare: a systematic review and governance audit.","authorString":"Castagno S, Subramanian A, Epanomeritakis IE, Gompels B, McDonnell S, Birch M, van der Schaar M, McCaskie A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Castagno S","firstName":"Simone","lastName":"Castagno","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3411-5880"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, University of Cambridge, Cambridge, CB2 0QQ, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Subramanian A","firstName":"Alagu","lastName":"Subramanian","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-7716-7801"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, University of Cambridge, Cambridge, CB2 0QQ, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Epanomeritakis IE","firstName":"Ilias E","lastName":"Epanomeritakis","initials":"IE","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4810-4197"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, University of Cambridge, Cambridge, CB2 0QQ, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Gompels B","firstName":"Benjamin","lastName":"Gompels","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, University of Cambridge, Cambridge, CB2 0QQ, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"McDonnell S","firstName":"Stephen","lastName":"McDonnell","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3181-4192"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, University of Cambridge, Cambridge, CB2 0QQ, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Birch M","firstName":"Mark","lastName":"Birch","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, University of Cambridge, Cambridge, CB2 0QQ, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"van der Schaar M","firstName":"Mihaela","lastName":"van der Schaar","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3933-6049"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics, University of Cambridge, Cambridge, CB3 0WA, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"McCaskie A","firstName":"Andrew","lastName":"McCaskie","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6476-0832"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, University of Cambridge, Cambridge, CB2 0QQ, United Kingdom."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6476-0832"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3181-4192"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3411-5880"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4810-4197"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3933-6049"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-7716-7801"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4204600,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association : JAMIA","medlineAbbreviation":"J Am Med Inform Assoc","issn":"1067-5027","essn":"1527-974X","isoabbreviation":"J Am Med Inform Assoc","nlmid":"9430800"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"ocag102","abstractText":"<h4>Objectives</h4>To evaluate the clinical applications and translation readiness of model-based synthetic tabular data in healthcare, and identify gaps in governance reporting that may hinder translation.<h4>Materials and methods</h4>We systematically searched Ovid MEDLINE and Embase (2010-August 2025; PROSPERO: CRD42025635514) for studies that generated and applied model-based synthetic tabular data in clinical contexts. Screening used a \"human-in-the-loop\" large language model workflow alongside independent manual review, achieving 100% sensitivity for included studies. Unlike prior reviews focused primarily on evaluation methodology, we mapped use-cases and deployment paradigms, and audited translation-readiness reporting using a predefined governance framework (validation depth, privacy, fairness, regulatory alignment).<h4>Results</h4>Thirty-seven studies (2019-2025) were included. GANs predominated; other approaches included VAEs, diffusion models, LLM-based synthesis, and Bayesian networks. Dataset augmentation was the primary application, often improving downstream model performance for rare outcomes. Emerging applications included synthetic control cohorts and algorithmic bias mitigation. Translation-readiness reporting was limited: 34/37 studies (92%) relied solely on internal validation, 9/37 (24%) used formal privacy models, 6/37 (16%) reported explicit fairness evaluations, and 6/37 (16%) addressed regulatory alignment. Few studies distinguished \"no-release\" from \"delayed-release\" paradigms.<h4>Discussion</h4>A systemic gap exists between methodological innovation and deployment-readiness reporting. Model-based synthetic data show clear value for augmentation and class balancing, but inconsistent reporting of validation, privacy, fairness, and regulatory considerations limits confidence in clinical deployment.<h4>Conclusion</h4>We propose TRUST-SD (Transparency and Reporting for Utility, Safety, and Translation of Synthetic Data), an author-derived, preliminary, evidence-informed reporting checklist spanning 7 domains, as a starting point for community refinement and consensus-building.","affiliation":"Department of Surgery, University of Cambridge, Cambridge, CB2 0QQ, United Kingdom.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Churchill Scholarship of the Winston Churchill Foundation of the United States","orderIn":0},{"agency":"University of Cambridge Harding","orderIn":0},{"agency":"GSK","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Onassis Foundation Scholarship","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"G21156","agency":"Versus Arthritis","acronym":"VAC_","orderIn":0},{"agency":"AstraZeneca","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Cambridge Center for AI in Medicine","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"G124606","agency":"Louis and Valerie Freedman Studentship in Medical Sciences from Trinity College Cambridge and the ORUK/Versus Arthritis: AI in MSK Research Fellowship","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"NIHR203312","agency":"NIHR Cambridge Biomedical Research Center","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Systematic review","Privacy","Synthetic Data","Generative Ai","Governance And Translation Readiness"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/jamia/ocag102"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-18","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-18","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-18","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-18"},{"id":"42383194","source":"MED","pmid":"42383194","pmcid":"PMC13316258","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13316258"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.esmorw.2026.100723","title":"Large language models in oncology: promise, pitfalls, and the path to real-world adoption.","authorString":"McInerney S, Hall PS, Cresswell K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"McInerney S","firstName":"S","lastName":"McInerney","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-7543-7450"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Genetics and Cancer, University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Hall PS","firstName":"P S","lastName":"Hall","initials":"PS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Genetics and Cancer, University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Cresswell K","firstName":"K","lastName":"Cresswell","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6634-9537"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Usher Institute, University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh, UK."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6634-9537"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-7543-7450"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"13","journalIssueId":4219706,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","journal":{"title":"ESMO real world data and digital oncology","medlineAbbreviation":"ESMO Real World Data Digit Oncol","issn":"2949-8201","essn":"2949-8201","isoabbreviation":"ESMO Real World Data Digit Oncol","nlmid":"9919053637506676"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"100723","affiliation":"Institute of Genetics and Cancer, University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh, UK.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Editorial"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.esmorw.2026.100723"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13316258"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13316258?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-22"},{"id":"42130876","source":"MED","pmid":"42130876","pmcid":"PMC13160892","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13160892"]},"doi":"10.3389/fpubh.2026.1792627","title":"Ethical issues in multi-agent AI systems for healthcare: a narrative review.","authorString":"Xie Z, Wang H, Dai L, Wang Z, Song H, Qian J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Xie Z","firstName":"Zhibin","lastName":"Xie","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"KoGuan School of Law, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang H","firstName":"Hongyu","lastName":"Wang","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Dai L","firstName":"Lexuan","lastName":"Dai","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Southern California, Los Angeles, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang Z","firstName":"Zikai","lastName":"Wang","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Shanghai Artificial Intelligence Research Institute, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Song H","firstName":"Haitao","lastName":"Song","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Shanghai Artificial Intelligence Research Institute, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Qian J","firstName":"Jingzhe","lastName":"Qian","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-8490-9413"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Shanghai Artificial Intelligence Research Institute, Shanghai, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-8490-9413"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4110762,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in public health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Public Health","issn":"2296-2565","essn":"2296-2565","isoabbreviation":"Front Public Health","nlmid":"101616579"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1792627","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Multi-agent AI systems are believed to bring significant improvements in digital health, but it also brings new and more serious ethical issues. Such systems distribute the decision-making process among multiple interacting agents, and this decentralized decision-making system has raised ethical concerns in the medical field. On the one hand, it continues the ethical issues of traditional AI tools; on the other hand, the interaction processes within complex systems have also brought about new dilemmas. This narrative review aims to synthesize the ethical issues related to multi-agent AI systems in healthcare presented and explore the corresponding mitigation strategies.<h4>Methods</h4>The study outcomes were synthesized using a narrative approach. Relevant records were gathered through Boolean searches in databases such as PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science. A total of 21 articles related to multi-agent AI, healthcare, and ethical issues are included in this review.<h4>Results</h4>Seven key ethical challenges were identified: (1) compound opacity, where interacting AI agents create layers of inscrutable decision-making; (2) error propagation and attribution difficulties, complicating accountability for clinical harm; (3) increased clinician dependence and automation bias, leading to potential deskilling and overreliance; (4) erosion of human oversight, as multi-agent AI systems operate beyond effective human control; (5) privacy and data security risks, stemming from complex data flows among agents; (6) threats to patient autonomy and informed consent, due to opaque or paternalistic AI recommendations; and (7) contextual blindness, reflecting a loss of individualized patient understanding in modular AI workflows. Furthermore, this review also summarized solutions proposed in the existing literature for these ethical issues.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Multi-agent AI systems intensify existing ethical concerns in healthcare by distributing decision-making and blurring responsibility. To mitigate these issues, recent research advocates for the development of adaptive governance models, clear accountability frameworks, human-AI collaboration structures that preserve clinician authority, enhanced systems for explainability, and privacy-centered designs. In order to successfully incorporate agentic AI into healthcare, it is essential to maintain transparency, protect patient rights, and ensure that human-centered values continue to guide clinical decision-making in an era dominated by autonomous, interacting AI systems.","affiliation":"KoGuan School of Law, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ES","qualifierName":"ethics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Delivery of Health Care","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ES","qualifierName":"ethics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ES","qualifierName":"ethics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Healthcare Ethics","Digital Health","Autonomous Decision-Making","Agentic Ai Systems","Responsibility And Accountability"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2026.1792627"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13160892"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13160892?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-15","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-28","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-28"},{"id":"42183521","source":"MED","pmid":"42183521","pmcid":"PMC13195682","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13195682"]},"doi":"10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1690291","title":"Artificial intelligence as a digital analytic third: the SADAR framework for reflective supervision in psychotherapy.","authorString":"Signorini S, Paganin W.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Signorini S","firstName":"Sabrina","lastName":"Signorini","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-6535-0474"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Studio Psicologia Signorini, Guidonia Montecelio, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Paganin W","firstName":"Walter","lastName":"Paganin","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9007-0712"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Studio Psicologia Signorini, Guidonia Montecelio, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Università di Roma Tor Vergata, Rome, Italy."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9007-0712"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-6535-0474"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"17","journalIssueId":4112159,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in psychology","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Psychol","issn":"1664-1078","essn":"1664-1078","isoabbreviation":"Front Psychol","nlmid":"101550902"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1690291","abstractText":"The SADAR (Sistema Autoesplorativo Dialogogico Autentico Relazionale-Authentic Relational Dialogogic Self-Exploratory System) positions AI as a Digital Analytic Third for therapists' post-session reflection and clinical supervision, rather than for direct patient interventions or automated clinical decision-making. The SADAR frames AI as a dialogogic symbolic co-presence that expands the therapist's reflective space and deepens countertransferential awareness, without replacing professional judgment or human supervision. This perspective article defines the term \"dialogogic,\" presents a three-step reflective process (positioning, dialogogic prompting, and critical integration), and illustrates the framework's potential and limitations through two clinical vignettes. The operational implementation is detailed in a companion brief report. All outputs must adhere to reflective supervision protocols and should never replace direct patient data.","affiliation":"Studio Psicologia Signorini, Guidonia Montecelio, Italy.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Countertransference","psychotherapy","Reflective Supervision","Sadar","Digital Analytic Third","Internal Multiplicity","Psychodynamic Ai"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1690291"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13195682"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13195682?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-25","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-08","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-08"},{"id":"42130945","source":"MED","pmid":"42130945","pmcid":"PMC13161683","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13161683"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.patter.2026.101534","title":"Pitfalls and risks of generative AI in machine learning.","authorString":"Lones MA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lones MA","firstName":"Michael A","lastName":"Lones","initials":"MA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2745-9896"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Mathematical and Computer Sciences, Heriot-Watt University, Edinburgh, UK."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2745-9896"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"7","journalIssueId":4193806,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Patterns (New York, N.Y.)","medlineAbbreviation":"Patterns (N Y)","issn":"2666-3899","essn":"2666-3899","isoabbreviation":"Patterns (N Y)","nlmid":"101767765"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"101534","abstractText":"Modern generative AI approaches-including large language models and foundation models more generally-are increasingly part of machine learning (ML) workflows. This brings new opportunities but also new ways in which things can go wrong, including challenges around evaluation, security, data provenance, technical debt, regulatory compliance, and hidden financial costs. This tutorial gives a concise overview of emerging pitfalls and risks and offers guidance on how to navigate them, with the aim of helping readers make informed decisions and avoid costly mistakes. Written in plain language and assuming no deep prior knowledge, it explores four key ways in which generative AI is currently applied in ML workflows: as a component of ML pipelines, as a designer of ML pipelines, as a synthesizer of data, and as an analyst.","affiliation":"School of Mathematical and Computer Sciences, Heriot-Watt University, Edinburgh, UK.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["practice","Machine Learning","Guidance","Generative Ai","Foundation Models","Llms"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patter.2026.101534"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13161683"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13161683?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-15","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-22"},{"id":"PPR1252114","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-10012178/v1","title":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), Generative AI, and Agentic AI Governance: An Integrated Enterprise Governance Prioritization Architecture","authorString":"Rah A, Hahues S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Rah A","firstName":"Audrey","lastName":"Rah","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Hahues S","firstName":"Sven","lastName":"Hahues","initials":"S"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7501-7809"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>Enterprise adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) systems, including Generative AI (GenAI), Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), and agentic AI, is advancing faster than many organizations can adapt their governance, audit, cybersecurity, and executive oversight practices. This paper develops a proposed integrated AI governance prioritization architecture using a design-science research approach, supported by exploratory platform-risk analysis, a reproducible platform-risk dataset, a Monte Carlo robustness assessment, a framework coverage matrix, a Governance Priority Score (GPS)-based demonstration, and a public-document relevance assessment. The primary contribution is an integrated governance decision-support architecture that uses the Enterprise AI Platform Taxonomy (EAPT) to estimate platform burden, the Enterprise AI Risk Taxonomy (EART) to organize governance-risk exposure, the Enterprise AI Governance Maturity Model (EAGM) to represent organizational readiness, and the Integrated Agentic AI Governance Framework (IAGF) to identify agentic AI oversight requirements within a unified workflow. These components are operationally connected through GPS, which links platform burden, governancerisk gaps, organizational readiness, agentic runtime-control needs, and executive decision support. The proposed architecture draws upon NIST AI RMF, NIST AI 600-1, ISO/IEC 42001, COBIT, OWASP LLM Top 10, MITRE ATLAS, the EU AI Act, and related AI governance literature. A rubric-based analytical dataset covering 19 enterprise AI platforms evaluates each platform across eight subdimensions, with final platform-risk scores computed as the arithmetic mean of data access scope, operational autonomy, tool/API integration breadth, external connectivity, governance burden, cybersecurity exposure, adoption and shadow-AI risk, and supply-chain integrity. TThe expanded scoring method produces high rank stability relative to the original final-score dataset, with Spearman’s ρ = 0.996, and remains directionally stable under coding-uncertainty stress testing; these results are interpreted only as internal coding stability and robustness, not external validation. Public evidence from U.S. government agencies, universities, healthcare systems, and financial institutions is used to assess architecture relevance and observability, not to claim organizational effectiveness. The paper contributes a bounded design-science foundation for future inter-rater reliability testing, Delphi expert validation, organizational pilots, psychometric evaluation, and dashboard usability assessment.</p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-10012178/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1252114","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-06-15","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-15"},{"id":"41606555","source":"MED","pmid":"41606555","pmcid":"PMC12924558","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12924558"]},"doi":"10.1186/s12889-025-26148-9","title":"Generative AI and health misinformation: production, propagation, and mitigation-a systematic review.","authorString":"Saeidnia HR, Jahani S, Ghiasi N, Keshavarz H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Saeidnia HR","firstName":"Hamid Reza","lastName":"Saeidnia","initials":"HR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Knowledge and Information Science, Tarbiat Modares University, (TMU), Tehran, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Jahani S","firstName":"Shamim","lastName":"Jahani","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Research Committee, Babol University of Medical Sciences, Babol, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Ghiasi N","firstName":"Nasrin","lastName":"Ghiasi","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Public Health, School of Health, Ilam University of Medical Sciences, Ilam, Iran. ghnazi93@gmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Keshavarz H","firstName":"Hamid","lastName":"Keshavarz","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Knowledge and Information Science, Shahid Beheshti University, Tehran, Iran."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"26","journalIssueId":4082921,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"BMC public health","medlineAbbreviation":"BMC Public Health","issn":"1471-2458","essn":"1471-2458","isoabbreviation":"BMC Public Health","nlmid":"100968562"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"693","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Generative artificial intelligence technologies have disrupted information ecosystems, posing new threats to public health by enabling rapid, scalable manufacture of convincing but false health stories. This systematic review synthesizes evidence on how generative AI reconfigures health misinformation creation, dissemination, and moderation.<h4>Methods</h4>In line with PRISMA 2020, 15 empirical studies published between January 2023 and August 2025 were included. Databases consulted were MEDLINE (via PubMed), Embase, Scopus, Web of Science Core Collection, ACM Digital Library, IEEE Xplore, PsycINFO, Communication & Mass Media Complete, arXiv, and medRxiv/SSRN. Studies were contrasted on the basis of production capacity, propagation dynamics, and efficacy of mitigation at technical, sociotechnical, and governance layers.<h4>Results</h4>The synthesis indicates that generative AI substantially increases the volume, speed, and perceived credibility of health disinformation production, while altering its propagation dynamics. Users often struggle to distinguish AI‑generated from human‑authored health misinformation, and their sharing intentions are not tightly coupled with perceived accuracy. Existing detection systems show limited performance against AI‑generated content, and while labeling interventions can reduce perceived accuracy, their effects are context‑dependent.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Generative AI transforms the health misinformation landscape by lowering barriers to creation and exploiting platform and behavioral dynamics. Current mitigation strategies-spanning technical, sociotechnical, and governance layers-are promising but remain nascent and unevenly evaluated. Future work must prioritize multimodal, multilingual, and health‑specific verification, as well as real‑world testing of interventions, to build equitable and resilient health information ecosystems.","affiliation":"Department of Knowledge and Information Science, Tarbiat Modares University, (TMU), Tehran, Iran.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Communication"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Information Dissemination","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Health Communication"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Systematic review","Mitigation Strategies","Health Misinformation","Deepfakes","Generative Ai"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12889-025-26148-9"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12924558"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12924558?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-06","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-29"},{"id":"42368305","source":"MED","pmid":"42368305","pmcid":"PMC13306874","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13306874"]},"doi":"10.35772/ghm.2026.01063","title":"From companion technologies to social care infrastructure: A multi-level perspective on loneliness-related support in dementia care in an era of artificial intelligence (AI).","authorString":"Uenishi M, Song P.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Uenishi M","firstName":"Machiko","lastName":"Uenishi","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-5480-6962"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Global Health & Medicine, Bureau of Health Emergency and Management, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Song P","firstName":"Peipei","lastName":"Song","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Global Health & Medicine, Bureau of Health Emergency and Management, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."},{"affiliation":"National College of Nursing, Japan (NCNJ), Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-5480-6962"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"8","journalIssueId":4222530,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Global health & medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"Glob Health Med","issn":"2434-9186","essn":"2434-9194","isoabbreviation":"Glob Health Med","nlmid":"101771579"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"215-221","abstractText":"Loneliness and social isolation are important psychosocial concerns in dementia care, but they are difficult to address through pharmacological treatment or episodic social activities alone. Companion technologies, including socially assistive robots, humanoid and conversational robots, avatars, virtual agents, and artificial intelligence (AI)- enabled companions, are increasingly discussed as possible aids for engagement and interaction. However, current evidence does not justify treating these technologies as standalone interventions that directly reduce loneliness in people with dementia. We argue that these technologies may be more appropriately considered not only as devices or interventions, but also as potential components of social care infrastructure: tools that can encourage individual engagement and mediate human relationships but that need to be integrated into care ecosystems. We propose the multi-level care with social technologies (MCST) model, which consists of three connected layers: individual engagement, relational interaction, and the care ecosystem. The model emphasizes that loneliness-related support is produced through the interaction between technology, human facilitation, care workflows, ethical governance, and feedback-based adjustment. This approach is especially relevant as dementia care systems face workforce constraints and an increasing need for home-based care, while psychosocial needs remain and may be overlooked in routine care. AI-enabled and large-language-model-based companions may expand possibilities for personalization and continuous engagement, but dementia-specific evidence remains preliminary and safety concerns are substantial. Future research should validate the MCST model in real-world dementia care and establish evaluation frameworks that address psychosocial outcomes, sustained use, safety, privacy, human oversight, and accountability.","affiliation":"Division of Global Health & Medicine, Bureau of Health Emergency and Management, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Letter"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Perspective","Dementia","Artificial Intelligence (Ai)","Companion Technologies","Loneliness-related Distress","Social Care Infrastructure"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.35772/ghm.2026.01063"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13306874"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13306874?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.35772/ghm.2026.01063"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},{"id":"42369004","source":"MED","pmid":"42369004","pmcid":"PMC13294403","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13294403"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1814651","title":"Agentic AI systems in electrical power systems engineering: current state-of-the-art and challenges.","authorString":"Ghosh S, Mittal G.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ghosh S","firstName":"Soham","lastName":"Ghosh","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Independent Researcher, Overland Park, Kansas, KS, United States."},{"affiliation":"IEEE IAS/PES Kansas City Section, Kansas City, KS, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Mittal G","firstName":"Gaurav","lastName":"Mittal","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Independent Researcher, Overland Park, Kansas, KS, United States."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","issn":"2624-8212","essn":"2624-8212","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1814651","abstractText":"Agentic AI systems have recently emerged as a critical and transformative approach in artificial intelligence, offering capabilities that extend far beyond traditional AI agents and contemporary generative AI models. This rapid evolution necessitates a clear conceptual and taxonomical understanding to differentiate this new paradigm. Our paper addresses this gap by providing a comprehensive review that establishes a precise definition and taxonomy for \"agentic AI,\" with the aim of distinguishing it from previous AI paradigms. The concepts are gradually introduced, starting with a highlight of its diverse applications across the broader field of engineering. The paper then presents four detailed, state-of-the-art use-case applications within electrical power systems engineering, a domain where the impact of agentic AI systems is expected to be particularly significant. The high impact of agentic AI systems in the field of electrical power systems is primarily driven by global trends toward clean energy transition and higher levels of grid automations, all of which create an environment where agentic AI can be readily deployed and effectively leveraged. These case studies demonstrate current and innovative state-of-the-art, ranging from an advanced agentic framework for streamlining complex power system studies and benchmarking to a novel agentic AI system developed for survival analysis of dynamic pricing strategies in battery swapping stations. Finally, robust deployment of these autonomous agents brings a unique set of challenges that are discussed in this manuscript through detailed failure mode investigations. From these findings, we derive actionable recommendations for the design and implementation of safe, reliable, and accountable agentic AI systems, offering a critical resource for researchers and practitioners.","affiliation":"Independent Researcher, Overland Park, Kansas, KS, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Survival analysis","Autonomous Agents","Large Language Model (Llm)","Zero Trust Architecture","Agentic Ai","Model Context Protocol (Mcp)","Agentic Collusion","Power System Automation"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1814651"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13294403"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13294403?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-11"},{"id":"42061104","source":"MED","pmid":"42061104","pmcid":"PMC13140055","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13140055"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.identj.2026.109608","title":"From Framework Inventory to Reporting Architecture: Next Steps for Dental AI Reporting Standards.","authorString":"Mourão CF, Juliasse LE.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mourão CF","firstName":"Carlos Fernando","lastName":"Mourão","initials":"CF","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Basic and Clinical Translational Sciences, Tufts University School of Dental Medicine, Boston, Massachusetts, USA. Electronic address: carlos.mourao@tufts.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Juliasse LE","firstName":"Luiz Eduardo","lastName":"Juliasse","initials":"LE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Basic and Clinical Translational Sciences, Tufts University School of Dental Medicine, Boston, Massachusetts, USA."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"76","journalIssueId":4227336,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01","journal":{"title":"International dental journal","medlineAbbreviation":"Int Dent J","issn":"0020-6539","essn":"1875-595X","isoabbreviation":"Int Dent J","nlmid":"0374714"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"109608","affiliation":"Department of Basic and Clinical Translational Sciences, Tufts University School of Dental Medicine, Boston, Massachusetts, USA. Electronic address: carlos.mourao@tufts.edu.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Letter"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.identj.2026.109608"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13140055"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13140055?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-30","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-29"},{"id":"PPR1244964","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-9890887/v1","title":"Enterprise Generative AI: A Systematic Review of Security Risks and Governance","authorString":"Rah A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Rah A","firstName":"Audrey","lastName":"Rah","initials":"A"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7501-7809"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>The unprecedented acceleration of generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) and agentic ecosystems within United States organizations has outpaced established corporate risk management frameworks, creating a critical vulnerability vector. This study presents a systematic literature review analyzing the multi-tiered landscape of enterprise AI platform taxonomy, adoption dynamics, and cybersecurity risk classification. Evaluating an extensive body of retrieved records under a rigorous, multi-framework protocol compliant with PRISMA, Kitchenham, and Webster Watson standards, high-quality primary sources spanning peer-reviewed literature, international standards, and authoritative industry disclosures were synthesized. The review introduces five author-derived synthesis instruments: the Composite Adoption Score (CAS), the Enterprise AI Risk Classification Framework (EARCF), the Enterprise AI Platform Taxonomy (EAPT), the Enterprise AI Governance Maturity Model (EAGM), and the Enterprise AI Governance Lifecycle (EAGL). CAS and EARCF are proposed as comparative synthesis frameworks intended to support evidence classification and structured analysis rather than predictive assessment. Our findings show that generative AI adoption expanded rapidly across commercial segments, driven primarily by Tier-1 productivity suites and enterprise infrastructure APIs. Concurrently, the emergence of agentic architectures characterized by multi-step reasoning and autonomous tool-use capabilities has introduced acute vulnerabilities, including prompt injection, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) corpus poisoning, and excessive agency. Threat modeling demonstrates that while traditional security controls mitigate baseline boundary failures, they are less effective against autonomous state manipulation and memory-auditing lapses. Finally, by cross-mapping the coverage of dominant governance standards, including NIST AI RMF, ISO/IEC 42001, OWASP LLM Top 10, and MITRE ATLAS, this review identifies significant gaps in multi-agent accountability protocols and memory-state auditing. The paper concludes by presenting a governance maturity roadmap designed to support the transition from ad hoc mitigation practices to auditable, zero-trust AI architectures.</p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9890887/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1244964","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-06-03","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-04","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-03"},{"id":"41878375","source":"MED","pmid":"41878375","pmcid":"PMC13006881","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13006881"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1807664","title":"Editorial: Ethical considerations of large language models: challenges and best practices.","authorString":"Velmovitsky PE, Arbuckle L, Papadopoulou P.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Velmovitsky PE","firstName":"Pedro Elkind","lastName":"Velmovitsky","initials":"PE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Digital Therapeutics, University Health Network (UHN), Toronto, ON, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Institute of Health Policy, Management and Evaluation, University of Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Arbuckle L","firstName":"Luk","lastName":"Arbuckle","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6828-2129"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"IQVIA Applied AI Science, Ottawa, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Papadopoulou P","firstName":"Paraskevi","lastName":"Papadopoulou","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8194-7495"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"American College of Greece, Athens, Greece."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6828-2129"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8194-7495"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","issn":"2673-253X","essn":"2673-253X","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1807664","affiliation":"Centre for Digital Therapeutics, University Health Network (UHN), Toronto, ON, Canada.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Editorial"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Ethics","Bias","Access","Equity","Governance","Large Langauge Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1807664"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13006881"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13006881?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-25","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-09"},{"id":"42181403","source":"MED","pmid":"42181403","pmcid":"PMC13194745","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13194745"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.107501","title":"Machine Learning-Based Data Extraction Tools in Healthcare: A Systematic Review.","authorString":"Khalpey Z, Rorvig M, Khalpey ZI, Kumar U, Khaliel FH, King N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Khalpey Z","firstName":"Zain","lastName":"Khalpey","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cardiothoracic Surgery, HonorHealth, Scottsdale, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Rorvig M","firstName":"Matthew","lastName":"Rorvig","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Research, Honor Health, Scottsdale, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Khalpey ZI","firstName":"Zacharya I","lastName":"Khalpey","initials":"ZI","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Surgery, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Kumar U","firstName":"Ujjawal","lastName":"Kumar","initials":"U","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cardiothoracic Surgery, HonorHealth, Scottsdale, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Khaliel FH","firstName":"Feras H","lastName":"Khaliel","initials":"FH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cardiac Surgery, King Faisal Specialist Hospital and Research Centre, Riyadh, SAU."},{"affiliation":"Cardiac Surgery, Heart Center, King Faisal Specialist Hospital and Research Centre, Riyadh, SAU."}]}},{"fullName":"King N","firstName":"Nicholas","lastName":"King","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cardiology, HonorHealth, Austin, USA."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4172604,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","issn":"2168-8184","essn":"2168-8184","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e107501","abstractText":"The healthcare industry's digital transformation has led to an unprecedented volume of multimodal data. Machine learning (ML)-based extraction tools offer promising solutions for managing this data explosion, particularly when integrated with federated database systems. If a large language model (LLM) is trained to extract data from this multimodal information and ensure high accuracy while remaining affordable, the potential to improve the data extraction process within the medical field would be limitless, reducing costs and manpower across the board. A systematic review was conducted following Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) guidelines, searching major databases for studies published between 2018 and 2024, supplemented by grey literature sources. Analysis focused on the performance and implementation costs of ML-based extraction tools in healthcare settings. From 1,247 initial records, 21 studies met the inclusion criteria. ML-based extraction demonstrated superior accuracy, ranging from 61% to 98%, compared to traditional methods. Implementation costs averaged between $500,000 and $2.5 million. Two primary categories of tools emerged: image-based and text-oriented. ML-based extraction tools show significant promise in healthcare data management, though successful implementation requires careful consideration of costs, security protocols, and regulatory compliance. The development of a dedicated LLM capable of efficiently extracting data from various medical sources could revolutionize healthcare by streamlining data management and reallocating resources toward patient care and research advancements.","affiliation":"Cardiothoracic Surgery, HonorHealth, Scottsdale, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Systematic review","Medical Informatics","Machine Learning","Data Extraction","Federated Database","Healthcare Technology","Medical Data Management","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.107501"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13194745"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13194745?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-25","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-21"},{"id":"41970523","source":"MED","pmid":"41970523","pmcid":"PMC13062252","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13062252"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1761641","title":"Large language models in healthcare quality management: a European perspective on process automation and compliance.","authorString":"Knott M, Krebs M, Kerscher A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Knott M","firstName":"Markus","lastName":"Knott","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9147-3203"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Klinikum Stuttgart, Stuttgart Cancer Center - Tumorzentrum Eva Mayr-Stihl, Stuttgart, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Krebs M","firstName":"Markus","lastName":"Krebs","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Comprehensive Cancer Center Augsburg, Medical Faculty, University of Augsburg, Augsburg, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Bavarian Cancer Research Center (BZKF), Erlangen, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Kerscher A","firstName":"Alexander","lastName":"Kerscher","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Bavarian Cancer Research Center (BZKF), Erlangen, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Department of Gynecology and Obstetrics, Erlangen University Hospital, Comprehensive Cancer Center Erlangen-European Metropolitan Area Nuremberg (CCC ER-EMN), Friedrich Alexander University of Erlangen-Nürnberg, Erlangen, Germany."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9147-3203"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","issn":"2673-253X","essn":"2673-253X","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1761641","abstractText":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are transforming back-office quality management processes in European healthcare systems through automation of compliance monitoring, quality assurance, and process optimization without direct patient interaction. This narrative review synthesizes evidence from recent systematic reviews and implementation studies (2023-2025) examining LLM deployment within the European regulatory framework encompassing the Medical Device Regulation (MDR), General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), and the EU Artificial Intelligence Act (Regulation EU 2024/1689). Current research demonstrates meaningful efficiency gains: individual studies of AI-assisted documentation tools report improvements ranging from modest increases in documentation speed to reductions in processing time approaching 50%, while broader policy analyses estimate administrative workload reductions of up to 30% through digital health and AI solutions. Clinical trial applications show particular maturity, with LLM-generated informed consent forms demonstrating improved readability (76% vs. 67%) without compromising accuracy. However, critical gaps persist between research achievements and practical deployment. Analysis of 519 evaluation studies reveals that only 5% utilized real patient care data, while 95% focused exclusively on accuracy metrics to the neglect of fairness (16%), deployment readiness (5%), and calibration (1%). No LLM-based quality management system has yet received regulatory clearance, and implementation science frameworks remain underdeveloped. We propose a risk-stratified implementation framework emphasizing process-oriented applications-standard operating procedure automation, audit documentation, deviation management, and compliance monitoring-that avoid medical device classification while capturing substantial operational benefits. Advanced methodological approaches including retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) architectures, digital twin integration, and natural language processing-based pattern recognition offer pathways toward comprehensive quality intelligence platforms. The convergence of LLMs with emerging technologies such as knowledge graphs, digital twin architectures and multimodal analysis creates opportunities for predictive quality management that anticipates rather than merely documents quality-relevant events. Evidence supports deployment in administrative quality processes, with particular potential for applications that redirect human expertise from documentation toward quality improvement activities, though current evidence derives predominantly from non-European healthcare contexts and simulated or limited-scope settings. Success requires adapted validation methodologies addressing LLM non-determinism, robust governance structures, and comprehensive change management that maintains the high standards European healthcare systems demand.","affiliation":"Klinikum Stuttgart, Stuttgart Cancer Center - Tumorzentrum Eva Mayr-Stihl, Stuttgart, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Regulatory Compliance","Digital Health","Medical Device Regulation","Healthcare Quality Management","Process Automation","Eu Ai Act","Large Language Models","Retrieval-augmented Generation"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1761641"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13062252"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13062252?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["nct","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-13","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-26"},{"id":"41952677","source":"MED","pmid":"41952677","pmcid":"PMC13053261","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13053261"]},"doi":"10.3389/fonc.2026.1795441","title":"Large language models in patient education for brain tumors: opportunities, risks, and ethical considerations.","authorString":"Christodoulou RC, Papageorgiou PS, Lucio Pereira AC, Solomou EE, Papageorgiou SG, Vassiliou E, Georgiou MF.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Christodoulou RC","firstName":"Rafail C","lastName":"Christodoulou","initials":"RC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Neuroimaging and Neurointervention, Department of Radiology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Papageorgiou PS","firstName":"Platon S","lastName":"Papageorgiou","initials":"PS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Medical School, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Lucio Pereira AC","firstName":"Ana Carolina","lastName":"Lucio Pereira","initials":"AC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Oncological Research Institute (IPON)/Discipline of Gynecology and Obstetrics, Federal University of Triângulo Mineiro, Uberaba, Brazil."}]}},{"fullName":"Solomou EE","firstName":"Elena E","lastName":"Solomou","initials":"EE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine-Hematology, University of Patras Medical School, Rion, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Papageorgiou SG","firstName":"Sokratis G","lastName":"Papageorgiou","initials":"SG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"1st Department of Neurology, Medical School, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, Eginition Hospital, Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Vassiliou E","firstName":"Evros","lastName":"Vassiliou","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Kean University, Union, NJ, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Georgiou MF","firstName":"Michalis F","lastName":"Georgiou","initials":"MF","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, University of Miami, Miami, FL, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-6102-3344"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"16","journalIssueId":4110619,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in oncology","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Oncol","issn":"2234-943X","essn":"2234-943X","isoabbreviation":"Front Oncol","nlmid":"101568867"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1795441","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Patients with brain tumors often struggle to understand their condition because of complex imaging findings, multidisciplinary care pathways, and frequent cognitive and emotional vulnerability. Effective patient education is, therefore, essential but difficult to deliver within routine clinical encounters.<h4>Objective</h4>This narrative review evaluates the role of large language models (LLMs) in supporting patient education for individuals with brain tumors.<h4>Content</h4>We synthesize evidence from neuro-oncology, radiology, and digital health literature on the use of LLMs to explain imaging results, diagnoses, and treatment options in patient-centered language. Potential benefits include improved health literacy, accessibility, and continuity of education. Key limitations are also discussed, including hallucinations, output variability, overtrust, data privacy concerns, and ethical challenges. A clinician-guided framework for responsible integration is proposed.<h4>Conclusion</h4>When used under clinician supervision as educational support tools, LLMs may enhance patient understanding and engagement in brain tumor care. Safe implementation will require structured governance, oversight, and alignment with ethical standards.","affiliation":"Division of Neuroimaging and Neurointervention, Department of Radiology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Brain tumors","Patient education","Neuro-oncology","Artificial Intelligence In Healthcare","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fonc.2026.1795441"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13053261"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13053261?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-09","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-24"},{"id":"42354574","source":"MED","pmid":"42354574","pmcid":"PMC13300344","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13300344"]},"doi":"10.3390/healthcare14121716","title":"Explainability and Human Oversight for AI-Generated Exercise Guidance in Digital Healthcare: A Governance-Oriented Narrative Review.","authorString":"Pan K, Huang C, Lin X, Huang S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Pan K","firstName":"Kaijiang","lastName":"Pan","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-0108-1035"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Marxism, Xiamen Ocean Vocational College, Xiamen 361100, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Huang C","firstName":"Caihua","lastName":"Huang","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5134-0169"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Research and Communication Center for Exercise and Health, Xiamen University of Technology, Xiamen 361024, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lin X","firstName":"Xinyu","lastName":"Lin","initials":"X","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-8182-2431"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Film and Communication, Xiamen University of Technology, Xiamen 361024, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Huang S","firstName":"Shengqi","lastName":"Huang","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-0522-478X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Research and Communication Center for Exercise and Health, Xiamen University of Technology, Xiamen 361024, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5134-0169"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-0108-1035"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-0522-478X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-8182-2431"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"12","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4221244,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Healthcare (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","issn":"2227-9032","essn":"2227-9032","isoabbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","nlmid":"101666525"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1716","abstractText":"<b>Background:</b> Large language models and other generative artificial intelligence (AI) tools are increasingly being embedded in digital healthcare services, including mobile health applications, telerehabilitation, remote monitoring, and hybrid care pathways. In this review, digital healthcare refers to technology-mediated healthcare services in which digital platforms, mobile applications, wearables, remote communication, and AI-enabled interfaces support health assessment, self-management, rehabilitation, clinical decision support, or service delivery. When AI-generated exercise guidance moves from general education to individualized recommendations about dose, progression, contraindications, or rehabilitation, it may become directly actionable and safety-relevant. <b>Objectives</b>: This review aimed to clarify when AI-generated exercise guidance in digital healthcare may warrant safety-relevant governance attention and to outline implementation considerations for explainability, human oversight, and service-level governance. It addresses a gap in the literature: general AI-governance and exercise-prescription discussions rarely specify how point-of-use explanations, review thresholds, and escalation safeguards can be organized for directly actionable AI exercise guidance. <b>Methods</b>: We conducted a governance-oriented narrative review of peer-reviewed literature and representative regulatory or guidance documents. This review was not designed as a systematic review, scoping review, or exhaustive evidence map; transparent source mapping was used to support conceptual synthesis. Searches and source mapping focused on generative AI, large language models, explainable AI, clinical decision support, digital health, mobile health, exercise prescription, rehabilitation, trust, automation bias, and human oversight. Sources were included when they informed the safety, explainability, governance, or real-world implementation of patient-facing AI-generated exercise guidance. Extracted material was grouped by evidentiary role and synthesized through framework synthesis and governance mapping to distinguish literature-supported observations, author interpretation, and proposed implementation tools. Results: The included sources were first organized into five thematic groups: digital exercise delivery and exercise-prescription evidence; explainability, trust, and automation bias literature; professional responsibility, ethics, and patient disclosure literature; regulatory and policy documents; and digital literacy, patient/clinician attitudes, and equity literature. The synthesis then proceeded from safety relevance to explanation needs, human oversight and escalation needs, and selected regulatory and policy signals before translating these strands into conceptual and implementation-oriented outputs rather than empirically validated instruments. AI-generated exercise guidance was most safety-relevant in scenarios involving individualized dose, progression, contraindication-sensitive action, or rehabilitation strategy. Across the included sources, generic transparency alone was not sufficient to support reviewable use; relevant explanation elements included evidence sources, risk warnings, reasoning paths, and reasonable alternatives. Oversight considerations varied with embodied risk, clinical ambiguity, user vulnerability, and likelihood of direct enactment. Implementation considerations linked interface design, clinical review, escalation, auditability, and post-deployment monitoring. Conclusions: AI-generated exercise guidance in digital healthcare may warrant governance attention as a patient-safety and accountability issue when it influences actionable exercise decisions. The proposed framework offers a conceptual basis for designing more reviewable and accountable mobile and remote exercise-support services. Future work can validate these outputs in patient-facing services, clinician review workflows, usability studies, implementation pilots, and safety evaluations.","affiliation":"School of Marxism, Xiamen Ocean Vocational College, Xiamen 361100, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Patient Safety","Exercise Prescription","Telerehabilitation","Digital Healthcare","Explainable Artificial Intelligence","Human Oversight","Generative Ai"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare14121716"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13300344"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13300344?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-15"},{"id":"42359442","source":"MED","pmid":"42359442","pmcid":"PMC13290728","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13290728"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1830254","title":"Artificial intelligence in undergraduate medical education clinical skills curricula: a scoping review of implementations since 2022.","authorString":"Thind BS, Javidi D, Schwartz LM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Thind BS","firstName":"Birpartap S","lastName":"Thind","initials":"BS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, California University of Science and Medicine, Colton, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Javidi D","firstName":"Daryoush","lastName":"Javidi","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Education, California University of Science and Medicine, Colton, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Schwartz LM","firstName":"Lisa M","lastName":"Schwartz","initials":"LM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Education, California University of Science and Medicine, Colton, CA, United States."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","issn":"2673-253X","essn":"2673-253X","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1830254","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose</h4>To systematically identify and synthesize peer-reviewed literature describing implemented AI innovations within undergraduate medical education clinical skills curricula from January 2022 through January 2026.<h4>Method</h4>The authors conducted a scoping review querying PubMed and Scopus, supplemented by SciSpace as an AI-assisted citation discovery tool. Eligible studies described utilizing AI to deliver the clinical skills curriculum in innovative ways (e.g., instruction in history-taking, communication, clinical reasoning, clinical documentation, OSCE/simulation assessment). We extracted data into standardized templates and thematically sorted to characterize how AI-assisted instruction was being implemented across educational objectives.<h4>Results</h4>From 1,130 initial records, 39 studies met inclusion criteria. AI-assisted instruction clustered into eight thematic categories: LLM-Based Virtual Patient and Clinical Simulation Systems (<i>n</i> = 19), AI-Augmented OSCE and Simulation Assessment Tools (<i>n</i> = 6), Embodied and Robotic AI Clinical Simulations (<i>n</i> = 4), AI-Supported Procedural and Technical Skills Training (<i>n</i> = 3), AI-Assisted Clinical Documentation and EHR-Based Skills Training (<i>n</i> = 2), Multimodal Analytics for Skills Assessment (<i>n</i> = 2), Educator-Facing AI Case Authoring and Simulation Design Tools (<i>n</i> = 2), and AI-Supported Clinical Reasoning and Tutoring Tools (<i>n</i> = 1). Publication activity concentrated heavily in 2024-2025, with virtual patient applications representing the dominant category.<h4>Conclusions</h4>AI implementation in clinical skills education has accelerated substantially since 2022, with large language model-powered virtual patient simulations emerging as the predominant application. Current implementations primarily position AI as a supplementary formative tool rather than a replacement for established pedagogical approaches. Robust evidence regarding long-term educational impact remains limited, indicating need for rigorous longitudinal evaluation alongside continued innovation.","affiliation":"School of Medicine, California University of Science and Medicine, Colton, CA, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Undergraduate Medical Education","Clinical Skills","Virtual Patient Simulation","Scoping Review","Osce Assessment","Generative Ai"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1830254"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13290728"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13290728?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-10"},{"id":"42141031","source":"MED","pmid":"42141031","pmcid":"PMC13179377","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13179377"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41746-026-02740-0","title":"Large language models require a new form of oversight: capability-based monitoring.","authorString":"Kellogg KC, Ye B, Hu Y, Savova GK, Wallace B, Bitterman DS.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kellogg KC","firstName":"Katherine C","lastName":"Kellogg","initials":"KC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"MIT Sloan School of Management, Boston, MA, USA. kkellogg@mit.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Ye B","firstName":"Bingyang","lastName":"Ye","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"AI in Medicine Program, Mass General Brigham, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Brigham and Women's Hospital/Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, Brandeis University, Waltham, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Hu Y","firstName":"Yifan","lastName":"Hu","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Harvard John A. Paulson School Of Engineering And Applied Sciences, Cambridge, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Savova GK","firstName":"Guergana K","lastName":"Savova","initials":"GK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Computation Health Informatics Program, Boston Children's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Wallace B","firstName":"Byron","lastName":"Wallace","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Khoury College of Computer Sciences, Northeastern University, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Bitterman DS","firstName":"Danielle S","lastName":"Bitterman","initials":"DS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"AI in Medicine Program, Mass General Brigham, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA. dbitterman@bwh.harvard.edu."},{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Brigham and Women's Hospital/Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, MA, USA. dbitterman@bwh.harvard.edu."},{"affiliation":"Computation Health Informatics Program, Boston Children's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA. dbitterman@bwh.harvard.edu."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4188236,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","issn":"2398-6352","essn":"2398-6352","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"375","affiliation":"MIT Sloan School of Management, Boston, MA, USA. kkellogg@mit.edu.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Letter"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"ACS.ASTRO-CSDG-24-1244514-01-CTPS.pc.gr.222210","agency":"American Cancer Society","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01 CA294033","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U24CA248010","agency":"National Cancer Institute","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"UM1TR004408","agency":"National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01CA294033-01","agency":"Division of Cancer Prevention, National Cancer Institute","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U24CA248010","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U54CA274516-01A1","agency":"National Cancer Institute","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U24 CA248010","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"UM1TR004408","agency":"NCATS NIH HHS","acronym":"TR","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"ME-2024C2-37484","agency":"Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute","acronym":"PCORI","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U54CA274516-01A1","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-026-02740-0"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13179377"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13179377?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-17","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-21","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-15"},{"id":"42174156","source":"MED","pmid":"42174156","pmcid":"PMC13197458","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13197458"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41746-026-02785-1","title":"Expert perspectives on the ecosystem of medical AI oversight in the GenAI era.","authorString":"Gilbert S, Dai T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Gilbert S","firstName":"Stephen","lastName":"Gilbert","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Else Kröner Fresenius Center for Digital Health, Faculty of Medicine, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany. stephen.gilbert@tu-dresden.de."},{"affiliation":"Faculty of Business and Economics, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany. stephen.gilbert@tu-dresden.de."}]}},{"fullName":"Dai T","firstName":"Tinglong","lastName":"Dai","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Carey Business School, Johns Hopkins University, Baltimore, MD, USA."},{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Johns Hopkins University, Baltimore, MD, USA."},{"affiliation":"Hopkins Business of Health Initiative, Johns Hopkins University, Washington, DC, USA."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4188236,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","issn":"2398-6352","essn":"2398-6352","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"395","affiliation":"Else Kröner Fresenius Center for Digital Health, Faculty of Medicine, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany. stephen.gilbert@tu-dresden.de.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-026-02785-1"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13197458"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13197458?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-22","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-22"},{"id":"41909056","source":"MED","pmid":"41909056","pmcid":"PMC13022756","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13022756"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1736616","title":"Through the looking glass: ethical considerations regarding LLM-induced hallucinations to medical questions.","authorString":"Kozlakidis Z, Wootton T, Mayrhofer MT.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kozlakidis Z","firstName":"Zisis","lastName":"Kozlakidis","initials":"Z","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3836-7209"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"International Agency for Research on Cancer, World Health Organization, Lyon, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Wootton T","firstName":"Tracy","lastName":"Wootton","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"International Agency for Research on Cancer, World Health Organization, Lyon, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Mayrhofer MT","firstName":"Michaela Th","lastName":"Mayrhofer","initials":"MT","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Human Genetics, Medical University of Innsbruck, Innsbruck, Austria."},{"affiliation":"Papillon Pathways e.U., Landskron, Austria."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3836-7209"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","issn":"2673-253X","essn":"2673-253X","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1736616","affiliation":"International Agency for Research on Cancer, World Health Organization, Lyon, France.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["discussion","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"001","agency":"World Health Organization","acronym":"WHO_","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Ethics","Hallucinations","Dynamic Framework","Ethical Accountability"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1736616"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13022756"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13022756?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-30","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-30","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-13"},{"id":"42325280","source":"MED","pmid":"42325280","pmcid":"PMC13275711","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13275711"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1755696","title":"The Agentic AI Framework (AAIF): a policy-enforced architecture for accountable and high-performance intrusion detection.","authorString":"Adabara I, Sadiq BO, Shuaibu AN, Danjuma YI, Maninti V, Joe M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Adabara I","firstName":"Ibrahim","lastName":"Adabara","initials":"I","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computing, Faculty of Science and Technology, Kampala International University, Kampala, Uganda."}]}},{"fullName":"Sadiq BO","firstName":"Bashir Olaniyi","lastName":"Sadiq","initials":"BO","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Electrical, Telecommunication, and Computer Engineering, Kampala International University, Kampala, Uganda."}]}},{"fullName":"Shuaibu AN","firstName":"Aliyu Nuhu","lastName":"Shuaibu","initials":"AN","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Electrical, Telecommunication, and Computer Engineering, Kampala International University, Kampala, Uganda."}]}},{"fullName":"Danjuma YI","firstName":"Yale Ibrahim","lastName":"Danjuma","initials":"YI","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computing, Faculty of Science and Technology, Kampala International University, Kampala, Uganda."}]}},{"fullName":"Maninti V","firstName":"Venkateswarlu","lastName":"Maninti","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computing, Faculty of Science and Technology, Kampala International University, Kampala, Uganda."}]}},{"fullName":"Joe M","firstName":"Mutebi","lastName":"Joe","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computing, Faculty of Science and Technology, Kampala International University, Kampala, Uganda."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8949-7540"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","issn":"2624-8212","essn":"2624-8212","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1755696","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence plays a central role in modern cybersecurity, yet systems optimized for detection accuracy often lack mechanisms for accountability, transparency, and policy compliance. This study proposes the Agentic AI Framework (AAIF), a policy-aware intrusion detection architecture that integrates predictive modeling with executable governance. Guided by Design Science Research, the framework combines a deep learning detection model with a governance layer aligned to the NIST AI Risk Management Framework 2.0. A key component is an interpretable Policy Engine that enforces operational and ethical constraints through a declarative YAML-based domain-specific language, ensuring that each decision is auditable and policy-compliant. The framework was evaluated on the CICIDS2017 dataset, which contains over 2.8 million network flow records across benign and malicious traffic. Results show that AAIF preserves predictive performance relative to baseline models, including Random Forest, Support Vector Machine, and Deep Neural Network, achieving a weighted <i>F</i>1-score of 0.483 and an AUROC of 0.978. At the same time, the framework achieved complete compliance under the defined policy schema, with an Ethical Compliance Rate of 1.0 and a False Escalation Rate of 0.0. The Governance Compliance Index improved from 0.947 to 0.983, demonstrating stronger alignment between system decisions and governance requirements. These findings show that policy-enforced inference can support accountable autonomy without degrading detection capability. The AAIF provides a reproducible and governance-aware approach that transforms conventional intrusion detection systems into transparent and auditable decision systems. This work establishes a practical foundation for deploying policy-aligned AI in cybersecurity environments.","affiliation":"Department of Computing, Faculty of Science and Technology, Kampala International University, Kampala, Uganda.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Accountability","Cybersecurity","Intrusion Detection System","Explainable Ai","Ai Governance","Agentic Ai","Nist Ai Risk Management Framework","Policy-aware Ai"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1755696"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13275711"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13275711?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-22","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-04","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-04"},{"id":"42358410","source":"MED","pmid":"42358410","pmcid":"PMC13291560","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13291560"]},"doi":"10.1002/mco2.70833","title":"Generative Artificial Intelligence and Large Language Models in Clinical Oncology.","authorString":"Yu Y, Zhao Z, Wang Z, Lin R, Tan Y, Chen Y, Li T, Baptista-Hon D, Zhang X, Wu C, Tong M, Zheng L, Wu J, Monteiro O, Zhang K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Yu Y","firstName":"Yunfang","lastName":"Yu","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2579-6220"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence Cross Disciplinary Research Institute, Faculty of Medicine, Faculty of Innovation Engineering, School of Computer Science and Engineering Macau University of Science and Technology Macau SAR China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Oncology, Department of Pharmacy, Phase I Clinical Trial Centre, Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hospital, School of Computer Science and Engineering & Key Laboratory of Machine Intelligence and Advanced Computing Sun Yat-sen University Guangzhou China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Breast Surgery, The First Affiliated Hospital Jinan University Guangzhou China."},{"affiliation":"Guangdong Provincial Key Laboratory of Cancer Pathogenesis and Precision Diagnosis and Treatment, AI Big Data Laboratory, Shenshan Medical Center Memorial Hospital of Sun Yat-Sen University Shanwei China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhao Z","firstName":"Zhenhui","lastName":"Zhao","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence Cross Disciplinary Research Institute, Faculty of Medicine, Faculty of Innovation Engineering, School of Computer Science and Engineering Macau University of Science and Technology Macau SAR China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang Z","firstName":"Zehua","lastName":"Wang","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence Cross Disciplinary Research Institute, Faculty of Medicine, Faculty of Innovation Engineering, School of Computer Science and Engineering Macau University of Science and Technology Macau SAR China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lin R","firstName":"Ruichong","lastName":"Lin","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence Cross Disciplinary Research Institute, Faculty of Medicine, Faculty of Innovation Engineering, School of Computer Science and Engineering Macau University of Science and Technology Macau SAR China."}]}},{"fullName":"Tan Y","firstName":"Yujie","lastName":"Tan","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence Cross Disciplinary Research Institute, Faculty of Medicine, Faculty of Innovation Engineering, School of Computer Science and Engineering Macau University of Science and Technology Macau SAR China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Oncology, Department of Pharmacy, Phase I Clinical Trial Centre, Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hospital, School of Computer Science and Engineering & Key Laboratory of Machine Intelligence and Advanced Computing Sun Yat-sen University Guangzhou China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen Y","firstName":"Yongjian","lastName":"Chen","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine Solna, Center For Molecular Medicine Karolinska Institutet Stockholm Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Li T","firstName":"Ting","lastName":"Li","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2339-5901"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence Cross Disciplinary Research Institute, Faculty of Medicine, Faculty of Innovation Engineering, School of Computer Science and Engineering Macau University of Science and Technology Macau SAR China."}]}},{"fullName":"Baptista-Hon D","firstName":"Daniel","lastName":"Baptista-Hon","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence Cross Disciplinary Research Institute, Faculty of Medicine, Faculty of Innovation Engineering, School of Computer Science and Engineering Macau University of Science and Technology Macau SAR China."},{"affiliation":"School of Medicine University of Dundee Dundee UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang X","firstName":"Xiaoxi","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Oncology, Department of Pharmacy, Phase I Clinical Trial Centre, Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hospital, School of Computer Science and Engineering & Key Laboratory of Machine Intelligence and Advanced Computing Sun Yat-sen University Guangzhou China."},{"affiliation":"Shenzhen Loop Area Institute Shenzhen China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wu C","firstName":"Chuan","lastName":"Wu","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Computing and Data Science The University of Hong Kong Hong Kong SAR China."}]}},{"fullName":"Tong M","firstName":"Man","lastName":"Tong","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Biomedical Sciences The Chinese University of Hong Kong Hong Kong SAR China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zheng L","firstName":"Lijun","lastName":"Zheng","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"China United Network Communications Corporation GuangZhou Branch Guangzhou China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wu J","firstName":"Junyan","lastName":"Wu","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Oncology, Department of Pharmacy, Phase I Clinical Trial Centre, Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hospital, School of Computer Science and Engineering & Key Laboratory of Machine Intelligence and Advanced Computing Sun Yat-sen University Guangzhou China."}]}},{"fullName":"Monteiro O","firstName":"Olivia","lastName":"Monteiro","initials":"O","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence Cross Disciplinary Research Institute, Faculty of Medicine, Faculty of Innovation Engineering, School of Computer Science and Engineering Macau University of Science and Technology Macau SAR China."},{"affiliation":"School of Medicine University of Dundee Dundee UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang K","firstName":"Kang","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence Cross Disciplinary Research Institute, Faculty of Medicine, Faculty of Innovation Engineering, School of Computer Science and Engineering Macau University of Science and Technology Macau SAR China."},{"affiliation":"The First Affiliated Hospital of Chongqing Medical University Chongqing China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Ophthalmology, Zhuhai People's Hospital and the First Affiliated Hospital of Faculty of Medicine Macau University of Science and Technology Zhuhai China."},{"affiliation":"Guangzhou National Laboratory Guangzhou China."},{"affiliation":"Eye and Vision Innovation Center Eye Valley Wenzhou China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2339-5901"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2579-6220"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"7","journalIssueId":4218550,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","journal":{"title":"MedComm","medlineAbbreviation":"MedComm (2020)","issn":"2688-2663","essn":"2688-2663","isoabbreviation":"MedComm (2020)","nlmid":"101769925"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e70833","abstractText":"Cancer remains a major global health challenge, and the increasing availability of multimodal biomedical data has created unprecedented opportunities for precision oncology. Recent advances in generative artificial intelligence (AI), particularly large language models (LLMs), have enabled new approaches for integrating heterogeneous data sources, including electronic health records, medical imaging, pathology, genomics, and clinical text. However, current studies remain fragmented across specific tasks, cancer types, and model architectures, and a comprehensive synthesis of how generative AI can support the entire oncology continuum is still lacking. This review provides an overview of generative AI in clinical oncology, covering LLMs, generative adversarial networks, diffusion models, and multimodal foundation models. We summarize their methodological foundations and discuss applications in cancer diagnosis, prognosis prediction, treatment planning, patient management, and clinical trial optimization. Particular attention is given to multimodal data integration, synthetic data generation, clinical reasoning, and decision support, together with current challenges related to interpretability, reliability, data privacy, regulatory governance, and real-world implementation. By consolidating recent technological advances and clinical evidence, this review highlights future priorities toward safe, trustworthy, and clinically deployable intelligent oncology systems. Emerging agent-based architectures and human-AI collaborative workflows may further expand the clinical utility of generative AI in oncology.","affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence Cross Disciplinary Research Institute, Faculty of Medicine, Faculty of Innovation Engineering, School of Computer Science and Engineering Macau University of Science and Technology Macau SAR China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"0022/2025/RIA1","agency":"Fundo para o Desenvolvimento das Ciências e da Tecnologia","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"0055/2022/A1","agency":"Fundo para o Desenvolvimento das Ciências e da Tecnologia","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"82404087","agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Oncology","Clinical Decision Support","Multimodal Data Integration","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/mco2.70833"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13291560"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13291560?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-23","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-23"},{"id":"42224752","source":"MED","pmid":"42224752","doi":"10.1016/j.ijmedinf.2026.106513","title":"Safety guardrails in patient-facing large language model systems for chronic disease self-management: a realist review.","authorString":"Zhao Y, Miao Y, Luo Y, Guo R, Wang H, Wu Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zhao Y","firstName":"Yuhan","lastName":"Zhao","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-0461-1913"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Capital Medical University, No. 10 Xitoutiao, Youanmenwai, Fengtai District, Beijing 100069, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Miao Y","firstName":"Yiqun","lastName":"Miao","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Capital Medical University, No. 10 Xitoutiao, Youanmenwai, Fengtai District, Beijing 100069, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Luo Y","firstName":"Yuan","lastName":"Luo","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1198-3877"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Capital Medical University, No. 10 Xitoutiao, Youanmenwai, Fengtai District, Beijing 100069, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Guo R","firstName":"Rongrong","lastName":"Guo","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Capital Medical University, No. 10 Xitoutiao, Youanmenwai, Fengtai District, Beijing 100069, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang H","firstName":"Huiying","lastName":"Wang","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Capital Medical University, No. 10 Xitoutiao, Youanmenwai, Fengtai District, Beijing 100069, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wu Y","firstName":"Ying","lastName":"Wu","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Capital Medical University, No. 10 Xitoutiao, Youanmenwai, Fengtai District, Beijing 100069, China. Electronic address: helenywu@vip.163.com."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1198-3877"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-0461-1913"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"218","journalIssueId":4222291,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","journal":{"title":"International journal of medical informatics","medlineAbbreviation":"Int J Med Inform","issn":"1386-5056","essn":"1872-8243","isoabbreviation":"Int J Med Inform","nlmid":"9711057"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"106513","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Patient-facing large language model (LLM) systems are increasingly proposed as scalable tools for chronic disease self-management support. In this setting, safety depends not only on factual accuracy but also on whether outputs are interpretable, trusted, and used safely over time.<h4>Objective</h4>To explain how safety guardrails shape outcomes in patient-facing LLM-supported self-management across different task-risk, user, and interaction contexts.<h4>Methods</h4>We conducted a realist review of LLM-based or LLM-enabled generative conversational systems used for self-management of long-term physical health conditions. Searches of PubMed, Web of Science Core Collection, IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, and arXiv covered all indexed years to 1 April 2026 and used terms for chronic disease or self-management, patient-facing conversational systems, and LLMs or generative AI, identifying 1,154 records before deduplication. The core evidence base comprised 21 studies and 38 context-mechanism-outcome configurations (CMOCs). The patient-facing self-management task, rather than disease label, was the unit of synthesis.<h4>Results</h4>At the study level, the dominant patient-facing task was coded as low risk in 6 studies, moderate risk in 11, and high risk in 4; 17 studies evaluated mainly single-turn interactions and 4 included multi-turn, sequential, or simulated-consultation elements. Most evidence concerned simulated or expert-judged patient-facing tasks rather than sustained real-world deployment. Three patterns recurred. Provenance-related safeguards improved transparency and checkability more consistently than they ensured safe downstream action. Communication-oriented safeguards improved readability or perceived comprehensibility while leaving recurring gaps in completeness or actionability. Boundary-control strategies, including source-bounded retrieval, clinician deferral, and escalation support, became more important as task actionability and interaction complexity increased.<h4>Conclusions</h4>In patient-facing chronic disease self-management, safety cannot be judged adequately by answer plausibility alone. This review develops a refined programme theory and a risk-linked, theory-generating heuristic framework, but many proposed mechanisms remain indirect and require real-world, longitudinal, multi-turn testing before deployment.","affiliation":"School of Nursing, Capital Medical University, No. 10 Xitoutiao, Youanmenwai, Fengtai District, Beijing 100069, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Chronic Disease","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Patient Safety"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Self-Management","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Evaluation","Chronic disease","Self-management","Governance","Large Language Models","Patient-facing Ai","Safety Guardrails"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijmedinf.2026.106513"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-28","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-27"},{"id":"41942581","source":"MED","pmid":"41942581","pmcid":"PMC13053716","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13053716"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41746-026-02621-6","title":"Guardrails for GenAI drafted replies in patient portal messaging.","authorString":"Sakr J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sakr J","firstName":"Johnny","lastName":"Sakr","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-0732-6373"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Law, Prince Sultan University, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia. jsakr@psu.edu.sa."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-0732-6373"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4171893,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","issn":"2398-6352","essn":"2398-6352","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"283","affiliation":"College of Law, Prince Sultan University, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia. jsakr@psu.edu.sa.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Letter"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Prince Sultan University","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-026-02621-6"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13053716"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13053716?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-06","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-06","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-07","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-06","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-06"},{"id":"42231938","source":"MED","pmid":"42231938","pmcid":"PMC13223092","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13223092"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1785700","title":"Artificial intelligence and digital health equity: a post-pandemic evidence synthesis and implementation safeguards framework.","authorString":"Okwei ANN.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Okwei ANN","firstName":"Albert Nii Noi","lastName":"Okwei","initials":"ANN","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"L. Douglas Wilder School of Government and Public Affairs, Virginia Commonwealth University, Richmond, VA, United States."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","issn":"2673-253X","essn":"2673-253X","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1785700","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>The rapid expansion of AI-enabled digital health after COVID-19 created new possibilities for extending care while raising concerns about unequal access, subgroup underperformance, and weak accountability. These effects remain difficult to interpret because telehealth infrastructure, predictive analytics, clinical decision support, and generative AI operate through different equity mechanisms.<h4>Methods</h4>A transparent narrative evidence synthesis was conducted using peer-reviewed literature and selected governance documents. Structured searches of PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science, and IEEE Xplore were refreshed on March 5, 2026, for English-language sources published between January 1, 2020, and December 31, 2025. A second reviewer independently assessed a sample of 12 full-text inclusion and exclusion decisions using the same pre-specified criteria, with 100% agreement. The final corpus included 29 sources.<h4>Results</h4>Evidence was strongest for digital access barriers, subgroup underperformance, and implementation-related governance concerns. AI-assisted screening and digitally mediated remote support showed conditional benefits in targeted settings when high-touch support was built into implementation. Evidence on generative AI and language-support tools was comparatively smaller, newer, and concentrated in representational-bias applications, with limited evidence of sustained downstream equity gains.<h4>Conclusion</h4>AI-enabled digital health may reduce selected barriers to care when supported by equitable access conditions, subgroup validation, accountable governance, and implementation oversight. Equity gains were conditional rather than automatic, and evidentiary depth varied substantially across modalities. The review distills recurring patterns into a synthesis-derived operational roadmap for procurement, validation, implementation, and post-deployment monitoring.","affiliation":"L. Douglas Wilder School of Government and Public Affairs, Virginia Commonwealth University, Richmond, VA, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Health Policy","Health Disparities","Telehealth","Digital Divide","Algorithmic Bias","Covid-19","Digital Health Equity"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1785700"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13223092"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13223092?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-03","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-03","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-04","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-18","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-18"},{"id":"41972116","source":"MED","pmid":"41972116","pmcid":"PMC13061868","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13061868"]},"doi":"10.3389/jaws.2026.16349","title":"Large Language Models in Surgery: Promise, Pitfalls, and Practical Use.","authorString":"Denham DT, Wang CY, Maric E, Hinton LR, Heniford BT.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Denham DT","firstName":"Danette T","lastName":"Denham","initials":"DT","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastrointestinal and General Surgery, Department of Surgery, Endeavor Health, Evanston, IL, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang CY","firstName":"Colin Y","lastName":"Wang","initials":"CY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastrointestinal and General Surgery, Department of Surgery, Endeavor Health, Evanston, IL, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Maric E","firstName":"Emil","lastName":"Maric","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2826-0934"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastrointestinal and General Surgery, Department of Surgery, Endeavor Health, Evanston, IL, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Hinton LR","firstName":"Lucy R","lastName":"Hinton","initials":"LR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastrointestinal and General Surgery, Department of Surgery, Endeavor Health, Evanston, IL, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Heniford BT","firstName":"B Todd","lastName":"Heniford","initials":"BT","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastrointestinal and General Surgery, Department of Surgery, Endeavor Health, Evanston, IL, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2826-0934"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"5","journalIssueId":4119730,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of abdominal wall surgery : JAWS","medlineAbbreviation":"J Abdom Wall Surg","issn":"2813-2092","essn":"2813-2092","isoabbreviation":"J Abdom Wall Surg","nlmid":"9918713888406676"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"16349","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Large Language Models (LLMs) represent a transformative advancement in artificial intelligence (AI) with rapidly expanding applications in medicine. While AI-related medical publications increased 36-fold between 2000-2022, practical guidance for surgeons remains limited. This mini-review delineates pragmatic applications of LLMs in surgical practice while addressing key limitations, implementation considerations, and ethical considerations.<h4>Methods</h4>We reviewed contemporary LLM platforms and their integration into clinical workflows, patient communication, surgical research and academic writing, evaluating both benefits, constraints and risk mitigation relevant to practicing surgeons.<h4>Findings</h4>LLMs demonstrate significant utility across multiple domains. In clinical workflows, ambient documentation and chart summarization may reduce documentation burden and support rapid synthesis of complex patient data. For patient communication, these tools can simplify complex medical information, tailor or translate patient instructions to appropriate reading levels or languages, and generate empathetic responses to patient messages with improved efficiency. In research, LLMs assist with literature summarization, study design optimization, and risk of bias assessment in RCT, allowing surgeons to focus on higher-level scientific reasoning. Despite promising applications, several constraints demand attention. Effective prompting requires specific techniques including clear clinical objectives, explicit instructions, and iterative refinement. LLM outputs require verification to prevent \"hallucinations\" - fabricated or inaccurate information. Protected health information (PHI) must never be entered into public LLM platforms to maintain HIPAA compliance. Liability frameworks for AI-generated errors remain ambiguous, with unclear responsibility deferred amongst providers, institutions, and developers.<h4>Conclusion</h4>LLMs offer surgeons valuable tools for enhancing workflow efficiency and patient communication when deployed with appropriate oversight. Success requires understanding prompt engineering principles, maintaining rigorous fact-checking protocols, protecting patient privacy, and recognizing that human judgment remains irreplaceable in clinical decision-making.","affiliation":"Division of Gastrointestinal and General Surgery, Department of Surgery, Endeavor Health, Evanston, IL, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Surgery","Academic Research","Patient Outcomes","Artificial Intelligence (Ai)","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/jaws.2026.16349"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13061868"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13061868?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-13","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-26"},{"id":"42488378","source":"MED","pmid":"42488378","doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1862980","title":"Algorithmic scholarship and academic evaluation: governance misalignment in the age of generative AI.","authorString":"Baciu C, Mohan G, Wilson J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Baciu C","firstName":"Cristina","lastName":"Baciu","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"W. P. Carey School of Business, Arizona State University, Tempe, AZ, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Mohan G","firstName":"Gopalakrishnan","lastName":"Mohan","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"W. P. Carey School of Business, Arizona State University, Tempe, AZ, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Wilson J","firstName":"Jeffrey","lastName":"Wilson","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"W. P. Carey School of Business, Arizona State University, Tempe, AZ, United States."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","issn":"2624-8212","essn":"2624-8212","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1862980","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Generative artificial intelligence (AI), particularly large language models, is rapidly becoming embedded in academic research and scholarly publishing. These systems assist with drafting, literature synthesis, and analytical writing, increasingly contributing to the production of academic text. This shift raises a central question: how should higher education institutions evaluate scholarly contribution when parts of research production become technologically mediated?<h4>Methods</h4>This paper examines governance misalignment between publication standards and institutional evaluation systems in higher education. Drawing on an exploratory qualitative survey of 18 journal editors and associate editors across business-related disciplines, we analyze editorial perspectives on AI-assisted manuscript preparation, authorship, accountability, productivity, and academic evaluation.<h4>Results</h4>We identify three recurring concerns: policy fragmentation, ambiguity surrounding authorship and accountability, and apprehension about AI-enabled productivity acceleration. We introduce the concept of <i>metric distortion</i> to describe the weakening relationship between measurable scholarly outputs and the intellectual labor those outputs are assumed to represent under conditions of AI-assisted production. As drafting and textual production become more technologically scalable, publication-based indicators may become less reliable proxies for conceptual contribution and interpretive effort.<h4>Discussion</h4>Building on scholarship on academic capitalism, audit culture, and digital governance, the paper argues that generative AI functions as a stress test for existing evaluation regimes. We propose a process-based framework emphasizing disclosure, documented intellectual contribution, and institutional alignment between editorial governance and tenure evaluation systems.","affiliation":"W. P. Carey School of Business, Arizona State University, Tempe, AZ, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["authorship","Higher Education","Research Integrity","Distributed Cognition","Academic Evaluation","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Scholarly Governance"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1862980"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-23","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-08","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-08"},{"id":"42371372","source":"MED","pmid":"42371372","doi":"10.1007/s10439-026-04242-2","title":"Un-AI-ing: Compliance, Evasion, and the Distortion of Research Writing in the Age of AI Detection.","authorString":"Giray L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Giray L","firstName":"Louie","lastName":"Giray","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1940-035X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Liberal Arts, School of Foundational Studies and Education, Mapua University, Manila, Philippines. lggiray@mapua.edu.ph."},{"affiliation":"Research Center for International and Global Higher Education, Khazar University, Baku, Azerbaijan. lggiray@mapua.edu.ph."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1940-035X"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4205552,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Annals of biomedical engineering","medlineAbbreviation":"Ann Biomed Eng","issn":"0090-6964","essn":"1573-9686","isoabbreviation":"Ann Biomed Eng","nlmid":"0361512"}},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"To protect research integrity from an influx of AI-generated content, academic journals have increasingly deployed AI detection tools. AI detection tools are designed to evaluate text and predict whether it was written by a human or a large language model. Rather than verifying factual truth or authorial honesty, they operate by measuring statistical predictability, primarily analyzing text through metrics like perplexity (word predictability) and burstiness (sentence structure variation). While journals adopt these tools as an efficient, scalable gatekeeping defense against academic fraud, this algorithmic surveillance has triggered a troubling counter-behavior known as un-AI-ing. Un-AI-ing is the deliberate modification of text specifically to evade algorithmic detection thresholds. Because formal, peer-reviewed scientific prose inherently relies on highly standardized and predictable language, authentic human writing is frequently misclassified as AI-generated, forcing authors to alter their work. This creates a dangerous systemic paradox divided into two behaviors. (1) Dishonest actors engage in opportunistic un-AI-ing, using AI humanizers or other maneuvers to artificially disrupt text predictability and easily launder synthetic content into literature. (2) Conversely, honest researchers, disproportionately non-native English speakers, are forced into compliant un-AI-ing. They must systematically degrade their clear, well-edited prose into awkward phrasing simply to bypass false positive thresholds. By policing metrics rather than merit, journals are not catching fraud, they are manufacturing marginalization. To restore true accountability, academic publishing must abandon automated gatekeeping and return to context-sensitive, disclosure-based authorship policies that judge the integrity of the scholar, not the algorithmic conformity of the text.","affiliation":"Department of Liberal Arts, School of Foundational Studies and Education, Mapua University, Manila, Philippines. lggiray@mapua.edu.ph.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Letter"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["False positives","Scientific Authorship","Algorithmic Governance","Ai Detection Bias","Generative Ai In Research","Manuscript Integrity","Postplagiarism Framework","Un-ai-ing","Ai Humanizers"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10439-026-04242-2"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-29"},{"id":"41977864","source":"MED","pmid":"41977864","pmcid":"PMC13074417","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13074417"]},"doi":"10.3390/s26072079","title":"A Systematic Review of Safety-Driven Approaches in Human-Robot Collaborative Systems.","authorString":"Khan A, Akhtar M, Qureshi SM, Mustafa M, Alsaleh NA, Ahmad I.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Khan A","firstName":"Akhtar","lastName":"Khan","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Electrical Engineering, Iqra Nations University, Peshawar 25000, Pakistan."}]}},{"fullName":"Akhtar M","firstName":"Maaz","lastName":"Akhtar","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5061-8090"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Industrial Engineering Department, College of Engineering, Imam Mohammad Ibn Saud Islamic University (IMSIU), Riyadh 11432, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Qureshi SM","firstName":"Sheheryar Mohsin","lastName":"Qureshi","initials":"SM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3728-9720"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Computing, Engineering and Physical Sciences, University of the West of Scotland, Paisley PA1 2BE, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Mustafa M","firstName":"Muzzamil","lastName":"Mustafa","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4896-0305"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Artificial Intelligence, University of Management and Technology, Lahore 54782, Pakistan."}]}},{"fullName":"Alsaleh NA","firstName":"Naser A","lastName":"Alsaleh","initials":"NA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5779-0451"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Industrial Engineering Department, College of Engineering, Imam Mohammad Ibn Saud Islamic University (IMSIU), Riyadh 11432, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Ahmad I","firstName":"Imran","lastName":"Ahmad","initials":"I","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1931-3659"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Industrial Engineering, University of Engineering and Technology, Peshawar 25000, Pakistan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5779-0451"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3728-9720"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4896-0305"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5061-8090"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1931-3659"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"26","journalIssueId":4179674,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Sensors (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Sensors (Basel)","issn":"1424-8220","essn":"1424-8220","isoabbreviation":"Sensors (Basel)","nlmid":"101204366"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"2079","abstractText":"Collaboration between humans and robots (HRC) is advancing rapidly due to the intersection of robotics and generative artificial intelligence (GenAI). The current paper includes a systematic review of 103 studies on the role of generative models, including Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), Variational Autoencoders (VAEs), diffusion models, and Large Language Models (LLMs) in improving the safety, trust, and adaptability of collaborative robotics using a PRISMA-based systematic approach. The review recognizes four major themed areas of GenAI-based safety frameworks-namely, data-driven simulation to synthesize hazards, predictive reasoning to forecast human motion, adaptive control to reduce risks dynamically, and trust-aware cognition to explain human-robot interaction. Findings indicate that generative models transform robotic safety from a reactive mechanism to proactive, contextual and interpretable systems. Nevertheless, real-time performance, interpretability, standard benchmarking, and ethical assurance are still some of the challenges to be overcome. The paper proposes a taxonomy linking generative modeling layers and physical, cognitive and ethical aspects of HRC safety, and gives a roadmap of certifiable hybrid systems with generative foresight and deterministic control. This synthesis provides a foundation for developing transparent, adaptive, and trustworthy collaborative robotic systems.","affiliation":"Department of Electrical Engineering, Iqra Nations University, Peshawar 25000, Pakistan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"IMSIU-DDRSP2602","agency":"This work was supported and funded by the Deanship of Scientific Research at Imam Mohammad Ibn Saud Islamic University (IMSIU)","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cooperative Behavior"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Safety"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Robotics","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Adversarial Networks"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Autoencoder"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Prisma","Generative Adversarial Networks","Generative Ai","Large Language Models","Variational Auto Encoders"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s26072079"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13074417"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13074417?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-14","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-27"},{"id":"42354470","source":"MED","pmid":"42354470","pmcid":"PMC13299217","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13299217"]},"doi":"10.3390/healthcare14121612","title":"From Integrated Care to Learning Systems.","authorString":"Tsitiridis A, Perakis K, Antoniades A, Manias G.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tsitiridis A","firstName":"Aristeidis","lastName":"Tsitiridis","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6544-2676"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Face Recognition & Artificial Vision, Escuela Técnica Superior de Ingeniería Informática (ETSII), Universidad Rey Juan Carlos, Calle Tulipan S/N, 28933 Mostoles, Madrid, Spain."},{"affiliation":"Department of Informatics and Systems Engineering, Defence Academy of the UK, Cranfield University, Shrivenham, Swindon SN6 8LA, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Perakis K","firstName":"Konstantinos","lastName":"Perakis","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2236-9816"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ubitech Ltd., 15231 Chalandri, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Antoniades A","firstName":"Athos","lastName":"Antoniades","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5789-5103"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Stremble Ventures Ltd., 4042 Limassol, Cyprus."}]}},{"fullName":"Manias G","firstName":"George","lastName":"Manias","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0128-2022"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Digital Systems, University of Piraeus, Karaoli & Dimitriou 80, 18534 Piraeus, Greece."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6544-2676"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2236-9816"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5789-5103"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0128-2022"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"12","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4221244,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Healthcare (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","issn":"2227-9032","essn":"2227-9032","isoabbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","nlmid":"101666525"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1612","abstractText":"Integrated care is increasingly shaped by digital infrastructures, data governance, and AI-enabled analytics, yet the relevant literature remains fragmented across health-services research, digital health, and machine learning. This article reports a scoping review, conducted in line with PRISMA-ScR guidance, that maps how integrated care models have evolved conceptually, what digital and AI-enabled infrastructures support them, how their clinical, economic, and equity impacts can be evaluated, and what current implementations imply for sustainable scaling. We searched PubMed, Scopus, Semantic Scholar, and Crossref (retrieval date 31 October 2025; forward screening to 31 March 2026) and added grey literature from named policy bodies. The searches identified 15,189 records, reducing to 11,789 after intra- and cross-source deduplication and grey-literature integration; 620 full texts were assessed and 192 were included in the synthesis. Four domains were synthesised: conceptual foundations of integrated care, AI and multimodal analytics, implementation barriers, and digital-governance foundations. We chart the field using a Type I-V maturity scheme (disease, cohort, whole-system, digital-integrated, learning), benchmarked against the Rainbow, MacColl, EMRAM/AMAM, and NHS ICS models. Most deployments cluster at digitally integrated but only weakly adaptive Type IV; recurrent failure modes-temporal blind spots, maintenance debt, semantic drift, and governance gaps-block progression to Type V, and high-profile clinical-AI failures illustrate the cost of attempting Type V analytics on Type IV-or-worse infrastructure. A walk through nine world regions maps each to its current Type I-V position and shows that organisational and payment integration-not digital sophistication alone-is currently the dominant driver of progress. The COMFORTage Integrated Care Model Library is positioned as a workflow of AI agents orchestrating predictive, preventive, and personalised care across the integrated-care lifecycle rather than as a single federated-learning programme. The review positions AI-enabled integrated care less as a finished model than as an emerging design space requiring longitudinal data assets, stewarded model lifecycles, accountable governance, and outcome-based contracting for clinically useful, equitable, and trustworthy learning systems.","affiliation":"Face Recognition & Artificial Vision, Escuela Técnica Superior de Ingeniería Informática (ETSII), Universidad Rey Juan Carlos, Calle Tulipan S/N, 28933 Mostoles, Madrid, Spain.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Global Health","Integrated Care Models","Integrated Care","Healthy Ageing","Scoping Review","Learning Health Systems","Data Governance","Prisma-scr","Eu Ai Act","Large Language Models","Future-ai","Multimodal Analytics","Stewarded Learning"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare14121612"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13299217"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13299217?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"PPR1181656","source":"PPR","type":"Preprint in","note":"Link created based on a title-first author match","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-08","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-08"},{"id":"41148218","source":"MED","pmid":"41148218","pmcid":"PMC13065136","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13065136"]},"doi":"10.34067/kid.0000001037","title":"Artificial Intelligence in Critical Care Nephrology: Current Applications, Emerging Techniques, and Challenges to Clinical Integration.","authorString":"Cheungpasitporn W, Thongprayoon C, Kashani K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Cheungpasitporn W","firstName":"Wisit","lastName":"Cheungpasitporn","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9954-9711"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota."}]}},{"fullName":"Thongprayoon C","firstName":"Charat","lastName":"Thongprayoon","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8313-3604"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota."}]}},{"fullName":"Kashani K","firstName":"Kianoush","lastName":"Kashani","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2184-3683"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota."},{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9954-9711"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8313-3604"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2184-3683"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"7","journalIssueId":4164147,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Kidney360","medlineAbbreviation":"Kidney360","issn":"2641-7650","essn":"2641-7650","isoabbreviation":"Kidney360","nlmid":"101766381"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"664-677","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI), including machine learning, deep learning, reinforcement learning, and generative AI, has the potential to advance critical care nephrology (CCN) by enhancing prediction accuracy, improving diagnostic capabilities, supporting clinical decision making, and streamlining workflow processes. Current applications in CCN include AKI prediction, nephrotoxin surveillance, intradialytic hypotension forecasting, and AI-guided dialysis and continuous KRT management, with performance often exceeding traditional models. However, the effect on patient-centered outcomes such as mortality, dialysis dependence, and cost-effectiveness remains uncertain. Emerging techniques, such as conformal prediction for calibrated risk estimates, causal inference for intervention modeling, and reinforcement learning for adaptive ultrafiltration, show promise in enhancing reliability, interpretability, and individualized care. Generative AI and large language models extend these applications to clinical documentation, reasoning, and patient education, while raising new challenges, including hallucinations, regulatory oversight, and clinician trust. Persistent barriers such as data heterogeneity, limited external validation, alert fatigue, and economic constraints hinder broad adoption. This review synthesizes the current evidence and outlines four priorities for advancing AI in CCN: ( 1 ) rigorous multicenter validation focused on clinical outcomes, ( 2 ) integration of uncertainty quantification and causal modeling into AI tools, ( 3 ) development of clinician-centered interfaces that minimize cognitive load, and ( 4 ) establishment of transparent, adaptive regulatory and governance frameworks. Realizing the promise of AI in CCN will require multidisciplinary collaboration, fairness and generalizability testing, and sustainable implementation strategies that align technologic innovation with measurable improvements in patient care.","affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Critical Care","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Nephrology","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Acute Kidney Injury","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Clinical Decision-Making"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Deep Learning"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Dialysis","acute renal failure","Aki","Acute Kidney Failure"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.34067/KID.0000001037"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13065136"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13065136?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":3,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-30","dateOfCreation":"2025-10-28","firstIndexDate":"2025-10-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-10-28","firstPublicationDate":"2025-10-28"},{"id":"42025798","source":"MED","pmid":"42025798","pmcid":"PMC13170483","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13170483"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.mayocp.2026.04.007","title":"The Evolving Role of Large Language Models in Health Care Research and Scientific Communication.","authorString":"Zand J, Suvakov M, Overgaard SM, Silvano CJ, Meiners LM, Fan JW, Carter RE, Wieland ML, Patten CA, Dugani SB, Dieter HL, Wi CL, Juhn YJ, Enders FT, Callstrom MR, Shah VH, Garovic VD.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zand J","firstName":"Jaleh","lastName":"Zand","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Suvakov M","firstName":"Milovan","lastName":"Suvakov","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Overgaard SM","firstName":"Shauna M","lastName":"Overgaard","initials":"SM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Digital Health, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Silvano CJ","firstName":"Carmen J","lastName":"Silvano","initials":"CJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Meiners LM","firstName":"Laura M","lastName":"Meiners","initials":"LM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7308-1812"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Fan JW","firstName":"Jungwei W","lastName":"Fan","initials":"JW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Department of Artificial Intelligence and Informatics, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Carter RE","firstName":"Rickey E","lastName":"Carter","initials":"RE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Wieland ML","firstName":"Mark L","lastName":"Wieland","initials":"ML","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Community Internal Medicine, Geriatrics, and Palliative Care, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Patten CA","firstName":"Christi A","lastName":"Patten","initials":"CA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Psychiatry and Psychology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Dugani SB","firstName":"Sagar B","lastName":"Dugani","initials":"SB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Division of Hospital Internal Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Dieter HL","firstName":"Heidi L","lastName":"Dieter","initials":"HL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research Administration, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Wi CL","firstName":"Chung-Li","lastName":"Wi","initials":"CL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatric and Adolescent Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Juhn YJ","firstName":"Young J","lastName":"Juhn","initials":"YJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Department of Pediatric and Adolescent Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Enders FT","firstName":"Felicity T","lastName":"Enders","initials":"FT","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Division of Clinical Trials and Biostatistics, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Callstrom MR","firstName":"Matthew R","lastName":"Callstrom","initials":"MR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Shah VH","firstName":"Vijay H","lastName":"Shah","initials":"VH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Garovic VD","firstName":"Vesna D","lastName":"Garovic","initials":"VD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN. Electronic address: garovic.vesna@mayo.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7308-1812"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4166742,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Mayo Clinic proceedings","medlineAbbreviation":"Mayo Clin Proc","issn":"0025-6196","essn":"1942-5546","isoabbreviation":"Mayo Clin Proc","nlmid":"0405543"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S0025-6196(26)18483-3","affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"UL1TR002377","agency":"National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"UL1 TR002377","agency":"NCATS NIH HHS","acronym":"TR","orderIn":0}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mayocp.2026.04.007"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13170483"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13170483?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mayocp.2026.04.007"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"Y","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"Y","manuscriptId":"NIHMS2168058","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-21"},{"id":"42332966","source":"MED","pmid":"42332966","pmcid":"PMC13287375","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13287375"]},"doi":"10.2196/88536","title":"Blockchain-Based Dynamic and Revocable Consent for Secondary Health Data Use: Systematic Review.","authorString":"Phuyal S, Bhandari M, Bista R, Ferreira JC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Phuyal S","firstName":"Sudip","lastName":"Phuyal","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7524-1843"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Sciences, Technology and Architecture Research Center (ISTAR), Iscte - Instituto Universitário de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal."}]}},{"fullName":"Bhandari M","firstName":"Manila","lastName":"Bhandari","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8354-581X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Sciences, Technology and Architecture Research Center (ISTAR), Iscte - Instituto Universitário de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal."}]}},{"fullName":"Bista R","firstName":"Rabindra","lastName":"Bista","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0638-5840"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Kathmandu University, Dhulikhel, Nepal."}]}},{"fullName":"Ferreira JC","firstName":"João Carlos","lastName":"Ferreira","initials":"JC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6662-0806"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Sciences, Technology and Architecture Research Center (ISTAR), Iscte - Instituto Universitário de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"Faculty of Logistics, Molde University College, Britvegen 2, Molde, 6410, Norway, 47 71214000."},{"affiliation":"Inov Inesc, Lisbon, Portugal."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7524-1843"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0638-5840"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6662-0806"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8354-581X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4204633,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR medical informatics","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Med Inform","issn":"2291-9694","essn":"2291-9694","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Med Inform","nlmid":"101645109"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e88536","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>The secondary use of health data holds substantial potential for advancing biomedical research, strengthening population health analytics, and enabling artificial intelligence-driven decision-making support. Yet, ensuring that such reuse respects patient autonomy, privacy, and regulatory obligations remains a major challenge. Conventional consent mechanisms are typically static, difficult to revoke, and offer limited transparency or accountability after data disclosure.<h4>Objective</h4>This review aimed to systematically examine blockchain-based frameworks that enable dynamic, auditable, and revocable consent for the secondary use of health data.<h4>Methods</h4>A structured literature search was conducted in PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science covering the period 2020 to 2025. Following PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses) guidelines, 55 peer-reviewed studies meeting predefined inclusion criteria were analyzed. Data extraction focused on four dimensions: (1) consent life cycle management, (2) auditability and traceability, (3) usability and patient empowerment, and (4) legal and ethical alignment.<h4>Results</h4>Findings indicate that blockchain technologies provide a robust foundation for automating consent life cycles, ensuring immutable auditability, and enabling decentralized patient control. Most frameworks used smart contracts, decentralized identifiers, and verifiable credentials to implement programmable and verifiable consent processes. Nevertheless, key challenges persist, including limited usability testing, complexities in real-time revocation propagation, interoperability gaps with clinical systems, and tensions with regulatory requirements such as the General Data Protection Regulation right to erasure. Only a small subset of studies reported real-world deployments or user-centered evaluations.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Blockchain offers substantial promise for improving the trustworthiness, transparency, and accountability of consent management for secondary health data use. However, wider adoption requires human-centered design approaches, stronger interoperability through standards such as Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources, verifiable credentials, and consent receipts, and clearer legal guidance for compliance. Future research should prioritize integrating blockchain-enabled consent infrastructures into national and cross-border digital health ecosystems such as the European Health Data Space to support secure, patient-controlled, and ethically governed secondary data use.","affiliation":"Information Sciences, Technology and Architecture Research Center (ISTAR), Iscte - Instituto Universitário de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Confidentiality"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Informed Consent"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Blockchain"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Data Privacy","Dynamic Consent","Blockchain","Auditability","General Data Protection Regulation","Gdpr","Ehds","European Health Data Space","Health Care Data Sharing","Revocable Consent"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/88536"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13287375"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13287375?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-22"},{"id":"41978750","source":"MED","pmid":"41978750","pmcid":"PMC13070263","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13070263"]},"doi":"10.21542/gcsp.2026.9","title":"Reimagining cardiac care with AI, LLMs, blockchain, and metaverse.","authorString":"Musamih A, Salah K, Omar M, Ellaham S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Musamih A","firstName":"Ahmad","lastName":"Musamih","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Management Science and Engineering, Abu Dhabi, UAE."}]}},{"fullName":"Salah K","firstName":"Khaled","lastName":"Salah","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer and Information Engineering, Abu Dhabi, UAE."}]}},{"fullName":"Omar M","firstName":"Mohammed","lastName":"Omar","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Management Science and Engineering, Abu Dhabi, UAE."}]}},{"fullName":"Ellaham S","firstName":"Samer","lastName":"Ellaham","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Heart and Vascular Institute, Cleveland Clinic Abu Dhabi, Abu Dhabi, UAE."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"2026","journalIssueId":4179706,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"Global cardiology science & practice","medlineAbbreviation":"Glob Cardiol Sci Pract","issn":"2305-7823","essn":"2305-7823","isoabbreviation":"Glob Cardiol Sci Pract","nlmid":"101613130"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e202609","abstractText":"<b>Objective:</b> Artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping cardiology by analyzing complex data and enabling precision care. Among its recent advances, large language models (LLMs) have gained prominence for their potential to enhance clinical reasoning, documentation, and education. Alongside AI, blockchain, and metaverse-based extended reality (XR) are transforming data security, interoperability, and immersive visualization. This review consolidates the current literature on these technologies, evaluates their roles in cardiology, and introduces a conceptual framework for integrating them to support a secure, intelligent, and interactive digital ecosystem. <b>Methods:</b> A structured narrative review was conducted using PubMed and Scopus to identify peer-reviewed studies published between 2018 and 2025. Systematic identification and screening of the literature were performed and reported using PRISMA 2020. Eligible publications included original research, reviews, and conceptual papers addressing the use of AI, LLMs, blockchain, or XR in cardiology. The literature was analyzed thematically, and findings were synthesized qualitatively to describe applications, limitations, and opportunities for integration. <b>Results:</b> Across 50 studies, LLMs showed promise in documentation, education, and decision support but faced challenges in accuracy, validation, and bias. Blockchain applications improved data exchange, consent management, and provenance tracking, though scalability and interoperability remain unresolved. XR technologies enhanced procedural precision, training, and patient engagement but were limited by cost and validation. A conceptual integration model illustrates how these technologies function within a unified cardiovascular ecosystem where blockchain provides the trust layer, LLMs deliver analytical intelligence, and XR enables immersive interaction. <b>Conclusion:</b> Integrating AI, LLMs, blockchain, and XR offers a pathway toward intelligent, secure, and interactive cardiovascular care. Future research should validate integrated frameworks, develop ethical and regulatory standards, and promote responsible adoption of AI in cardiology.","affiliation":"Department of Management Science and Engineering, Abu Dhabi, UAE.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21542/gcsp.2026.9"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13070263"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13070263?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-14","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-28","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-28"},{"id":"41837238","source":"MED","pmid":"41837238","pmcid":"PMC12979488","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12979488"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1759211","title":"Audit-as-code: a policy-as-code framework for continuous AI assurance.","authorString":"Muhammad AE, Yow KC, Alsenan S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Muhammad AE","firstName":"Aoun E","lastName":"Muhammad","initials":"AE","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-4127-1260"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Engineering and Applied Science, University of Regina, Regina, SK, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Yow KC","firstName":"Kin-Choong","lastName":"Yow","initials":"KC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Engineering and Applied Science, University of Regina, Regina, SK, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Alsenan S","firstName":"Shrooq","lastName":"Alsenan","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Information Systems, College of Computer and Information Sciences, Princess Nourah Bint Abdurahman University, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-4127-1260"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","issn":"2624-8212","essn":"2624-8212","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1759211","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Existing AI assurance and governance frameworks rely heavily on documented written policies and manual reviews of the implementation. The primary challenge is not the length of these documents, but to operationalize the gap from transforming qualitative requirements into verifiable controls. This approach makes ensuring continuous compliance through the development life cycle hard to enforce, scale, and reproduce.<h4>Methods</h4>This study presents a continuous assurance framework called Audit-as-Code that maps governance requirements to technically-auditable rules, that can be a combination of versioned policy specification and executable checks for evidence artifacts, linked to structured evidence regarding data, models, provenance, performance, decisions, and explanations regarding the decisions being made. While the framework addresses the governance and regulatory mapping requirements, the primary focus of this study is MLOps/CI-CD (continuous integration/continuous delivery) operationalization, and turning these requirements into deterministic checks and gate decisions integrated in operational workflows. In this study, we introduce an assured readiness score that integrates the governance risk with other key responsible AI principles, such as traceability and explainability. This approach helps in aligning deployment decisions with predefined risk tiers, and the framework automates decisions on whether a system can proceed, requires remediation and fixes, or should be blocked. It also provides targeted suggestions for improvement and compliance for the lags identified.<h4>Results</h4>We evaluated this framework on representative AI systems and demonstrated how a single evidence bundle can be used to support assessment across different governance regulations.<h4>Discussion</h4>In doing so, Audit-as-Code ensures AI assurance transforms from a documentation-driven policy module to a quantitative, auditable, reproducible, and operationally practical module to ensure compliance.","affiliation":"Faculty of Engineering and Applied Science, University of Regina, Regina, SK, Canada.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Reproducibility","Compliance","Traceability","Governance","Explainability","Ai Assurance","Ci/Cd","Policy-As-Code"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1759211"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12979488"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12979488?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-17","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-26"},{"id":"PMC13297825","source":"PMC","pmcid":"PMC13297825","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13297825"]},"title":"Artificial Intelligence in Infectious Disease Care: Selected Applications in Tuberculosis, Sepsis, and Antimicrobial Stewardship","authorString":"Caliman-Sturdza O, Gheorghita R, Filip R, Lobiuc A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Caliman-Sturdza O","firstName":"Olga Adriana","lastName":"Caliman-Sturdza","initials":"O","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine and Biological Sciences, Stefan cel Mare University of Suceava, 720229 Suceava, Romania; (O.A.C.-S.); (A.L.)"}]}},{"fullName":"Gheorghita R","firstName":"Roxana Elena","lastName":"Gheorghita","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine and Biological Sciences, Stefan cel Mare University of Suceava, 720229 Suceava, Romania; (O.A.C.-S.); (A.L.)"}]}},{"fullName":"Filip R","firstName":"Roxana","lastName":"Filip","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine and Biological Sciences, Stefan cel Mare University of Suceava, 720229 Suceava, Romania; (O.A.C.-S.); (A.L.)"}]}},{"fullName":"Lobiuc A","firstName":"Andrei","lastName":"Lobiuc","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine and Biological Sciences, Stefan cel Mare University of Suceava, 720229 Suceava, Romania; (O.A.C.-S.); (A.L.)"}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"12","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4220334,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Diagnostics (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Diagnostics (Basel)","issn":"2075-4418","essn":"2075-4418","isoabbreviation":"Diagnostics (Basel)","nlmid":"101658402"}},"pubYear":"2026","language":"eng","pubModel":"Undetermined","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"760073/23.05.2023","agency":"Ministry of Research, Innovation and Digitization, under the Romania’s National Recovery and Resilience Plan","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Infection","Machine Learning","Treatment Monitoring","Deep Learning","Diagnosis Accuracy"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"PubMedCentral","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13297825/?tool=EBI"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"PubMedCentral","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13297825/pdf/?tool=EBI"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13297825"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13297825?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCreation":"2026-06-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-26","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},{"id":"42147713","source":"MED","pmid":"42147713","pmcid":"PMC13177757","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13177757"]},"doi":"10.5005/jp-journals-10071-25188","title":"Artificial Intelligence Literacy in Intensive Care: From Algorithmic Fluency to Clinical Accountability.","authorString":"Ghatak T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ghatak T","firstName":"Tanmoy","lastName":"Ghatak","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5916-1832"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Sanjay Gandhi Postgraduate Institute of Medical Sciences, Lucknow, Uttar Pradesh, India."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5916-1832"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"30","journalIssueId":4194819,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Indian journal of critical care medicine : peer-reviewed, official publication of Indian Society of Critical Care Medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"Indian J Crit Care Med","issn":"0972-5229","essn":"1998-359X","isoabbreviation":"Indian J Crit Care Med","nlmid":"101208863"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"272-274","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Artificial intelligence (AI), particularly large language models (LLMs), is increasingly being used in intensive care environments to assist with documentation, literature synthesis, and clinical information retrieval. While these tools offer efficiency and cognitive support in high-pressure settings such as the intensive care unit (ICU), they also introduce risks related to hallucination, automation bias, and a lack of explainability.<h4>Aim</h4>To examine the emerging challenges associated with AI use in critical care-especially hallucination and algorithmic overconfidence-and to highlight the need for structured AI literacy and governance frameworks for intensivists.<h4>Findings</h4>Recent analyses show that AI hallucination-the generation of fabricated or distorted information-is an intrinsic feature of probabilistic language models rather than a rare technical error. In critical care contexts, this may manifest as fabricated references, distorted clinical reasoning, or overconfident recommendations. Additional concerns include automation bias, where clinicians may inadvertently rely on algorithmic outputs, and opacity in AI decision-making processes. Emerging evidence demonstrates that while AI tools may enhance efficiency in tasks such as documentation and knowledge summarization, they remain unreliable for unsupervised clinical reasoning.<h4>Conclusion</h4>The integration of AI into intensive care practice should be guided by structured AI literacy, institutional governance frameworks, and continued human oversight. Artificial intelligence can serve as a supportive cognitive tool, but responsibility for clinical judgment, patient safety, and ethical decision-making must remain firmly with the clinician.<h4>How to cite this article</h4>Ghatak T. Artificial Intelligence Literacy in Intensive Care: From Algorithmic Fluency to Clinical Accountability. Indian J Crit Care Med 2026;30(4):272-274.","affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Sanjay Gandhi Postgraduate Institute of Medical Sciences, Lucknow, Uttar Pradesh, India.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Clinical Decision-making","Critical Care Medicine","Artificial Intelligence Hallucination"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.5005/jp-journals-10071-25188"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13177757"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13177757?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-18","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-18","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-19","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-01"},{"id":"41965650","source":"MED","pmid":"41965650","pmcid":"PMC13085389","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13085389"]},"doi":"10.1186/s12912-026-04555-6","title":"NAIL-G: a conceptual best-fit framework for nursing ai literacy and governance anchored in socio-technical safety science.","authorString":"Ibrahim FM, Shahrour G, Abuijlan IAM, Abdallah AM, Alsuwayed A, Alanazi AR.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ibrahim FM","firstName":"Fatma M","lastName":"Ibrahim","initials":"FM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2762-3350"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Nursing, Mansoura University, Mansoura, Egypt. fatma_magdi@mans.edu.eg."},{"affiliation":"RAK Medical and Health Science University, RAK College of Nursing, RAK, UAE. fatma_magdi@mans.edu.eg."}]}},{"fullName":"Shahrour G","firstName":"Ghada","lastName":"Shahrour","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Nursing, Jordan University of Science and Technology, Irbid, Jordan."},{"affiliation":"RAK Medical and Health Science University, RAK College of Nursing, RAK, UAE."}]}},{"fullName":"Abuijlan IAM","firstName":"Israa A M","lastName":"Abuijlan","initials":"IAM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"RAK Medical and Health Science University, RAK College of Nursing, RAK, UAE."}]}},{"fullName":"Abdallah AM","firstName":"Amal Mohammed","lastName":"Abdallah","initials":"AM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"RAK Medical and Health Science University, RAK College of Nursing, RAK, UAE."}]}},{"fullName":"Alsuwayed A","firstName":"Asmaa","lastName":"Alsuwayed","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8564-6964"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Management, College of Business, King Saud University, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Alanazi AR","firstName":"Abdulkarem Radhi","lastName":"Alanazi","initials":"AR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Public Administration Department, College of Business, King Saud University, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2762-3350"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8564-6964"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"25","journalIssueId":4177715,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"BMC nursing","medlineAbbreviation":"BMC Nurs","issn":"1472-6955","essn":"1472-6955","isoabbreviation":"BMC Nurs","nlmid":"101088683"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"370","abstractText":"BACKGROUND: Artificial intelligence is reshaping health care delivery and education, but nurses lack discipline-specific guidance that integrates artificial intelligence literacy with socio-technical safety and governance. Existing AI literacy and digital competence models are often generic and do not reflect nursing standards, work systems or responsibilities for patient safety and equity. METHODS: A best-fit framework synthesis and concept development were used to integrate scholarship on artificial intelligence literacy, digital competence, socio-technical safety, nursing competency standards and policy guidance on transparent use of artificial intelligence in scholarly work, with extracted constructs mapped to the AACN Essentials and socio-technical work-system dimensions. RESULTS: The Nursing Artificial Intelligence Literacy and Governance (NAIL-G) framework comprises five domains covering Foundations & Tools; Quality, Safety & Risk; Professionalism, Ethics & Law; Systems, Equity & Social Impact; and Scholarship, Communication & Governance. It positions artificial intelligence literacy as competency-plus-governance, emphasizing critical judgement, verification, disclosure and equity-aware practice across nursing education, clinical work and scholarly communication. CONCLUSIONS: NAIL-G offers curriculum designers, educators, regulators and clinical leaders a structured way to embed artificial intelligence capabilities, safeguards and governance expectations across nursing practice and education. The framework can guide integration of artificial intelligence-related learning outcomes, assessment tasks and safety checks into curricula, support reflective use of artificial intelligence in clinical reasoning and documentation, and strengthen nurses’ confidence to raise concerns about unsafe or inequitable systems.","affiliation":"Faculty of Nursing, Mansoura University, Mansoura, Egypt. fatma_magdi@mans.edu.eg.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Conceptual framework","Nursing Education","Global Health Policy","Digital Competence","Ai Literacy","Nursing Governance","Socio-technical Safety"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12912-026-04555-6"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13085389"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13085389?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-11","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-11"},{"id":"41728578","source":"MED","pmid":"41728578","pmcid":"PMC12920046","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12920046"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.101890","title":"Large Language Models in Radiologist-Patient Communication: A Narrative Review for Clinical Practice.","authorString":"Naidu J, Muthyala H, Naidu SS, Muralidharan S, Baskaradoss VK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Naidu J","firstName":"Jatin","lastName":"Naidu","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Radiology, Division of Surgery and Interventional Science, University College London, London, GBR."},{"affiliation":"Radiology, Lister Hospital, East and North Hertforshire NHS Trust, Stevanage, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Muthyala H","firstName":"Hitesh","lastName":"Muthyala","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Radiology, University College London Medical School, London, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Naidu SS","firstName":"Sonia S","lastName":"Naidu","initials":"SS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology, University College London Medical School, London, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Muralidharan S","firstName":"Sandeep","lastName":"Muralidharan","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Computer Science, University of Hull, Hull, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Baskaradoss VK","firstName":"Vasanth K","lastName":"Baskaradoss","initials":"VK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Radiology, Kettering General Hospital NHS Foundation Trust, Kettering, GBR."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9849-1976"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4084352,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","issn":"2168-8184","essn":"2168-8184","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e101890","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) are used in radiology to simplify reports, translate findings, and support patient-facing communication, yet their clinical value and safety remain uncertain. This narrative review was conducted in accordance with the Scale for the Assessment of Narrative Review Articles (SANRA) quality criteria and synthesises evidence from 49 studies published between 2020 and 2025, focusing on clinician-mediated use of LLMs across four domains: report simplification, multilingual translation, patient education, and patient attitudes. Across studies, LLMs consistently improved readability by 2-6 grade levels, but only one randomised trial directly assessed patient comprehension. A professional review was required in up to 80% of outputs in controlled settings, compared with <10% in observational studies. Harmful factual errors were uncommon but non-negligible (0-10% depending on task and model). Translation performance was highest for high-resource languages, while semantic drift was more frequent in low-resource languages, necessitating bilingual review. Patients generally accepted AI-assisted communication when clinician oversight was explicit. Current regulatory and professional guidance support supervised, institution-hosted deployment. Evidence supports specific use cases, patient summaries, translation drafts, and educational materials, but does not justify autonomous deployment or direct patient self-use. Key evidence gaps remain in comprehension outcomes, workflow impact, and real-world validation.","affiliation":"Radiology, Division of Surgery and Interventional Science, University College London, London, GBR.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Radiology","Governance","Patient Communication","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.101890"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12920046"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12920046?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-21","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-20"},{"id":"42205634","source":"MED","pmid":"42205634","pmcid":"PMC13203309","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13203309"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.107729","title":"Detecting Citation Veneer in Guideline-Grounded Clinical and Public Health Large Language Model (LLM) Outputs: A Clinical Evidence Audit Grid Using CENHOV Tags.","authorString":"Harada Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Harada Y","firstName":"Yuusuke","lastName":"Harada","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Public Policy, Hosei University, Tokyo, JPN."},{"affiliation":"Humanities and Social Sciences, Hiroshima University, Hiroshima, JPN."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4172604,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","issn":"2168-8184","essn":"2168-8184","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e107729","abstractText":"Large language model (LLM) systems are increasingly used to summarize clinical guidelines, draft patient-facing education, and answer evidence-linked clinical or public health questions. We define citation veneer as an observable audit state in which an output presents citation cues or apparently supportive source information while still containing incorrect, incomplete, or materially unsupported content. We develop and evaluate the Clinical Evidence Audit Grid (CEAG), an analyst-facing monitoring surface that encodes six binary response-quality signals into a fixed-length plain-text audit tag (CENHOV): clinical correctness (C), evidential support (E), numeric concordance (N), hedging (H), stylistic ornamentation (O), and a derived CEAG-V supported-error citation veneer marker (V=1 when E=1 and C=0). CEAG was evaluated on a controlled benchmark (synthetic, n=120 questions per condition) and on a 12-case public check built from PubHealth-style health-claim examples and public medical or public-health sources. The controlled benchmark used scripted response regimes rather than a live LLM/API benchmark; therefore, model identity, sampling temperature, and seed are not applicable to the main controlled analysis. In the synthetic benchmark, the RUSH regime retained high evidential-support rates (93.3%, 95% CI 87.4%-96.6%) while dropping to 73.3% correctness (95% CI 64.8%-80.4%) and producing 25.8% CEAG-V veneer (95% CI 18.8%-34.3%). In the 12-case public check, RUSH produced 66.7% CEAG-V veneer (95% CI 39.1%-86.2%), whereas VERIFY and POSTER produced none; because n=12, these public-case rates are descriptive pilot data only. CEAG is not proposed as a patient-facing display or a replacement for conventional charts. It is a clinical informatics monitoring aid for reviewers who need to detect apparently well-sourced hallucinations, compare response regimes, and separate stylistic change from substantive trust change.","affiliation":"Public Policy, Hosei University, Tokyo, JPN.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Quality assurance","Patient education","Public Health","Patient Safety","Clinical Decision Support","Clinical Informatics","Large Language Models","Retrieval-augmented Generation","Citation Veneer","Hallucination Auditing"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.107729"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13203309"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13203309?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-26"},{"id":"42241685","source":"MED","pmid":"42241685","pmcid":"PMC13235976","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13235976"]},"doi":"10.2196/93710","title":"AI-Assisted Formative Assessment in Clinical Education: From Algorithms to Agency.","authorString":"Nguyen QT, Ha TM, Mai T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Nguyen QT","firstName":"Quang Thanh","lastName":"Nguyen","initials":"QT","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8413-1910"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Health Sciences, VinUniversity, Da Ton, Gia Lam, Hanoi, Hanoi, 100000, Vietnam, 84 89902123."},{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, Vietnam National Children's Hospital, Hanoi, Hanoi, Vietnam."},{"affiliation":"Royal College of Surgeons in Ireland and University College Dublin Malaysia Campus, George Town, Penang, Malaysia."}]}},{"fullName":"Ha TM","firstName":"Thuy Minh","lastName":"Ha","initials":"TM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2256-200X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Medical and Health Sciences Education, University of Auckland, Auckland, New Zealand."}]}},{"fullName":"Mai T","firstName":"Tina","lastName":"Mai","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-3140-387X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, University of California, San Francisco, San Francisco, CA, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8413-1910"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2256-200X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-3140-387X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"12","journalIssueId":4204590,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR medical education","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Med Educ","issn":"2369-3762","essn":"2369-3762","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Med Educ","nlmid":"101684518"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e93710","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly reshaping clinical education by embedding assessment and feedback into everyday learning activities. Medical students can now use machine learning dashboards, generative AI, large language models, and emerging agentic systems to practice clinical reasoning, communication, and procedural skills while receiving individualized feedback within seconds. However, the availability of more data and more feedback does not necessarily produce better learning. This Viewpoint is intended for clinical educators, assessment leaders, curriculum committees, faculty developers, and institutional leaders who must decide how AI should be used in formative activities without reducing education to automated scoring. AI-assisted formative assessment is defined in this paper as the intentional use of AI tools to generate, organize, and support interpretation of performance information for learning rather than grading. Its distinctive contribution lies in the scale, adaptivity, conversational simulation, pattern detection, and possible autonomy of AI systems. However, AI outputs become formative only when learners and educators interpret them critically, judge their trustworthiness, and translate them into a small number of focused follow-on learning actions. This paper synthesizes the current evidence base while noting that much of it remains early, heterogeneous, and concentrated in short-term or single-setting studies. It examines key risks, including hallucination, automation bias, epistemic overtrust, hidden curricular effects, and broader concerns related to professional identity, power asymmetries, data privacy, and inequitable access. It also presents context-specific implementation examples for preclinical case-based learning, communication and objective structured clinical examination preparation, procedural skill laboratories, clerkship learning, and programmatic assessment portfolios, together with practical implications for faculty development, institutional governance, and phased local implementation. As a Viewpoint rather than an empirical study or systematic review, the framework and examples should be interpreted as evidence-informed design propositions that require local evaluation and validation. Overall, the value of AI-assisted formative assessment depends less on the volume of AI-generated feedback than on educational designs that preserve learner agency, professional judgment, and human accountability.","affiliation":"College of Health Sciences, VinUniversity, Da Ton, Gia Lam, Hanoi, Hanoi, 100000, Vietnam, 84 89902123.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Algorithms"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Education, Medical","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Educational Measurement","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Clinical Competence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Feedback","Medical education","AI","Formative Assessment","Clinical Education"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/93710"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13235976"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13235976?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-09","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-06","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-04","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-04"},{"id":"42359444","source":"MED","pmid":"42359444","pmcid":"PMC13292296","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13292296"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1808373","title":"The EU AI Act: implications and compliance guidance for healthcare facilities.","authorString":"Dennstädt F, Hastings J, Windisch P, Jovanovic A, Marić TŽ, Brüningk S, Aebersold DM, Knopf A, Cihoric N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Dennstädt F","firstName":"Fabio","lastName":"Dennstädt","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Inselspital, Bern University Hospital and University of Bern, Bern, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Hastings J","firstName":"Janna","lastName":"Hastings","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, University of St. Gallen, St. Gallen, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Institute for Implementation Science in Health Care, University of Zurich, Zurich, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Swiss Institute of Bioinformatics, Lausanne, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Windisch P","firstName":"Paul","lastName":"Windisch","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Inselspital, Bern University Hospital and University of Bern, Bern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Cantonal Hospital Winterthur, Winterthur, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Jovanovic A","firstName":"Aleksa","lastName":"Jovanovic","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Wemedoo AG, Steinhausen, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Marić TŽ","firstName":"Tijana Žunić","lastName":"Marić","initials":"TŽ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Zunic Law, Belgrade, Serbia."}]}},{"fullName":"Brüningk S","firstName":"Sarah","lastName":"Brüningk","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Inselspital, Bern University Hospital and University of Bern, Bern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, Department Digital Medicine, University of Bern, Bern, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Aebersold DM","firstName":"Daniel M","lastName":"Aebersold","initials":"DM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Inselspital, Bern University Hospital and University of Bern, Bern, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Knopf A","firstName":"Antje","lastName":"Knopf","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8212-571X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Inselspital, Bern University Hospital and University of Bern, Bern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, Department Digital Medicine, University of Bern, Bern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Sitem Center for Translational Medicine and Biomedical Entrepreneurship, University of Bern, Bern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Department of Radiotherapy and Radiation Oncology, Faculty of Medicine, University Hospital Carl Gustav Carus, Technische Universität Dresden, Dresden, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Cihoric N","firstName":"Nikola","lastName":"Cihoric","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Inselspital, Bern University Hospital and University of Bern, Bern, Switzerland."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8212-571X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","issn":"2673-253X","essn":"2673-253X","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1808373","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>The European Union AI Act [Regulation (EU) 2024/1689] establishes the first comprehensive legal framework for artificial intelligence. While AI offers transformative potential in healthcare, its deployment introduces risks regarding safety, bias, and accountability. There is currently a lack of practical operational frameworks to help healthcare facilities transition from legal theory to clinical compliance.<h4>Methods</h4>We performed a qualitative regulatory analysis of the EU AI Act, specifically focusing on the obligations of \"deployers\" (Articles 26, 27, and 50) in clinical settings. The Act's requirements were cross-referenced with established clinical governance standards (e.g., MDR 2017/745 and FUTURE-AI guidelines). A 10-step compliance roadmap was synthesized and exemplified through a hypothetical case study of a high-risk multimodal breast cancer AI system.<h4>Results</h4>The analysis identifies healthcare as a primary focus of the Act, with most clinical AI tools classified as \"high-risk\". We established a four-phase implementation framework: (1) Foundational Strategy and Governance, (2) System Analysis & Risk Assessment, (3) Operational Integration, and (4) Ongoing Compliance. Key results include the definition of mandatory Fundamental Rights Impact Assessments (FRIA), requirements for site-specific technical validation, and the necessity of establishing trust through structured human oversight mechanisms to mitigate automation bias.<h4>Conclusion</h4>The EU AI Act necessitates a shift from transactional procurement to a lifecycle-spanning compliance partnership between vendors and hospitals. While the administrative burden is substantial, the Act provides the essential framework for the safe scaling of medical AI. Proactive alignment with these standards, particularly regarding AI literacy and human oversight, is a strategic necessity for healthcare facilities to ensure patient safety and regulatory readiness by the August 2026 enforcement deadline.","affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Inselspital, Bern University Hospital and University of Bern, Bern, Switzerland.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"225913","agency":"Swiss National Science Foundation","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Regulation","Compliance","Digitalization","Guidance","Artifiicial Intelligence","Eu Ai Act","Ai Implementation"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1808373"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13292296"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13292296?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-10"},{"id":"42013455","source":"MED","pmid":"42013455","pmcid":"PMC13146229","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13146229"]},"doi":"10.2196/94813","title":"Clinical Model Autophagy: The Risk of Interpretative Drift in Recursive Medical AI.","authorString":"Shih PF.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Shih PF","firstName":"Pei Fan","lastName":"Shih","initials":"PF","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-3689-5568"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-3689-5568"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4169896,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR medical informatics","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Med Inform","issn":"2291-9694","essn":"2291-9694","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Med Inform","nlmid":"101645109"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e94813","abstractText":"The rapid integration of large language models into electronic medical record systems introduces a critical theoretical vulnerability. Drawing on foundational computer science proofs of \"model collapse,\" this viewpoint introduces the concept of \"Clinical Model Autophagy\"-a systemic degradation of diagnostic integrity that occurs when clinical artificial intelligence (AI) models are recursively trained on unverified, AI-generated synthetic data. As these recursive models may progressively regress toward statistical means, they undergo \"Interpretative Drift,\" a clinically concerning phenomenon where rare pathological variances are systematically erased and complex diseases are homogenized into benign averages. To prevent the irreversible contamination of health care data ecosystems, the author urgently proposes the Data Purity Standard (DPS). The DPS mandates the cryptographic watermarking of all AI-assisted clinical entries for provenance tracking, alongside the establishment of \"Human Vaults.\" These physically segregated repositories of physician-verified heritage data will serve as immutable biological anchors to safely guide future AI training, ensuring the long-term reliability of digital health infrastructure.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Electronic Health Records"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Diagnostic errors","Electronic Health Records","Data Governance","Large Language Models","Model Collapse"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/94813"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13146229"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13146229?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-22","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-21"},{"id":"41977066","source":"MED","pmid":"41977066","pmcid":"PMC13073410","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13073410"]},"doi":"10.3390/jcm15072767","title":"Artificial Intelligence in Head and Neck Surgical Oncology: A State-of-the-Art Review.","authorString":"Chen SX, Feucht M, Bhatt A, Farlow JL.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chen SX","firstName":"Steven X","lastName":"Chen","initials":"SX","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Feucht M","firstName":"Maria","lastName":"Feucht","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Bhatt A","firstName":"Aditya","lastName":"Bhatt","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-2111-3813"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Farlow JL","firstName":"Janice L","lastName":"Farlow","initials":"JL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1214-4960"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1214-4960"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-2111-3813"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4179668,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of clinical medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"J Clin Med","issn":"2077-0383","essn":"2077-0383","isoabbreviation":"J Clin Med","nlmid":"101606588"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"2767","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly reshaping head and neck surgical oncology by augmenting decision-making across the full perioperative continuum. This state-of-the-art review aims to provide head and neck surgical oncologists with a conceptual framework for understanding and critically appraising AI tools entering clinical practice, summarizing how machine learning, deep learning, and generative AI are being integrated into contemporary surgical workflows. Preoperative applications include detection of occult nodal metastasis and extranodal extension. Intraoperative innovations include augmented reality-assisted navigation, real-time margin assessment, and improving visual clarity and tissue handling for robotic platforms. Postoperatively, AI can predict complications like free flap failure and oncologic outcomes. Large language models are being operationalized for clinician-facing applications such as documentation and inbox support, as well as patient-facing education. Despite promising results, broad clinical deployment remains limited by concerns about privacy, validation, reliability, safety, and ethics. Widespread adoption will require prospective clinical trials, robust governance, and human-centered workflows that ensure AI remains a safe, assistive copilot.","affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Computer vision","Surgery","Oncology","Otorhinolaryngology","Head And Neck","Machine Learning","Augmented Reality","Deep Learning","Large Language Model"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm15072767"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13073410"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13073410?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-14","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-06","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-06"},{"id":"42061228","source":"MED","pmid":"42061228","pmcid":"PMC13132531","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13132531"]},"doi":"10.2196/94234","title":"Backcasting the Trust Gap: A Strategic Road Map for Clinician Adoption of AI Diagnostics by 2040.","authorString":"Yu Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Yu Y","firstName":"Yunguo","lastName":"Yu","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-2003-3813"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Zyter|TruCare, 2600 Tower Oaks Blvd, Suite 700, Rockville, MD, 20852, United States, 1 6177801330."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-2003-3813"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4166924,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","issn":"1439-4456","essn":"1438-8871","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e94234","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>The integration of artificial intelligence (AI) into clinical medicine presents a persistent paradox: diagnostic models routinely demonstrate benchmark superiority over human experts, yet bedside adoption remains fragile, and clinician trust is low. Conventional forecasting approaches-projecting model performance along optimistic trend lines-are epistemologically insufficient because they cannot account for the nonlinear sociotechnical transitions that separate technical capability from institutional trust. This Viewpoint applies backcasting, a normative futures methodology with a 4-decade evidence base in energy policy and public governance, to the specific challenge of clinician adoption of AI diagnostics, with the aim of identifying the structural interventions required to achieve durable trust by 2040. Consistent with the tradition of single-expert normative foresight analysis, we applied backcasting as a structured reasoning framework using a STEEP (social, technological, economic, environmental, and political) analysis. Sources from PubMed, IEEE Xplore, Google Scholar, and policy repositories (the US Food and Drug Administration, World Health Organization, Organisation for Economic Co-Operation and Development, and European Commission) published between 2010 and 2025 were reviewed; barriers and enablers were coded across STEEP dimensions to identify pivot points representing convergent, time-bound structural changes. Working backward from a defined 2040 vision state-a health care ecosystem with risk-stratified clinician trust thresholds, semantic transparency of AI outputs, integrated AI governance, and futures literacy in medical education-we identified three temporal pivot points: (1) the 2030 standardization of dual-process AI architectures, in which large language models are verified in real time by locally deployed small language models, producing a calibrated confidence score; (2) the 2035 institutionalization of agentic AI orchestration governed by a formally designated chief AI officer; and (3) the 2040 integration of futures literacy and human-AI teaming competencies into standard medical curricula. The AI trust gap is an institutional design problem, not a technical inevitability. Backcasting reframes the central question from \"when will AI be ready for medicine?\" to \"what must we build to make medicine ready for AI?\" The 3 pivot points identified here-verifiable AI by 2030, agentic governance by 2035, and futures literacy by 2040-are structural commitments that clinicians, health system leaders, and policymakers can begin building today.","affiliation":"Zyter|TruCare, 2600 Tower Oaks Blvd, Suite 700, Rockville, MD, 20852, United States, 1 6177801330.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Trust"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Health Policy","Clinical Decision Support","Implementation Science","Backcasting","Confidence Calibration","Trustworthy Ai","Futures Literacy","Medical Futures Studies","Trustworthy Artificial Intelligence","Small Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/94234"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13132531"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13132531?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-30","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-30","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-30"},{"id":"42172199","source":"MED","pmid":"42172199","pmcid":"PMC13196965","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13196965"]},"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0350135","title":"Audit data governance for disability-inclusive public services: A systematic review and integrative S-A-C framework.","authorString":"Chaiyasuk S, Rugchatjaroen K, Sirisunhirun S, Dhirathiti NS, Amornsiriphong S, Ploywan P.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chaiyasuk S","firstName":"Sitthisak","lastName":"Chaiyasuk","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-5303-550X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Audit Office of the Kingdom of Thailand, Bangkok, Thailand."}]}},{"fullName":"Rugchatjaroen K","firstName":"Krish","lastName":"Rugchatjaroen","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5136-8548"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Mahidol University, Nakhon Pathom, Thailand."}]}},{"fullName":"Sirisunhirun S","firstName":"Somboon","lastName":"Sirisunhirun","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Mahidol University, Nakhon Pathom, Thailand."}]}},{"fullName":"Dhirathiti NS","firstName":"Nopraenue Sajjarax","lastName":"Dhirathiti","initials":"NS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Mahidol University, Nakhon Pathom, Thailand."},{"affiliation":"International College, Mahidol University, Nakhon Pathom, Thailand."}]}},{"fullName":"Amornsiriphong S","firstName":"Somsak","lastName":"Amornsiriphong","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Mahidol University, Nakhon Pathom, Thailand."}]}},{"fullName":"Ploywan P","firstName":"Phut","lastName":"Ploywan","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Mahidol University, Nakhon Pathom, Thailand."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5136-8548"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-5303-550X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"21","journalIssueId":4187484,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"PloS one","medlineAbbreviation":"PLoS One","issn":"1932-6203","essn":"1932-6203","isoabbreviation":"PLoS One","nlmid":"101285081"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e0350135","abstractText":"This study consolidates interdisciplinary evidence on how audit data governance shapes accountability and service equity in data-intensive public services, with specific sensitivity to disability-inclusive delivery. Guided by the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) 2020, we conducted a protocol-driven systematic literature review of peer-reviewed studies and selected institutional and standards sources published between 2010 and 2025. We searched Scopus and complemented database retrieval with targeted searches of audit, digital government, accessibility, and data-governance portals. We applied a theory-led narrative synthesis to accommodate heterogeneity in designs, domains, and outcome reporting. Synthesizing 125 records, we find that governance effects are produced through three interacting dimensions-service delivery for people with disabilities (S), audit data governance (A), and data-driven culture (C)-that jointly condition transparency, contestability, and distributive impacts. Service delivery arrangements shape where data are generated, which encounters are recorded, and whether access needs, reasonable accommodations, and complaints become organizationally legible. Audit data governance conditions the credibility and usability of audit trails through provenance, metadata, retention, audit logging, and role-appropriate oversight access, shaping both oversight feasibility and privacy risk. Data-driven culture conditions how evidence is interpreted and acted upon, including data literacy, the standing of experiential evidence, and whether audit signals support learning or only compliance. Across the corpus, recurring tensions emerge between optimization and inclusion; transparency and privacy; standardization and reasonable accommodation; automated decisioning and meaningful contestability; and compliance-oriented auditing and learning-oriented governance. Drawing these mechanisms together, we propose an integrative framework linking S, A, and C to explain how auditability and disability inclusion are co-produced across the digital service lifecycle. The framework identifies leverage points for governance reform and evaluation and outlines implications for future empirical testing and for practitioners seeking trustworthy, oversight-ready, inclusive digital public services. A review protocol was prepared before the review; the review was not registered.","affiliation":"State Audit Office of the Kingdom of Thailand, Bangkok, Thailand.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Systematic Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Diversity, Equity, Inclusion"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Persons with Disabilities"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0350135"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13196965"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13196965?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-22","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-22"},{"id":"42294353","source":"MED","pmid":"42294353","pmcid":"PMC13261763","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13261763"]},"doi":"10.1093/nsr/nwag302","title":"Digital heart initiative: an ecosystem for digital discovery and precision medicine in cardiology.","authorString":"Ni H, Cao C, Zhou Y, Bai Y, Sheng J, Wang Y, Gu N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ni H","firstName":"Haibo","lastName":"Ni","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0083-4286"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical School, Nanjing University, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Cardiovascular Disease Center, Institute of Clinical Medicine, Jiangsu Key Laboratory for Cardiovascular Information and Health Engineering Medicine, Nanjing Drum Tower Hospital, Medical School, Nanjing University, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Cao C","firstName":"Chen","lastName":"Cao","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5343-808X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Biomedical Engineering and Informatics, Nanjing Medical University, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhou Y","firstName":"You","lastName":"Zhou","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5319-0934"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical School, Nanjing University, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Bai Y","firstName":"Yu","lastName":"Bai","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical School, Nanjing University, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Sheng J","firstName":"Jingyi","lastName":"Sheng","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Biological Science and Medical Engineering, Southeast University, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang Y","firstName":"Ying","lastName":"Wang","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Biological Science and Medical Engineering, Southeast University, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Gu N","firstName":"Ning","lastName":"Gu","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0047-337X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical School, Nanjing University, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Cardiovascular Disease Center, Institute of Clinical Medicine, Jiangsu Key Laboratory for Cardiovascular Information and Health Engineering Medicine, Nanjing Drum Tower Hospital, Medical School, Nanjing University, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5319-0934"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5343-808X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0047-337X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0083-4286"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"12","volume":"13","journalIssueId":4217789,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"National science review","medlineAbbreviation":"Natl Sci Rev","issn":"2095-5138","essn":"2053-714X","isoabbreviation":"Natl Sci Rev","nlmid":"101633095"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"nwag302","abstractText":"This paper proposes the development of a digital heart ecosystem comprising four interconnected micro-ecosystems: devices and data; digital tools; digital discovery and precision cardiology; and infrastructure and governance.","affiliation":"Medical School, Nanjing University, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["article-commentary","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwag302"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13261763"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13261763?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-25"},{"id":"41380485","source":"MED","pmid":"41380485","pmcid":"PMC12756637","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12756637"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.identj.2025.109322","title":"Artificial Intelligence in Dentistry: A Concise Review of Reporting Checklists and Guidelines.","authorString":"Khurshid Z, Osathanon T, Shire MA, Schwendicke F, Samaranayake L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Khurshid Z","firstName":"Zohaib","lastName":"Khurshid","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Prosthodontics and Dental Implantology, College of Dentistry, King Faisal University, Al-Ahsa, Saudi Arabia; Center of Artificial Intelligence and Innovation, Faculty of Dentistry, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok, Thailand."}]}},{"fullName":"Osathanon T","firstName":"Thanaphum","lastName":"Osathanon","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1649-6357"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center of Artificial Intelligence and Innovation, Faculty of Dentistry, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok, Thailand; Department of Anatomy, Centre of Excellence for Dental Stem Cell Biology, Faculty of Dentistry, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok, Thailand. Electronic address: thanaphum.o@chula.ac.th."}]}},{"fullName":"Shire MA","firstName":"Mohamedamin Abdullahi","lastName":"Shire","initials":"MA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science and Information Technology, NED University of Engineering and Technology, Karachi, Pakistan."}]}},{"fullName":"Schwendicke F","firstName":"Falk","lastName":"Schwendicke","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Conservative Dentistry and Periodontology, LMU University Hospital, LMU Munich, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Samaranayake L","firstName":"Lakshman","lastName":"Samaranayake","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center of Artificial Intelligence and Innovation, Faculty of Dentistry, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok, Thailand; Global Research Cell, Dr DY Patil Dental College and Hospital, Dr D Y Patil Vidyapeeth, Pimpri, Pune, 411018, India."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1649-6357"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"76","journalIssueId":4091606,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"International dental journal","medlineAbbreviation":"Int Dent J","issn":"0020-6539","essn":"1875-595X","isoabbreviation":"Int Dent J","nlmid":"0374714"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"109322","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) has become increasingly integrated into dental diagnostics, imaging and treatment planning. However, despite this growing adoption, adherence to standardised reporting frameworks remains inconsistent. Insufficient use of established checklists continues to impede reproducibility, transparency and regulatory credibility. This review systematically examines existing AI reporting frameworks relevant to dental research, mapping their methodological domains, areas of overlap and persistent implementation gaps. We analysed established medical reporting guidelines for artificial intelligence in healthcare covering trials, protocols, prediction models, bias assessment, decision-support systems, imaging and systematic reviews alongside dentistry-specific checklists and ethical frameworks. While dental AI research is expanding rapidly, its reporting remains fragmented and inconsistent. Existing frameworks provide a comprehensive foundation for transparency and methodological rigour, but are underutilised. Harmonising these frameworks and promoting active adherence through journal policies, regulatory integration and quantitative compliance tracking are essential to bridge the gap between algorithmic performance and trustworthy clinical adoption.","affiliation":"Department of Prosthodontics and Dental Implantology, College of Dentistry, King Faisal University, Al-Ahsa, Saudi Arabia; Center of Artificial Intelligence and Innovation, Faculty of Dentistry, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok, Thailand.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Chulalongkorn University","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Chulalongkorn University Faculty of Dentistry","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Dentistry","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Dental Research"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Checklist"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Reproducibility","Transparency","Dentistry","Clinical Validation","Reporting Checklist"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.identj.2025.109322"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12756637"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12756637?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":20,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-01-14","dateOfCreation":"2025-12-11","firstIndexDate":"2025-12-12","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-05","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-24","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-12-11","firstPublicationDate":"2025-12-11"},{"id":"42239922","source":"MED","pmid":"42239922","pmcid":"PMC13170471","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13170471"]},"doi":"10.15537/1658-3175.1002","title":"Artificial Intelligence in Radiology: Hidden Fragilities and the Path to Resilience.","authorString":"Alhasan MS.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Alhasan MS","firstName":"Mustafa S","lastName":"Alhasan","initials":"MS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-4401-5127"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine, College of Medicine, Taibah University, Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah, Kingdom of Saudi Arabia."},{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical Services, Teleradiology Solutions, 22 Llanfair Rd UNIT 6, Ardmore, PA 19003, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-4401-5127"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"47","journalIssueId":4206567,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"Saudi medical journal","medlineAbbreviation":"Saudi Med J","issn":"0379-5284","essn":"1658-3175","isoabbreviation":"Saudi Med J","nlmid":"7909441"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"195-218","abstractText":"Radiology artificial intelligence (AI) is advancing however its adoption faces fragile foundations that threaten sustainability. Despite bold promises of efficiency and accuracy, current deployment is undermined by weaknesses in economics, evidence, infrastructure, human factors, regulation, security, and environmental impact. Nearly 90% of radiology AI studies report process metrics rather than patient outcomes, while hidden costs elevate ownership to 400% to 500% of subscription fees. Technical fragilities include 25% or greater performance loss with routine protocol or scanner shifts, compounded by vendor consolidation that has eliminated 63% of companies since 2020, creating migration costs averaging 180,000 dollars per exit. Human factor challenges, including automation bias and progressive deskilling, intersect with regulatory requirements that mandate continuous evidence generation. Security risks and environmental costs remain underrecognized. This review introduces frameworks including risk assessment matrices, compliance guides, procurement checklists, evidence standards, lifecycle calculators, and implementation protocols to enable sustainable, patient centered, value driven integration.","affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine, College of Medicine, Taibah University, Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah, Kingdom of Saudi Arabia.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Radiology","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"EC","qualifierName":"economics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Segmentation","Imaging","Radiology","Machine Learning"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.15537/1658-3175.1002"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13170471"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13170471?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-08","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-06","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-04","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-07","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-07"},{"id":"41681707","source":"MED","pmid":"41681707","pmcid":"PMC12896711","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12896711"]},"doi":"10.3390/diagnostics16030390","title":"Large Language Models in Cardiovascular Prevention: A Narrative Review and Governance Framework.","authorString":"Ferreira Santos J, Dores H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ferreira Santos J","firstName":"José","lastName":"Ferreira Santos","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8690-3523"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Católica Medical School, Sintra Campus, Estrada Octávio Pato, 2635-631 Rio de Mouro, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Hospital da Luz Setubal, Luz Saúde, 2900-722 Setubal, Portugal."}]}},{"fullName":"Dores H","firstName":"Hélder","lastName":"Dores","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4736-5221"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Hospital da Luz Lisboa, Luz Saúde, 1600-209 Lisbon, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"Associate Laboratory REAL, Comprehensive Health Research Center (CHRC), 1099-085 Lisbon, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"NOVA Medical School, NOVA University Lisbon, 1069-061 Lisbon, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"CoLab Trials, 7000-811 Évora, Portugal."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8690-3523"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4736-5221"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4120552,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Diagnostics (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Diagnostics (Basel)","issn":"2075-4418","essn":"2075-4418","isoabbreviation":"Diagnostics (Basel)","nlmid":"101658402"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"390","abstractText":"<b>Background:</b> Large language models (LLMs) are becoming progressively integrated into clinical practice; however, their role in cardiovascular (CV) prevention remains unclear. This review synthesizes current evidence on LLM applications in preventive cardiology and proposes a governance framework for their safe translation into practice. <b>Methods:</b> We conducted a comprehensive narrative review of literature published between January 2015 and November 2025. Evidence was synthesized across three functional domains: (1) patient applications for health literacy and behavior change; (2) clinician applications for decision support and workflow efficiency; and (3) system applications for automated data extraction, registry construction, and quality surveillance. <b>Results:</b> Evidence suggests that while LLMs generate empathetic, guideline-concordant patient education, they lack the nuance required for unsupervised, personalized advice. For clinicians, LLMs effectively summarize clinical notes and draft documentation but remain unreliable for deterministic risk calculations and autonomous decision-making. System-facing applications demonstrate potential for automated phenotyping and multimodal risk prediction. However, safe deployment is constrained by hallucinations, temporal obsolescence, automation bias, and data privacy concerns. <b>Conclusions:</b> LLMs could help mitigate structural barriers in CV prevention but should presently be deployed only as supervised \"reasoning engines\" that augment, rather than replace, clinician judgment. To guide the transition from in silico performance to bedside practice, we propose the C.A.R.D.I.O. framework (Clinical validation, Auditability, Risk stratification, Data privacy, Integration, and Ongoing vigilance) as a roadmap for responsible integration.","affiliation":"Católica Medical School, Sintra Campus, Estrada Octávio Pato, 2635-631 Rio de Mouro, Portugal.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Risk stratification","Clinical Decision Support","Cardiovascular Prevention","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/diagnostics16030390"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12896711"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12896711?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"PPR1129723","source":"PPR","type":"Preprint in","note":"CrossRef Pre-print loader","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-13","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-02-16","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-26"},{"id":"42368301","source":"MED","pmid":"42368301","pmcid":"PMC13306875","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13306875"]},"doi":"10.35772/ghm.2026.01051","title":"Artificial intelligence (AI)-aided clinical data management: Applications, human-in-the-loop workflows, and regulatory considerations.","authorString":"Ohi S, Iwamoto T, Ikeda D, Kawanishi Y, Kitajima K, Ohyanagi H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ohi S","firstName":"Saya","lastName":"Ohi","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-8937-1279"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Bioinformatics, Department of Joint Center for Researchers, Associates and Clinicians (JCRAC), Center for Clinical Sciences, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Iwamoto T","firstName":"Tomoko","lastName":"Iwamoto","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Clinical Data Management, Department of Joint Center for Researchers, Associates and Clinicians (JCRAC), Center for Clinical Sciences, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Ikeda D","firstName":"Daiki","lastName":"Ikeda","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Bioinformatics, Department of Joint Center for Researchers, Associates and Clinicians (JCRAC), Center for Clinical Sciences, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kawanishi Y","firstName":"Yuichi","lastName":"Kawanishi","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Bioinformatics, Department of Joint Center for Researchers, Associates and Clinicians (JCRAC), Center for Clinical Sciences, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kitajima K","firstName":"Koji","lastName":"Kitajima","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Clinical Data Management, Department of Joint Center for Researchers, Associates and Clinicians (JCRAC), Center for Clinical Sciences, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Ohyanagi H","firstName":"Hajime","lastName":"Ohyanagi","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Joint Center for Researchers, Associates and Clinicians (JCRAC), Center for Clinical Sciences, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-8937-1279"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"8","journalIssueId":4222530,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Global health & medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"Glob Health Med","issn":"2434-9186","essn":"2434-9194","isoabbreviation":"Glob Health Med","nlmid":"101771579"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"182-192","abstractText":"Clinical data management (CDM) is central to the quality of clinical research. In Japan, CDM faces a shortage of qualified personnel, particularly in academic research organizations (AROs), as well as increasing data volume and complexity. Rapid advances in artificial intelligence (AI), especially large language models, have therefore attracted attention as a way to support CDM. This review summarizes domestic and international examples of AI utilization in CDM-related tasks, including data cleaning, medical coding, and query generation. Across the cases reviewed, a common implementation principle emerged: a human-in-the-loop design in which AI performs initial processing or detection, while final judgment remains with human personnel. This design is especially relevant to AROs, where high data quality must be maintained with limited CDM human resources. Regulatory frameworks, including ICH E6 (R3) and the FDA-EMA Guiding Principles, are beginning to address AI use, but how AI-aided processes should be handled under Good Clinical Practice remains under discussion. Comprehensive risk mitigation is therefore essential. AI and data are interdependent: better data improve AI performance, and better AI can further improve data quality. The shift from manual processes to human-AI collaborative workflows is likely to accelerate, and CDM must develop the technical, regulatory, and risk-management frameworks needed to support that transition.","affiliation":"Office of Bioinformatics, Department of Joint Center for Researchers, Associates and Clinicians (JCRAC), Center for Clinical Sciences, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial Intelligence (Ai)","Human-in-the-loop (Hitl)","Ai-related Regulations And Guidelines","Clinical Data Management (Cdm)"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.35772/ghm.2026.01051"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13306875"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13306875?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.35772/ghm.2026.01051"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},{"id":"42288564","source":"MED","pmid":"42288564","doi":"10.1038/s41598-026-55942-7","title":"AI‑driven framework for contract risk automation and compliance in oracle CPQ.","authorString":"Soma R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Soma R","firstName":"Rajesh","lastName":"Soma","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Independent Researcher, Cary, USA. rajeshsoma1207@gmail.com."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4202614,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Scientific reports","medlineAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","issn":"2045-2322","essn":"2045-2322","isoabbreviation":"Sci Rep","nlmid":"101563288"}},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Contract management and quoting processes are mission-critical operations in modern enterprises, yet they remain prone to inefficiencies, compliance risks, and human error-particularly in IT and SaaS sectors where contract complexity is high. This paper introduces the Artificial Intelligence Contract Risk and Intelligence Model (AICRIM), a simulation-validated, prototype-level framework that integrates generative AI (GPT-4) and BERT-based semantic matching into Oracle Configure-Price-Quote (CPQ) systems to perform real-time compliance and risk assessment during SaaS and cloud deal negotiations. AICRIM autonomously identifies anomalies in data privacy clauses, service-level agreements (SLAs), and pricing terms by interpreting unstructured contract text and benchmarking it against GDPR, HIPAA, and ISO 27,001 regulatory standards. The framework is evaluated through structured simulation experiments over a corpus of 500 synthetic SaaS contract documents, using manual legal review and rule-based NLP as baselines. Simulation experiments demonstrate a 27-32% reduction in contract errors, a 38.2% reduction in deal cycle time, and a compliance detection accuracy of 92.1%, with statistically significant improvements over both baselines (p < 0.01). These results are simulation-derived and serve as indicative upper-bound estimates pending real-world validation. AICRIM also embeds AI governance controls-AES-256 encryption, bias monitoring, and structured audit trails-to ensure ethical and auditable deployment. By extending Oracle CPQ via REST API event hooks, this model provides enterprises with a scalable, reproducible approach to contract risk automation.","affiliation":"Independent Researcher, Cary, USA. rajeshsoma1207@gmail.com.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Semantic Matching","Bert","Compliance Automation","Contract Intelligence","Contract Risk Assessment","Oracle Cpq","Saas Contracting","Generative Ai","Ai Governance"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-55942-7"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-16","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-13"},{"id":"42222849","source":"MED","pmid":"42222849","pmcid":"PMC13219290","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13219290"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1816692","title":"Recent advances in defending the privacy attacks of large language models for healthcare applications: a concise review.","authorString":"Aroua R, Kammoun I, Louati MA, Adjeroh DA, Duan T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Aroua R","firstName":"Rahma","lastName":"Aroua","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Allendale, MI, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Kammoun I","firstName":"Islem","lastName":"Kammoun","initials":"I","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Allendale, MI, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Louati MA","firstName":"Mahmoud Aziz","lastName":"Louati","initials":"MA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Allendale, MI, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Adjeroh DA","firstName":"Donald A","lastName":"Adjeroh","initials":"DA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Lane Department of Computer Science and Electrical Engineering, West Virginia University, Morgantown, WV, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Duan T","firstName":"Tiehang","lastName":"Duan","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Allendale, MI, United States."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","issn":"2624-8212","essn":"2624-8212","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1816692","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly adopted across healthcare applications, including clinical decision support and medical documentation systems. However, their deployment in medical settings raises significant privacy and security concerns due to the sensitivity of protected health information and stringent regulatory requirements. Recent studies have shown that LLM-based medical applications can cause medical data leakage through related API usages. This raises ethical concerns and threatens HIPAA (Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act) compliance, blocking the trustworthy deployment of LLMs in the medical domain. This review examines emerging privacy attacks and the related defense approaches in LLMs with a special focus on healthcare applications. We organize the attack and defense approaches based on Secure AI Framework (SAIF), systematically covering vulnerabilities across the data, model, application and infrastructure layers. We performed detailed analysis on major classes of privacy attacks and further examined state-of-the-art defense mechanisms under realistic healthcare application scenarios. A key finding of this review is the persistent privacy-utility tradeoff: stronger privacy protection often leads to substantial degradation in clinical performance, which may render models unsuitable for mission-critical medical tasks. The healthcare related deployment of LLMs needs to be evaluated against clinical utility thresholds rather than generic language modeling metrics. We identify open challenges in evaluation, system-level deployment and regulatory verification, and outline research directions that balance clinical utility with regulatory compliance.","affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Allendale, MI, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Healthcare Informatics","Differential Privacy","Hipaa Compliance","Trustworthy Ai","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1816692"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13219290"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13219290?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-15"},{"id":"42247409","source":"MED","pmid":"42247409","pmcid":"PMC13240874","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13240874"]},"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0350748","title":"The application of large language models in bariatric surgery: A scoping review.","authorString":"Guo N, Li X, Li X, Kang C, Gong X, Ji X, Zheng J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Guo N","firstName":"Ningjing","lastName":"Guo","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Shanxi Medical University, Shanxi, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li X","firstName":"Xuyan","lastName":"Li","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Shanxi Medical University, Shanxi, China."},{"affiliation":"Heping Hospital Affiliated to Changzhi Medical College, Shanxi, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li X","firstName":"Xiaoxue","lastName":"Li","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Shanxi Medical University, Shanxi, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Kang C","firstName":"Congmin","lastName":"Kang","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Shanxi Medical University, Shanxi, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Gong X","firstName":"Xiaoyan","lastName":"Gong","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Shanxi Medical University, Shanxi, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Ji X","firstName":"Xinyu","lastName":"Ji","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Shanxi Medical University, Shanxi, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zheng J","firstName":"Jie","lastName":"Zheng","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7332-4837"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Shanxi Medical University, Shanxi, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7332-4837"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"21","journalIssueId":4204536,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"PloS one","medlineAbbreviation":"PLoS One","issn":"1932-6203","essn":"1932-6203","isoabbreviation":"PLoS One","nlmid":"101285081"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e0350748","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Exploratory applications of large language models within the specialized field of metabolic and bariatric surgery have begun to emerge. Nevertheless, existing research remains fragmented, lacking comprehensive integration.<h4>Objective</h4>To conduct a scoping review of studies on the application of large language models in the field of metabolic and bariatric surgery, aiming to provide a reference for clinical practice and future research.<h4>Methods</h4>This scoping review adhered to the Joanna Briggs Institute methodological framework and followed the preferred reporting items for systematic reviews and meta-Analyses extension for scoping reviews (PRISMA-ScR) guidelines.PubMed, Web of Science, The Cochrane Library, Embase, CINAHL, CNKI, Wanfang, and VIP databases were searched for relevant studies, with the search timeframe from database inception to November 2025. The included literature was summarized and analyzed.<h4>Results</h4>A total of 21 English-language studies were included. LLMs were primarily applied in scenarios such as patient education and information consultation, clinical decision support, and professional knowledge assessment. While LLMs performed well in information-provision tasks, they showed low consistency with expert opinions in complex clinical tasks such as individualized surgical recommendations. Performance varied across different models, with GPT-4 generally demonstrating superior performance, and domain-specific models showing professional potential. Current research still faces challenges regarding information accuracy, readability, and clinical applicability.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Large language models hold auxiliary potential in the field of metabolic and bariatric surgery, particularly for knowledge dissemination and patient education. However, their reliability in complex clinical decision-making remains limited. Future efforts should focus on conducting high-quality studies, advancing model specialization and standardized evaluation, and exploring safe and effective human-AI collaboration models.","affiliation":"School of Nursing, Shanxi Medical University, Shanxi, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Scoping Review","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Bariatric Surgery","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0350748"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13240874"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13240874?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-06","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-06","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-07","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-05"},{"id":"42453683","source":"MED","pmid":"42453683","doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1849190","title":"From &lt;b&gt;\"&lt;/b&gt;assistant\" to &lt;b&gt;\"&lt;/b&gt;autonomous\": legal liability and ethical traceability frameworks for generative AI in clinical misdiagnosis scenarios, with a special focus on paediatrics.","authorString":"Xu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Xu S","firstName":"Shiyi","lastName":"Xu","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Capital Medical University Second Clinical School, Capital Medical University, Beijing, China."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","issn":"2673-253X","essn":"2673-253X","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1849190","abstractText":"The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into clinical workflows has blurred the boundary between passive decision-support tools and systems capable of autonomous diagnostic reasoning. Current regulatory instruments-including the US FDA Clinical Decision Support Software (CDSS) guidance and the EU Artificial Intelligence Act (Regulation EU 2024/1689)-classify these systems primarily as \"decision support,\" yet the non-deterministic outputs and emergent reasoning of generative AI introduce liability complexities these frameworks were not designed to address. This hypothesis and theory article examines the regulatory gap surrounding LLM-associated clinical errors. Drawing on regulatory precedents, case law, and a representative scenario of polypharmacy mismanagement, we analyze the risks of automation bias and the structural inadequacy of the Learned Intermediary Doctrine when applied to generative AI. We advance the autonomy-liability correspondence hypothesis: legal liability should be allocated as a monotonic function of a system's structural autonomy from meaningful human review. From this we derive a \"Three-Tiered Liability Escalation Framework\" (T-LEF) that allocates accountability among AI developers, healthcare institutions, and clinicians according to measurable degrees of autonomy. Arguing that traditional Explainable AI is technically insufficient for foundation models, we propose Clinical Algorithmic Audit Trails (CAAT)-a cryptographically secured, privacy-preserving traceability infrastructure-as a complement to explainability, uncertainty estimation, human-factors evaluation, and post-market surveillance, alongside a structured Safe Harbor provision to support compliant innovation. Because paediatric patients-from neonates to adolescents-constitute a population in which these failure modes are both more probable and more consequential, we give paediatric care explicit focus: under-representation of paediatric data in training corpora, weight- and development-based dosing across narrow therapeutic windows, and children's inability to detect or contest erroneous recommendations jointly intensify these concerns, and a dedicated section examines how the T-LEF, the Clinically Deceptive Hallucination (CDH) evidentiary test, and CAAT apply to paediatric deployment. We acknowledge that the T-LEF and CAAT remain prospective proposals requiring empirical validation, stakeholder consultation, and refinement before adoption. This framework is offered as a structured contribution to regulatory deliberation, not a finalized implementation blueprint.","affiliation":"Capital Medical University Second Clinical School, Capital Medical University, Beijing, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Medical Liability","Paediatrics","Health Policy","Clinical Decision Support","Artificial Intelligence Ethics","Generative Ai","Paediatric Patient Safety"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1849190"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-16","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-30","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-30"},{"id":"41901113","source":"MED","pmid":"41901113","pmcid":"PMC13029413","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13029413"]},"doi":"10.3390/nu18060938","title":"Generative AI in Precision Nutrition: A Review of Current Developments and Future Directions.","authorString":"Abdur Rahman L, Dedousis V, Papathanail I, Poursoleymani R, Kafyra M, Kalafati IP, Mougiakakou SG.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Abdur Rahman L","firstName":"Lubnaa","lastName":"Abdur Rahman","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7271-2814"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School for Cellular and Biomedical Sciences, University of Bern, 3012 Bern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"ARTORG Center for Biomedical Engineering Research, University of Bern, 3008 Bern, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Dedousis V","firstName":"Vasileios","lastName":"Dedousis","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-1439-9564"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School for Cellular and Biomedical Sciences, University of Bern, 3012 Bern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"ARTORG Center for Biomedical Engineering Research, University of Bern, 3008 Bern, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Papathanail I","firstName":"Ioannis","lastName":"Papathanail","initials":"I","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7767-3831"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"ARTORG Center for Biomedical Engineering Research, University of Bern, 3008 Bern, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Poursoleymani R","firstName":"Rooholla","lastName":"Poursoleymani","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-4365-6782"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School for Cellular and Biomedical Sciences, University of Bern, 3012 Bern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"ARTORG Center for Biomedical Engineering Research, University of Bern, 3008 Bern, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Kafyra M","firstName":"Maria","lastName":"Kafyra","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-9665-0829"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nutrition and Dietetics, School of Health Science and Education, Harokopio University of Athens, 17676 Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Kalafati IP","firstName":"Ioanna Panagiota","lastName":"Kalafati","initials":"IP","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5572-7491"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nutrition and Dietetics, School of Health Science and Education, Harokopio University of Athens, 17676 Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Mougiakakou SG","firstName":"Stavroula Georgia","lastName":"Mougiakakou","initials":"SG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6355-9982"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"ARTORG Center for Biomedical Engineering Research, University of Bern, 3008 Bern, Switzerland."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5572-7491"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7271-2814"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6355-9982"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7767-3831"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-1439-9564"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-4365-6782"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-9665-0829"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4161783,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Nutrients","medlineAbbreviation":"Nutrients","issn":"2072-6643","essn":"2072-6643","isoabbreviation":"Nutrients","nlmid":"101521595"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"938","abstractText":"<b>Background:</b> Precision nutrition (PN) aims to personalize dietary guidance by accounting for inter-individual variability across biological, metabolic, lifestyle, and environmental factors influencing nutritional needs and health outcomes. While traditional Artificial Intelligence (AI) has advanced nutritional research through systems like automated dietary assessment, these models often operate rigidly. Generative AI (GenAI) introduces the capacity for adaptive interventions for enhanced PN. However, the scope and maturity of its applications remain insufficiently characterized. <b>Objective:</b> This review examined original works applying GenAI in PN, focusing on application, methodology, and limitations. <b>Methods:</b> A systematic search was conducted in PubMed, ACM Digital Library, and Scopus. Inclusion criteria focused on original works deploying GenAI models in PN contexts. Included works were further formally assessed based on data used, validation, transparency, bias, and security and privacy. <b>Results:</b> 21 eligible studies were identified, all published after 2024. The literature indicated a surge in large language model-based systems for personalized dietary recommendations, followed by applications in data foundation building and food effect understanding. A recurrent limitation was questionable evaluation on synthetic data and hallucinations, necessitating a human-expert-in-the-loop, especially in high-stakes clinical settings. Additionally, only 4 of 21 reviewed studies incorporated biological content or biological inputs, and fewer approached biologically grounded PN within implemented personalization workflows using metabolic and/or genomic variables. <b>Conclusions:</b> Although GenAI research in PN is expanding rapidly, most applications remain personalized at a user-preference level rather than including biological determinants. The need for standardized reporting, stronger genome-informed modeling, and consistent human-in-the-loop validation protocols is further highlighted to advance towards holistic PN.","affiliation":"Graduate School for Cellular and Biomedical Sciences, University of Bern, 3012 Bern, Switzerland.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"101080117","agency":"European Commission and the Swiss Confederation–State Secretariat for Education, Research and Innovation","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"101080117","agency":"European Commission and State Secretariat for Education, Research and Innovation (SERI)","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"10093560","agency":"UK Research and Innovation (UKRI) under the UK government’s Horizon Europe funding guarantee","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"101057730","agency":"European Commission and State Secretariat for Education, Research and Innovation (SERI)","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"101057730","agency":"European Commission and the Swiss Confederation–State Secretariat for Education, Research and Innovation","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"10106435","agency":"UK Research and Innovation (UKRI) under the UK government’s Horizon Europe funding guarantee","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Precision Medicine","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Personalized Nutrition","Precision Nutrition","Generative Artificial Intelligence"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/nu18060938"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13029413"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13029413?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":3,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-13","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-29","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-17"},{"id":"42440000","source":"MED","pmid":"42440000","doi":"10.1007/s40290-026-00621-1","title":"Medical Affairs in the Age of Complexity: From Scientific Support to Strategic Enterprise Integrator.","authorString":"Arvani M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Arvani M","firstName":"Mussa","lastName":"Arvani","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-2808-7522"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"MAestro Medical Solutions, Cologne, Germany. connect@maestroms.net."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-2808-7522"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4226453,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","journal":{"title":"Pharmaceutical medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"Pharmaceut Med","issn":"1178-2595","essn":"1179-1993","isoabbreviation":"Pharmaceut Med","nlmid":"101471195"}},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Regulatory acceleration, expanded reliance on real-world evidence (RWE), and increasing integration of artificial intelligence (AI) into healthcare systems have substantially reshaped expectations placed on Medical Affairs. Accelerated and conditional approval pathways are progressively shifting confirmatory evidence generation into the post-marketing phase, while regulatory agencies have formalized frameworks for incorporating RWE into benefit-risk evaluation and lifecycle decision making. In parallel, healthcare systems are increasingly conceptualized as learning health systems in which routine-care data inform iterative improvement. This Leading Article proposes a conceptual but operationally applicable framework that positions Medical Affairs as a Strategic Enterprise Integrator, responsible for coordinating lifecycle evidence architecture, scientific exchange, insight translation, and governance across development and real-world settings. The framework is structured around four interdependent capability domains: lifecycle evidence architecture; scientific translation and insight management; cross-functional governance; and AI/data governance, including oversight of large language model (LLM)-supported workflows in medical information and analytics. The proposed model explicitly delineates interfaces with Clinical Development, Pharmacovigilance, Market Access/Health Economics and Outcomes Research, and Commercial functions to reduce redundancy while preserving functional accountability. Methodological considerations for RWE are anchored in principles of fit-for-purpose data, study design discipline, and transparent interpretation, while AI-enabled processes are addressed through a scientific governance lens emphasizing validation, auditability, and context-of-use. Illustrative operational examples and a staged maturity model with measurable indicators are introduced to support practical implementation. The framework positions Medical Affairs as a scientifically accountable integrator of lifecycle evidence within evolving regulatory science. As a conceptual model this framework requires further empirical validation to assess its applicability and generalizability across organizational contexts.","affiliation":"MAestro Medical Solutions, Cologne, Germany. connect@maestroms.net.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s40290-026-00621-1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-13"},{"id":"41930309","source":"MED","pmid":"41930309","pmcid":"PMC13040884","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13040884"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.esmorw.2025.100679","title":"How to bring generative AI to oncology practice.","authorString":"Truhn D, Kather JN.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Truhn D","firstName":"D","lastName":"Truhn","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Diagnostic and Interventional Radiology, University Hospital Aachen, Aachen, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Lab for AI in Medicine, University Hospital Aachen, Aachen, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Kather JN","firstName":"J N","lastName":"Kather","initials":"JN","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3730-5348"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Else Kroener Fresenius Center for Digital Health, Faculty of Medicine, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine I, Faculty of Medicine, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Medical Oncology, National Center for Tumor Diseases (NCT), University Hospital Heidelberg, Heidelberg, Germany."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3730-5348"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"11","journalIssueId":4133860,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"ESMO real world data and digital oncology","medlineAbbreviation":"ESMO Real World Data Digit Oncol","issn":"2949-8201","essn":"2949-8201","isoabbreviation":"ESMO Real World Data Digit Oncol","nlmid":"9919053637506676"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"100679","abstractText":"Generative artificial intelligence (AI) is entering oncology. Large language models are the near-term workhorse because oncology runs on narrative text and structured tables. We review current adoption and outline a practical path from stand-alone chat models to retrieval-augmented systems and, ultimately, agentic assistants that plan tasks, call domain tools, and integrate multimodal data within the electronic health record. Concrete uses include molecular tumor board synthesis with transparent evidence, grading along guidelines, synoptic radiology and pathology drafting, and computable trial matching. We also map the constraints: fragmented hospital information technology, privacy and provenance requirements, domain shift across sites, and persistent hallucinations. We envision that evaluation must move beyond leaderboards toward multicenter, prospective designs with endpoints that reflect clinical utility, such as faithfulness to cited sources, extraction accuracy, time to task completion, plan correctness, recovery after tool failure, and silent clinical studies before exposure. Finally, we sketch an adoption trajectory. Institutions will replace <i>ad hoc</i> use of public tools with sanctioned drafting assistants, then embed retrieval and calculators inside the record, and only later enable event-driven agents that propose context-aware actions. The destination is augmentation, not automation: a learning assistant that shows its work, improves routine care, and leaves clinical judgment with clinicians.","affiliation":"Diagnostic and Interventional Radiology, University Hospital Aachen, Aachen, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"NIHR203331","agency":"National Institute for Health Research (NIHR)","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"101114631","agency":"European Research Council","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Oncology","Generative Ai","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.esmorw.2025.100679"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13040884"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13040884?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-03","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-03","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-30","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-30"},{"id":"41818744","source":"MED","pmid":"41818744","pmcid":"PMC13022535","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13022535"]},"doi":"10.2196/87830","title":"Trustworthy AI-Augmented Objective Structured Clinical Examinations in Nursing Education: Taiwan-Japan Viewpoint on 5 AI Roles, Governance, and Cross-Border Implementation.","authorString":"Kubota K, Nishimura A, Seto R, Li L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kubota K","firstName":"Kazumi","lastName":"Kubota","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2270-2313"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Research Organization, Shimonoseki City University, Shimonoseki, Yamaguchi, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Healthcare Information Management, University of Tokyo Hospital, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Nishimura A","firstName":"Ayako","lastName":"Nishimura","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6981-1667"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Healthcare, Division of Nursing, Tokyo Healthcare University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Seto R","firstName":"Ryoma","lastName":"Seto","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6154-9342"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Healthcare, Division of Healthcare Informatics, Tokyo Healthcare University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Li L","firstName":"Liu","lastName":"Li","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4800-644X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Show Chwan Health Care System, Taipei, Taiwan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4800-644X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6154-9342"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6981-1667"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2270-2313"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"10","journalIssueId":4136778,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR formative research","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","issn":"2561-326X","essn":"2561-326X","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","nlmid":"101726394"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e87830","abstractText":"Generative artificial intelligence (AI) is arriving in high-stakes assessment; however, governance, validity evidence, and faculty readiness remain uneven. From a Taiwan-Japan perspective, we outline a pragmatic, transferable approach to integrating AI into nursing objective structured clinical examinations (OSCEs) using a 5-AI-role model-learning assistant, AI‑augmented standardized patient, assessment assistant, case generator, and learning analyst-mapped across pre-OSCE, peri-OSCE, and post-OSCE workflows with human-in-the-loop final judgment. Taiwan contributes agile interdisciplinary development, staged pilots (practice, mock OSCE, and limited high-stakes stations), A/B comparisons, and explainability-by-design logging that links scores to time-stamped evidence. Japan contributes robust policy scaffolding (national AI use guidance in K-12, a revised nursing model core curriculum with outcomes and assessment blueprints, and institutional research cultures that support auditability and quality assurance). We distill 4 cross-cutting governance pillars-human oversight, learning process transparency, ethics and safety, and traceability-into implementable techniques (machine-readable rubrics, standardized patient persona cards, bias monitoring, and targeted faculty development). Aligning with international principles (International Advisory Committee for AI; Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development; United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization; World Health Organization; European Commission's High Level Expert Group; and National Institute of Standards and Technology), we propose a joint road map and shared registry to benchmark reliability, validity, equity, and workload impact. This viewpoint targets OSCE directors, nursing educators, and institutional leaders and provides a phase-gated governance blueprint rather than reporting original trial outcomes. Taiwan-led agility, complemented by Japan's standards-driven assurance, can form an Asia-Pacific reference model for trustworthy AI‑augmented OSCE in nursing education.","affiliation":"Research Organization, Shimonoseki City University, Shimonoseki, Yamaguchi, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Education, Nursing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Educational Measurement","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Japan"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Taiwan"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","AI","Nursing Education","Standardized Patient","Osce","Objective Structured Clinical Examination","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Generative Ai","Taiwan-japan Collaboration","Assessment Governance","Transparency And Traceability"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/87830"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13022535"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13022535?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-11","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-12"},{"id":"42289049","source":"MED","pmid":"42289049","pmcid":"PMC13265052","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13265052"]},"doi":"10.1093/bib/bbag312","title":"Reflections on the use of LLMs for cell annotation.","authorString":"Ray PP.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ray PP","firstName":"Partha Pratim","lastName":"Ray","initials":"PP","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2306-2792"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Applications, Sikkim University, PO Tadong, Gangtok, Sikkim 737102, India."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2306-2792"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"27","journalIssueId":4189139,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Briefings in bioinformatics","medlineAbbreviation":"Brief Bioinform","issn":"1467-5463","essn":"1477-4054","isoabbreviation":"Brief Bioinform","nlmid":"100912837"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"bbag312","abstractText":"This letter comments on the recently published AICellType platform for large language model (LLM)-based cell type annotation in single-cell and spatial transcriptomics. While appreciating the authors' systematic benchmarking and practical contribution, concerns are raised regarding the continued dependence on proprietary commercial LLMs such as Claude 3.5 Sonnet for biomedical annotation. Greater emphasis is suggested on open-source biomedical LLMs, multimodal vision-language models, local deployment, reproducibility, privacy preservation, and regulatory compliance to ensure more transparent, reliable, and sustainable medical annotation systems for translational bioinformatics.","affiliation":"Department of Computer Applications, Sikkim University, PO Tadong, Gangtok, Sikkim 737102, India.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Letter"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Computational Biology","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Software"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Single-Cell Analysis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Spatial Transcriptomics"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Reproducibility","Single-cell Transcriptomics","Cell Type Annotation","Biomedical Large Language Models","Open-source Medical Llms"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/bib/bbag312"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13265052"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13265052?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-01"},{"id":"41799484","source":"MED","pmid":"41799484","pmcid":"PMC12963277","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12963277"]},"doi":"10.3389/fpubh.2026.1769451","title":"Construction of a hospital intelligent integrity supervision platform: digital and intelligent practices for enhancing ethical governance in healthcare.","authorString":"Chen X.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chen X","firstName":"Xiaofei","lastName":"Chen","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Discipline Inspection and Supervision Office, Hangzhou Xixi Hospital, Hangzhou, Zhejiang, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4110762,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in public health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Public Health","issn":"2296-2565","essn":"2296-2565","isoabbreviation":"Front Public Health","nlmid":"101616579"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1769451","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>The digital transformation of governance in public hospitals is crucial for enhancing integrity and compliance. This study investigates the design, implementation, and impact of an Intelligent Integrity Supervision Platform in a hospital setting, aiming to provide a replicable model for data-driven ethical governance.<h4>Methods</h4>A case study approach was employed at Hangzhou Xixi Hospital, combining system analysis with outcome evaluation. The platform integrated multi-source data and established real-time early-warning mechanisms. Quantitative data on integrity incidents, medical disputes, and patient satisfaction were collected from quarterly reports (2024-2025) and analyzed to compare pre- and post-implementation periods.<h4>Results</h4>Following the platform's launch, integrity risk incidents decreased stepwise, achieving a 100% reduction (zero incidents) in the second half of 2025 compared to the baseline. Medical disputes fell by 75% year-on-year. Patient satisfaction metrics improved, including an increase in the platform complaint resolution rate from 95.1% to 97.3% and a 19.7% decline in discharge follow-up issue rates.<h4>Discussion</h4>The platform demonstrates the efficacy of digital-intelligent systems in transforming ethical governance by enabling proactive risk prevention, standardizing operations, and fostering accountability. It aligns with global efforts to leverage technology for anti-corruption in healthcare. The study concludes that such platforms serve as strategic tools for systemic governance reform, contributing to both institutional integrity and patient safety.","affiliation":"Discipline Inspection and Supervision Office, Hangzhou Xixi Hospital, Hangzhou, Zhejiang, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Hospitals, Public","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"OG","qualifierName":"organization & administration","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ES","qualifierName":"ethics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Risk Management"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Patient Satisfaction"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"China"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Clinical Governance"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Intelligent Systems"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Data integration","Smart Hospital","Digital Governance","Intelligent Supervision Platform","Compliance Risk Management","Ethical Healthcare","Healthcare Integrity"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2026.1769451"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12963277"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12963277?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-10","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-20"},{"id":"42014474","source":"MED","pmid":"42014474","pmcid":"PMC13287483","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13287483"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41746-026-02508-6","title":"Tracing the Pen: Electronic Health Records Amid the Rise of Generative AI.","authorString":"Nargesi AA, You JG, Bitterman DS, Succi MD, Mishuris RG, Poon EG, Landman AB.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Nargesi AA","firstName":"Arash A","lastName":"Nargesi","initials":"AA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Cardiovascular Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Clinical Informatics, Mass General Brigham, Somerville, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Broad Institute of MIT and Harvard, Cambridge, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"You JG","firstName":"Jacqueline G","lastName":"You","initials":"JG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of General Internal Medicine and Primary Care, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Digital, Mass General Brigham, Somerville, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Bitterman DS","firstName":"Danielle S","lastName":"Bitterman","initials":"DS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Digital, Mass General Brigham, Somerville, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"AI in Medicine Program, Mass General Brigham, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Succi MD","firstName":"Marc D","lastName":"Succi","initials":"MD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Innovation, Mass General Brigham, Somerville, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Medically Engineered Solutions in Healthcare Incubator, Innovation in Operations Research Center (MESH IO), Massachusetts General Hospital, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Mishuris RG","firstName":"Rebecca G","lastName":"Mishuris","initials":"RG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of General Internal Medicine and Primary Care, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Digital, Mass General Brigham, Somerville, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Poon EG","firstName":"Eric G","lastName":"Poon","initials":"EG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, NC, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biostatistics & Bioinformatics, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, NC, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Landman AB","firstName":"Adam B","lastName":"Landman","initials":"AB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Brown University Health, Providence, RI, USA. Adam.landman@brownhealth.org."},{"affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Brown University, Providence, RI, USA. Adam.landman@brownhealth.org."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4171893,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","issn":"2398-6352","essn":"2398-6352","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"482","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) are poised to transform physician interactions with electronic health records (EHRs) by assisting clinical documentation, drafting preliminary diagnostic reports, and supporting patient communication. While LLMs reduce administrative burden, their integration in clinical workflows introduces the risk of blending AI- and human-generated content within EHRs. This perspective reviews technological and policy solutions to ensure traceability of AI-generated content in EHRs to preserve clinical integrity.","affiliation":"Division of Cardiovascular Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Boston, MA, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"T15LM007092","agency":"National Library of Medicine/National Institutes of Health","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"T32HL007604","agency":"National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI) of the National Institutes of Health","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"T32HL007604","agency":"National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI) of the National Institutes of Health (NIH)","orderIn":0},{"agency":"the American Cancer Society and American Society for Radiation Oncology","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Woods Foundation","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institutes of Health National Cancer Institute","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-026-02508-6"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13287483"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13287483?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-22","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-21"},{"id":"42194487","source":"MED","pmid":"42194487","pmcid":"PMC13205439","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13205439"]},"doi":"10.3390/healthcare14101395","title":"Safety Mechanisms and Risk Mitigation in Generative AI Mental Health Chatbots: A Systematic Scoping Review.","authorString":"Olisaeloka L, Richardson CG, Wang AY, Munthali RJ, Vigo DV.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Olisaeloka L","firstName":"Lotenna","lastName":"Olisaeloka","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9131-0036"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."},{"affiliation":"School of Population and Public Health, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 1Z8, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Richardson CG","firstName":"Chris G","lastName":"Richardson","initials":"CG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."},{"affiliation":"School of Population and Public Health, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 1Z8, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang AY","firstName":"Angel Y","lastName":"Wang","initials":"AY","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2001-2220"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Munthali RJ","firstName":"Richard J","lastName":"Munthali","initials":"RJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8492-7409"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Vigo DV","firstName":"Daniel V","lastName":"Vigo","initials":"DV","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."},{"affiliation":"School of Population and Public Health, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 1Z8, Canada."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8492-7409"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9131-0036"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2001-2220"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4201779,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Healthcare (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","issn":"2227-9032","essn":"2227-9032","isoabbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","nlmid":"101666525"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1395","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Generative AI (GenAI) mental health chatbots are increasingly being developed to help address persistent barriers to mental healthcare. Unlike earlier rule-based and retrieval-based systems, GenAI chatbots generate open-ended outputs that can be inaccurate and unsafe. Documented harms from general-purpose GenAI chatbots have highlighted the need for purpose-built interventions with dedicated safeguards, yet how safety is implemented in such interventions remains poorly understood.<h4>Methods</h4>This scoping review followed the Joanna Briggs Institute methodology and PRISMA-ScR guidelines, with a prospectively registered and peer-reviewed protocol. A systematic search of seven academic databases and search engines including MEDLINE, Scopus, PsycINFO, ACM Digital Library, IEEE Xplore, Google Scholar and Consensus was conducted in July 2025. Two reviewers independently screened records and extracted data. Safety mechanisms and risk mitigation strategies were narratively synthesised across three pre-specified domains: technical safeguards, pre-deployment safety considerations, and delivery-phase risk mitigation strategies.<h4>Results</h4>Twenty-one studies across 11 countries were included. Most interventions incorporated at least one technical safety mechanism, most commonly fine-tuning and prompt engineering. A smaller subset implemented layered safety architectures combining retrieval systems, content filters or risk classifiers, and rule-based algorithms. Pre-deployment safeguards included clinical expert and user co-design approaches, research ethics procedures, and data privacy measures. During intervention delivery, detailed onboarding with role clarification was common, but human oversight was limited. Crisis referral protocols varied in rigour but were mostly underdeveloped, and systematic adverse event monitoring was sparse. Documented safety failures included missed suicidal ideation and provision of inaccurate clinical information.<h4>Conclusions</h4>GenAI chatbot interventions require a robust sociotechnical approach that integrates technical safeguards with user co-design, procedural controls, and human oversight. Future research is needed to evaluate efficacy, improve safeguards and standardise safety outcome measurement. Regulatory oversight proportional to the risks these systems carry is required to enable integration into stepped or blended mental healthcare.","affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Mental Health Chatbots","Ai Safety","Generative Ai","Chatgpt","Ai Chatbots"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare14101395"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13205439"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13205439?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"PPR1174289","source":"PPR","type":"Preprint in","note":"Link created based on a title-first author match","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-20"},{"id":"41019285","source":"MED","pmid":"41019285","pmcid":"PMC12460403","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12460403"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2025.1653631","title":"A systematic review of ethical considerations of large language models in healthcare and medicine.","authorString":"Fareed M, Fatima M, Uddin J, Ahmed A, Sattar MA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Fareed M","firstName":"Muhammad","lastName":"Fareed","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Riphah School of Computing & Innovation, Riphah International University, Islamabad, Pakistan."}]}},{"fullName":"Fatima M","firstName":"Madeeha","lastName":"Fatima","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Riphah School of Computing & Innovation, Riphah International University, Islamabad, Pakistan."}]}},{"fullName":"Uddin J","firstName":"Jamal","lastName":"Uddin","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Riphah School of Computing & Innovation, Riphah International University, Islamabad, Pakistan."}]}},{"fullName":"Ahmed A","firstName":"Adeel","lastName":"Ahmed","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Riphah School of Computing & Innovation, Riphah International University, Islamabad, Pakistan."}]}},{"fullName":"Sattar MA","firstName":"Muhammad Awais","lastName":"Sattar","initials":"MA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2431-8182"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, Electrical and Space Engineering, Luleå University of Technology, Luleå, Sweden."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2431-8182"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"7","journalIssueId":3897144,"dateOfPublication":"2025 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","issn":"2673-253X","essn":"2673-253X","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"1653631","abstractText":"The rapid integration of large language models (LLMs) into healthcare offers significant potential for improving diagnosis, treatment planning, and patient engagement. However, it also presents serious ethical challenges that remain incompletely addressed. In this review, we analyzed 27 peer-reviewed studies published between 2017 and 2025 across four major open-access databases using strict eligibility criteria, robust synthesis methods, and established guidelines to explicitly examine the ethical aspects of deploying LLMs in clinical settings. We explore four key aspects, including the main ethical issues arising from the use of LLMs in healthcare, the prevalent model architectures employed in ethical analyses, the healthcare application domains that are most frequently scrutinized, and the publication and bibliographic patterns characterizing this literature. Our synthesis reveals that bias and fairness ( n=7 , 25.9%) are the most frequently discussed concerns, followed by safety, reliability, transparency, accountability, and privacy, and that the GPT family predominates ( n=14 , 51.8%) among examined models. While privacy protection and bias mitigation received notable attention in the literature, no existing review has systematically addressed the comprehensive ethical issues surrounding LLMs. Most previous studies focus narrowly on specific clinical subdomains and lack a comprehensive methodology. As a systematic mapping of open-access literature, this synthesis identifies dominant ethical patterns, but it is not exhaustive of all ethical work on LLMs in healthcare. We also synthesize identified challenges, outline future research directions and include a provisional ethical integration framework to guide clinicians, developers, and policymakers in the responsible integration of LLMs into clinical workflows.","affiliation":"Riphah School of Computing & Innovation, Riphah International University, Islamabad, Pakistan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","systematic-review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Bias","Privacy","Fairness","Deep Learning","Bioethical Issues","Artificial Intelligence (Ai)","Chatgpt","Large Language Models (Llms)"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2025.1653631"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12460403"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12460403?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":12,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2025-09-29","dateOfCreation":"2025-09-29","firstIndexDate":"2025-09-30","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-09-27","dateOfRevision":"2025-10-01","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-09-11","firstPublicationDate":"2025-09-11"},{"id":"41994795","source":"MED","pmid":"41994795","pmcid":"PMC13080857","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13080857"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.105050","title":"The Role of Generative Artificial Intelligence in the Diagnosis and Treatment of Malocclusion.","authorString":"Alsayed NA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Alsayed NA","firstName":"Najah A","lastName":"Alsayed","initials":"NA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Dentistry, Qatar University, Doha, QAT."},{"affiliation":"Dentistry, Hamad Medical Corporation, Doha, QAT."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4139964,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","issn":"2168-8184","essn":"2168-8184","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e105050","abstractText":"Background Malocclusion is a prevalent dental disorder characterized by the improper alignment of the teeth and jaws, which adversely affects oral function and aesthetics. Traditional diagnosis and treatment planning procedures are time-consuming and rely heavily on expert evaluation. Recent developments in generative artificial intelligence (AI) offer promising tools to enhance diagnostic accuracy and to optimize treatment strategies in orthodontics. Methods This study developed a hybrid artificial AI model combining convolutional neural networks (CNNs), generative adversarial Networks (GANs), and large language models (LLMs), specifically, large language model Meta AI (LLaMA), to analyze orthodontic images and predict malocclusion types. A dataset comprising 150 anonymized, expert-labeled lateral cephalometric images from Hamad Dental Center in Doha was divided into training and testing sets using an 80:20 split. Data preprocessing, augmentation, and feature extraction were employed to improve model robustness. The model's performance was evaluated through classification accuracy and confidence scores. Results The hybrid model demonstrated high accuracy in predicting malocclusion classes, achieving confidence scores exceeding 85% across multiple test cases. Synthetic image augmentation via GAN improved the model's ability to generalize from limited data. The integration of LLM facilitated enhanced interpretation of clinical data, supporting precise treatment recommendations. Conclusion The generative AI-driven hybrid model effectively supports the diagnosis and treatment planning for malocclusion, thereby offering a valuable tool for orthodontic practice. Its ability to learn from limited data and provide high-confidence predictions streamlines clinical workflows. Future work will focus on expanding datasets, improving model explainability, and conducting clinical validation to ensure broader adoption in precision orthodontics.","affiliation":"Dentistry, Qatar University, Doha, QAT.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Medical Image Analysis","Orthodontic Treatment Planning","Convolutional Neural Networks","Generative Adversarial Networks","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Models","Malocclusion Diagnosis","Hybrid Artificial Intelligence Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.105050"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13080857"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13080857?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-17","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-18","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-11"},{"id":"41485858","source":"MED","pmid":"41485858","pmcid":"PMC12965389","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12965389"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.mayocp.2025.11.008","title":"Building Artificial Intelligence and Data Literacy Across the Health Care Research Workforce in the Mayo Clinic Center for Clinical and Translational Science: Why It Matters and What We Are Doing About It.","authorString":"Zand J, Suvakov M, Overgaard SM, Kozikowski CG, Jimison RC, Nelson KB, Silvano CJ, Meiners LM, Fan JW, Schultz KE, Carter RE, Wieland ML, Patten CA, Dugani SB, Weis JA, Poe JD, Holmes DR, Griffiths LG, Dieter HL, Juhn YJ, Enders FT, Callstrom MR, Shah V, Meyer FB, Garovic VD.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zand J","firstName":"Jaleh","lastName":"Zand","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Suvakov M","firstName":"Milovan","lastName":"Suvakov","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Overgaard SM","firstName":"Shauna M","lastName":"Overgaard","initials":"SM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4494-0008"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Digital Health, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Kozikowski CG","firstName":"Chester G","lastName":"Kozikowski","initials":"CG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Jimison RC","firstName":"Ryan C","lastName":"Jimison","initials":"RC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-1931-7018"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Nelson KB","firstName":"Kristina B","lastName":"Nelson","initials":"KB","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-7045-6414"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Silvano CJ","firstName":"Carmen J","lastName":"Silvano","initials":"CJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Meiners LM","firstName":"Laura M","lastName":"Meiners","initials":"LM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7308-1812"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Fan JW","firstName":"Jungwei W","lastName":"Fan","initials":"JW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Department of Artificial Intelligence and Informatics, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Schultz KE","firstName":"Kate E","lastName":"Schultz","initials":"KE","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-8507-8373"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Digital Health, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Carter RE","firstName":"Rickey E","lastName":"Carter","initials":"RE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Wieland ML","firstName":"Mark L","lastName":"Wieland","initials":"ML","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Community Internal Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Patten CA","firstName":"Christi A","lastName":"Patten","initials":"CA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Psychology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Dugani SB","firstName":"Sagar B","lastName":"Dugani","initials":"SB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Division of Hospital Internal Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Weis JA","firstName":"Jenny A","lastName":"Weis","initials":"JA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Mayo Clinic Health System, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Poe JD","firstName":"John D","lastName":"Poe","initials":"JD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Education Administration, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Holmes DR 3rd","firstName":"David R","lastName":"Holmes","initials":"DR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physiology and Biomedical Engineering, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Griffiths LG","firstName":"Leigh G","lastName":"Griffiths","initials":"LG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cardiovascular Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Dieter HL","firstName":"Heidi L","lastName":"Dieter","initials":"HL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research Administration, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Juhn YJ","firstName":"Young J","lastName":"Juhn","initials":"YJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Department of Pediatric and Adolescent Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Enders FT","firstName":"Felicity T","lastName":"Enders","initials":"FT","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Division of Clinical Trials and Biostatistics, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Callstrom MR","firstName":"Matthew R","lastName":"Callstrom","initials":"MR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Shah V","firstName":"Vijay","lastName":"Shah","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Meyer FB","firstName":"Fred B","lastName":"Meyer","initials":"FB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Neurologic Surgery, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Garovic VD","firstName":"Vesna D","lastName":"Garovic","initials":"VD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN. Electronic address: garovic.vesna@mayo.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4494-0008"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7308-1812"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-8507-8373"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-7045-6414"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-1931-7018"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"101","journalIssueId":4084538,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Mayo Clinic proceedings","medlineAbbreviation":"Mayo Clin Proc","issn":"0025-6196","essn":"1942-5546","isoabbreviation":"Mayo Clin Proc","nlmid":"0405543"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"17-25","affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"K23 MD016230","agency":"National Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"K23 MD016230","agency":"NIMHD NIH HHS","acronym":"MD","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"UL1 TR002377","agency":"NCATS NIH HHS","acronym":"TR","orderIn":0}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mayocp.2025.11.008"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12965389"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12965389?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mayocp.2025.11.008"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"Y","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"Y","manuscriptId":"NIHMS2147061","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-05","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},{"id":"42106879","source":"MED","pmid":"42106879","pmcid":"PMC13157658","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13157658"]},"doi":"10.1186/s41073-026-00212-3","title":"A call for clarity: a unified checklist for reporting use of large language models in writing scientific manuscripts.","authorString":"Mehta N, Felder RM, Kodish E, Imrey PB, Frankel RM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mehta N","firstName":"Neil","lastName":"Mehta","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8342-4252"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cleveland Clinic Lerner College of Medicine of Case Western Reserve University School of Medicine, Cleveland, Ohio, USA. mehtan@ccf.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Felder RM","firstName":"Ryan Marshall","lastName":"Felder","initials":"RM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1306-4946"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cleveland Clinic Center for Bioethics, Cleveland, Ohio, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Kodish E","firstName":"Eric","lastName":"Kodish","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-1609-3609"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cleveland Clinic Lerner College of Medicine of Case Western Reserve University School of Medicine, Cleveland, Ohio, USA."},{"affiliation":"Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, Ohio, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Imrey PB","firstName":"Peter B","lastName":"Imrey","initials":"PB","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0533-4603"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Cleveland Clinic, Cleveland Clinic Lerner College of Medicine of Case Western Reserve University School of Medicine, Cleveland, Ohio, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Frankel RM","firstName":"Richard M","lastName":"Frankel","initials":"RM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4132-4506"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Indiana University School of Medicine, Indianapolis, Indiana, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8342-4252"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0533-4603"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1306-4946"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4132-4506"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-1609-3609"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"11","journalIssueId":4191120,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Research integrity and peer review","medlineAbbreviation":"Res Integr Peer Rev","issn":"2058-8615","essn":"2058-8615","isoabbreviation":"Res Integr Peer Rev","nlmid":"101676020"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"24","abstractText":"The rapid integration of generative artificial intelligence (Gen AI) into academic writing has outpaced the establishment of consistent norms for responsible and transparent disclosure. Leading organizations including the International Committee of Medical Journal Editors (ICMJE), the Committee on Publication Ethics (COPE), the World Association of Medical Editors (WAME), and the European Commission, Directorate-General for Research and Innovation have issued guidance affirming that AI tools cannot be listed as authors and must be transparently disclosed. However, what remains missing is both a cross-journal consensus and guidance for authors on what constitutes acceptable versus unacceptable use of Gen AI. Furthermore, as authors may employ Gen AI at multiple, distinct stages of manuscript preparation, they currently have no standardized or granular method to report this varied use in sufficient detail. This ambiguity creates a critical gap between high-level disclosure principles and practical implementation, threatening not necessarily the integrity of the underlying research itself, but the reader's and editor's ability to objectively assess the reliability and provenance of reported findings.This paper responds to that gap by proposing a structured, domain-based framework for reporting Gen AI use in scholarly manuscripts. Drawing on a synthesis of evolving editorial statements and guidelines, we outline three domains in which Gen AI is commonly employed: conceptual contributions, linguistic assistance, and research assistance. For each domain, we distinguish uses that are generally acceptable from those that raise ethical or integrity concerns, providing examples to guide authors, reviewers, and journal editors and editorial staff.To operationalize this framework, we introduce a prototype of an online Gen AI use disclosure form that guides authors through documenting their use of Gen AI across the three domains. The tool automatically generates a standardized disclosure statement and assigns a unique, citable reference number. This reference number links to a persistent, publicly accessible summary of the declared Gen AI use, creating a transparent and auditable record. This system is proposed as a 'living' platform, designed to evolve through consensus among journal editors and editorial staff, authors, and research integrity organizations, functioning similarly to other reporting guidelines hosted by the EQUATOR Network.This system moves beyond ad hoc, narrative statements to establish a proactive and standardized disclosure process around the use of Gen AI in scholarly publishing. By embedding transparency, human accountability, and traceability directly into the publication workflow, our approach complements existing frameworks for authorship and conflict of interest. Like conflict-of-interest disclosures, AI use statements surface information that allows readers to contextualize potential risks and judge credibility for themselves. Ultimately, this work advances a practical model to strengthen trust between authors, journal editors and editorial staff, and readers, aligning the promise of generative AI with the enduring principles of research integrity.","affiliation":"Cleveland Clinic Lerner College of Medicine of Case Western Reserve University School of Medicine, Cleveland, Ohio, USA. mehtan@ccf.org.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Letter"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Publication Ethics","Research Transparency","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Gen Ai Disclosure","Standardize Acceptable Ai Use"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s41073-026-00212-3"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13157658"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13157658?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-09","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-10"}]}}